// Foundational quizzes — high-school-to-early-undergrad level (Easy/Medium only).
// Distinct from the deeper MCAT-style bank in bioBiochem/chemPhys/psychSoc — these
// cover intro concepts: cell biology, genetics, human body systems, chemistry
// foundations, physics foundations, intro psych/soc, and study skills.
export const FOUNDATIONS_QUIZZES = [
  {
  id:'fnd001', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Cell Theory & Discovery`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Who was the first person to observe and name "cells" after looking at cork under a microscope?`, ch:[`Robert Hooke`,`Antonie van Leeuwenhoek`,`Matthias Schleiden`,`Rudolf Virchow`], ans:0, exp:`Robert Hooke looked at thin slices of cork in 1665 and named the tiny box-like structures he saw "cells" because they reminded him of small rooms.` },
    { q:`Which scientist is famous for being among the first to observe living, moving microorganisms (which he called "animalcules") using a simple microscope he built himself?`, ch:[`Robert Hooke`,`Antonie van Leeuwenhoek`,`Theodor Schwann`,`Matthias Schleiden`], ans:1, exp:`Antonie van Leeuwenhoek built powerful single-lens microscopes and was the first to describe living microorganisms like bacteria and protozoa.` },
    { q:`What is the third and final part of the modern Cell Theory, added by Rudolf Virchow?`, ch:[`All living things are made of cells`,`The cell is the basic unit of structure and function in living things`,`All cells come from pre-existing cells`,`Cells contain a nucleus`], ans:2, exp:`Virchow added the idea that new cells arise only from cells that already exist, rejecting the older idea that life could spontaneously appear from non-living matter.` },
    { q:`Which two scientists are credited with proposing that all plants and all animals are made of cells, forming the basis of the first two parts of Cell Theory?`, ch:[`Hooke and Leeuwenhoek`,`Schleiden and Schwann`,`Virchow and Hooke`,`Darwin and Mendel`], ans:1, exp:`Matthias Schleiden concluded all plants are made of cells, and Theodor Schwann extended this idea to animals, laying the groundwork for Cell Theory.` },
    { q:`According to Cell Theory, what is described as the basic unit of structure and function in all living organisms?`, ch:[`The organ`,`The tissue`,`The cell`,`The organelle`], ans:2, exp:`Cell Theory states that the cell is the smallest unit that can carry out all the basic functions of life, making it the fundamental building block of organisms.` },
    { q:`Why was the invention of the microscope so important for the development of Cell Theory?`, ch:[`It allowed scientists to measure the temperature of cells`,`It allowed scientists to see structures too small for the naked eye, like individual cells`,`It allowed scientists to grow cells in a lab for the first time`,`It replaced the need for scientific observation entirely`], ans:1, exp:`Cells are far too small to see with the naked eye, so magnifying tools were essential before anyone could observe, describe, and study them.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd002', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Prokaryotic vs Eukaryotic Cells`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the key structural difference between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?`, ch:[`Prokaryotic cells have a nucleus and eukaryotic cells do not`,`Eukaryotic cells have a membrane-bound nucleus, while prokaryotic cells do not`,`Only eukaryotic cells contain DNA`,`Only prokaryotic cells can reproduce`], ans:1, exp:`Eukaryotic cells keep their DNA enclosed in a nucleus, while prokaryotic cells have their DNA floating freely in the cytoplasm.` },
    { q:`Which of the following organisms are examples of prokaryotes?`, ch:[`Humans`,`Mushrooms`,`Bacteria`,`Plants`], ans:2, exp:`Bacteria (and archaea) are prokaryotes, meaning their cells lack a nucleus and other membrane-bound organelles.` },
    { q:`Compared to eukaryotic cells, prokaryotic cells are generally...`, ch:[`Much larger`,`Much smaller and simpler`,`Identical in size and complexity`,`Only found in multicellular organisms`], ans:1, exp:`Prokaryotic cells are typically much smaller and structurally simpler than eukaryotic cells, lacking internal membrane-bound compartments.` },
    { q:`Which group of organisms is made up entirely of eukaryotic cells?`, ch:[`Bacteria`,`Archaea`,`Animals`,`None of the above have eukaryotic cells`], ans:2, exp:`Animals, along with plants, fungi, and protists, are eukaryotes, meaning their cells contain a true nucleus and membrane-bound organelles.` },
    { q:`Besides a nucleus, what else do eukaryotic cells typically have that prokaryotic cells generally lack?`, ch:[`A cell membrane`,`Ribosomes`,`Membrane-bound organelles like mitochondria`,`DNA`], ans:2, exp:`Eukaryotic cells contain specialized membrane-bound organelles, such as mitochondria and the endoplasmic reticulum, which prokaryotic cells do not have.` },
    { q:`Where is the genetic material located in a prokaryotic cell?`, ch:[`Inside a membrane-bound nucleus`,`Freely in the cytoplasm, often in a region called the nucleoid`,`Only inside mitochondria`,`Prokaryotic cells have no genetic material`], ans:1, exp:`Since prokaryotic cells lack a nucleus, their DNA is concentrated in a region of the cytoplasm called the nucleoid, but it is not enclosed by a membrane.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd003', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Cell Organelles & Their Jobs`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which organelle is often called the "powerhouse of the cell" because it produces most of the cell's energy?`, ch:[`Nucleus`,`Mitochondria`,`Ribosome`,`Golgi apparatus`], ans:1, exp:`Mitochondria convert energy from food into ATP, the usable energy currency of the cell, which is why they're nicknamed the powerhouse of the cell.` },
    { q:`What is the main job of ribosomes in a cell?`, ch:[`Storing water and nutrients`,`Breaking down waste materials`,`Building proteins`,`Controlling what enters and exits the cell`], ans:2, exp:`Ribosomes read genetic instructions and assemble amino acids into proteins, making them essential protein-building factories.` },
    { q:`Which organelle acts as the control center of the cell and houses most of its DNA?`, ch:[`Nucleus`,`Cytoplasm`,`Lysosome`,`Endoplasmic reticulum`], ans:0, exp:`The nucleus contains the cell's DNA and directs activities like growth and reproduction, functioning as the cell's control center.` },
    { q:`Which organelle is responsible for packaging and shipping proteins to their final destinations, similar to a post office?`, ch:[`Mitochondria`,`Nucleus`,`Golgi apparatus`,`Cell membrane`], ans:2, exp:`The Golgi apparatus modifies, sorts, and packages proteins and other molecules before sending them where they need to go, inside or outside the cell.` },
    { q:`What is the primary function of lysosomes?`, ch:[`Producing ATP`,`Breaking down waste and worn-out cell parts`,`Synthesizing proteins`,`Photosynthesis`], ans:1, exp:`Lysosomes contain digestive enzymes that break down waste materials, damaged organelles, and other debris, acting like the cell's recycling center.` },
    { q:`Which organelle forms a network of membranes involved in either building proteins (when studded with ribosomes) or making lipids?`, ch:[`Golgi apparatus`,`Endoplasmic reticulum`,`Vacuole`,`Cytoskeleton`], ans:1, exp:`The endoplasmic reticulum comes in "rough" (ribosome-covered, protein-building) and "smooth" (lipid-building) forms and works closely with the Golgi apparatus.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd004', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`The Cell Membrane & Transport Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`The cell membrane is best described as a "phospholipid bilayer," meaning it is made of two layers of phospholipid molecules arranged with their...`, ch:[`Hydrophobic tails facing outward and hydrophilic heads facing inward`,`Hydrophilic heads facing outward toward water and hydrophobic tails facing each other`,`Hydrophobic tails and hydrophilic heads mixed randomly`,`Only hydrophilic heads, with no tails at all`], ans:1, exp:`Phospholipids arrange themselves so their water-loving (hydrophilic) heads face the watery environments inside and outside the cell, while their water-fearing (hydrophobic) tails cluster together in the middle.` },
    { q:`What does it mean to say the cell membrane is "selectively permeable"?`, ch:[`It allows absolutely nothing to pass through`,`It allows everything to pass through equally`,`It controls which substances can enter or exit the cell`,`It only exists in plant cells`], ans:2, exp:`Selective permeability means the membrane acts like a gatekeeper, letting some molecules pass freely while restricting or regulating others.` },
    { q:`Which type of transport across the cell membrane requires the cell to use energy (ATP)?`, ch:[`Passive transport`,`Active transport`,`Simple diffusion`,`Osmosis`], ans:1, exp:`Active transport moves substances against their concentration gradient (from low to high concentration), which requires the cell to spend energy, usually in the form of ATP.` },
    { q:`Which type of transport moves substances across the membrane WITHOUT requiring the cell to use energy?`, ch:[`Active transport`,`Endocytosis`,`Exocytosis`,`Passive transport`], ans:3, exp:`Passive transport, such as diffusion, moves substances from an area of high concentration to low concentration naturally, without the cell needing to spend energy.` },
    { q:`What role do membrane proteins embedded in the phospholipid bilayer often play?`, ch:[`They have no function and are just there by accident`,`They act as channels or carriers that help specific molecules cross the membrane`,`They only provide color to the cell`,`They prevent the membrane from being flexible`], ans:1, exp:`Many membrane proteins act as channels or transporters that help specific molecules, like ions or glucose, move across the membrane that they couldn't cross on their own.` },
    { q:`What term describes the process by which a cell engulfs large particles or other cells by wrapping its membrane around them?`, ch:[`Diffusion`,`Osmosis`,`Endocytosis`,`Exocytosis`], ans:2, exp:`Endocytosis is a form of active transport where the cell membrane folds inward to surround and bring large particles into the cell.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd005', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Diffusion & Osmosis Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`Diffusion is the movement of particles from an area of...`, ch:[`Low concentration to high concentration`,`High concentration to low concentration`,`Equal concentration to equal concentration`,`Inside the cell to outside the cell only`], ans:1, exp:`Diffusion is the natural movement of particles from where they are more concentrated to where they are less concentrated, until the concentration evens out.` },
    { q:`Osmosis is specifically the diffusion of which substance across a semipermeable membrane?`, ch:[`Oxygen`,`Glucose`,`Water`,`Sodium ions`], ans:2, exp:`Osmosis refers specifically to the movement of water molecules across a selectively permeable membrane, from an area of higher water concentration to lower water concentration.` },
    { q:`If a cell is placed in a hypertonic solution (higher solute concentration outside the cell than inside), what will most likely happen to the cell?`, ch:[`It will swell and possibly burst`,`It will shrink as water leaves the cell`,`Nothing will change`,`It will immediately divide`], ans:1, exp:`In a hypertonic solution, water moves out of the cell toward the higher solute concentration outside, causing the cell to shrink.` },
    { q:`If a cell is placed in a hypotonic solution (lower solute concentration outside the cell than inside), what will most likely happen?`, ch:[`Water will move into the cell, causing it to swell`,`Water will move out of the cell, causing it to shrink`,`The cell membrane will dissolve`,`No water movement will occur`], ans:0, exp:`In a hypotonic solution, water moves into the cell because the solute concentration is lower outside, which can cause the cell to swell or even burst.` },
    { q:`When a cell is in an isotonic solution, what happens to the size of the cell?`, ch:[`It swells significantly`,`It shrinks significantly`,`It stays about the same size`,`It splits into two cells`], ans:2, exp:`In an isotonic solution, the solute concentration inside and outside the cell is equal, so water moves in and out at the same rate, keeping the cell's size stable.` },
    { q:`Why don't cells need to spend energy to carry out diffusion or osmosis?`, ch:[`Because these processes move substances along their natural concentration gradient`,`Because diffusion and osmosis only occur in dead cells`,`Because the cell membrane pumps the substances itself`,`Because these processes never actually move any molecules`], ans:0, exp:`Diffusion and osmosis are passive processes that rely on the natural tendency of particles to spread out from high to low concentration, so no cellular energy is required.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd006', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Mitosis: How Cells Divide`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the main purpose of mitosis?`, ch:[`To produce sex cells (sperm and eggs)`,`To produce two genetically identical daughter cells for growth and repair`,`To mix genetic material from two parents`,`To break down old, damaged cells for energy`], ans:1, exp:`Mitosis is a type of cell division that produces two daughter cells genetically identical to the original parent cell, used for growth, development, and repairing tissues.` },
    { q:`During which phase of the cell cycle does a cell spend most of its time growing and copying its DNA before it even begins mitosis?`, ch:[`Prophase`,`Metaphase`,`Interphase`,`Telophase`], ans:2, exp:`Interphase is the longest phase of the cell cycle, during which the cell grows, carries out normal functions, and replicates its DNA in preparation for division.` },
    { q:`During which phase of mitosis do the chromosomes line up in the middle of the cell?`, ch:[`Prophase`,`Metaphase`,`Anaphase`,`Telophase`], ans:1, exp:`In metaphase, the duplicated chromosomes line up along the cell's equator (the metaphase plate) before being pulled apart.` },
    { q:`During which phase of mitosis do sister chromatids separate and get pulled to opposite ends of the cell?`, ch:[`Prophase`,`Metaphase`,`Anaphase`,`Telophase`], ans:2, exp:`In anaphase, spindle fibers pull the sister chromatids apart and move them toward opposite poles of the cell.` },
    { q:`What typically happens right after telophase to complete the formation of two separate daughter cells?`, ch:[`DNA replication`,`Cytokinesis, the physical division of the cytoplasm`,`Fertilization`,`Meiosis`], ans:1, exp:`Cytokinesis is the process where the cytoplasm divides, physically splitting one cell into two separate daughter cells after the nuclear material has already divided.` },
    { q:`At the end of mitosis, how do the two new daughter cells compare to the original parent cell?`, ch:[`They have half the genetic material of the parent`,`They are genetically identical to the parent cell`,`They have double the genetic material of the parent`,`They have completely different genetic material`], ans:1, exp:`Mitosis produces two daughter cells with the same number of chromosomes and the same genetic information as the original parent cell.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd007', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Plant Cells vs Animal Cells`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which structure is found in plant cells but NOT in animal cells?`, ch:[`Nucleus`,`Mitochondria`,`Cell wall`,`Cell membrane`], ans:2, exp:`Plant cells have a rigid cell wall made of cellulose surrounding the cell membrane, which gives them extra structure and support; animal cells lack this feature.` },
    { q:`Which organelle allows plant cells to make their own food using sunlight, and is not found in animal cells?`, ch:[`Chloroplast`,`Lysosome`,`Ribosome`,`Golgi apparatus`], ans:0, exp:`Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and carry out photosynthesis, converting sunlight into food energy — a feature unique to plant cells (and some other photosynthetic organisms).` },
    { q:`Mature plant cells typically have one large, central structure used for storage and maintaining pressure inside the cell. What is it called?`, ch:[`Nucleolus`,`Central vacuole`,`Centriole`,`Cytoskeleton`], ans:1, exp:`Plant cells usually contain one large central vacuole that stores water, nutrients, and waste, and helps keep the cell firm by maintaining internal pressure.` },
    { q:`Which of these organelles is typically found in animal cells but is generally absent from plant cells?`, ch:[`Centrioles`,`Mitochondria`,`Nucleus`,`Ribosomes`], ans:0, exp:`Centrioles, which help organize spindle fibers during cell division, are typically found in animal cells but are usually absent in plant cells.` },
    { q:`Why do plant cells generally have a more rigid, box-like shape compared to the more rounded, irregular shape of animal cells?`, ch:[`Because plant cells have more mitochondria`,`Because of the rigid cell wall surrounding the plant cell membrane`,`Because plant cells lack a nucleus`,`Because plant cells are always larger`], ans:1, exp:`The cell wall provides structural support and a fixed shape to plant cells, while animal cells, lacking a cell wall, tend to have more flexible, rounded shapes.` },
    { q:`Which organelle is found in BOTH typical plant and animal cells?`, ch:[`Chloroplast`,`Cell wall`,`Mitochondria`,`Central vacuole (large, permanent)`], ans:2, exp:`Mitochondria, which produce energy for the cell, are found in both plant and animal cells since both need to generate energy to function.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd008', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Photosynthesis Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the main purpose of photosynthesis?`, ch:[`To break down glucose for energy`,`To convert light energy into chemical energy stored in glucose`,`To help plants reproduce`,`To remove water from the plant`], ans:1, exp:`Photosynthesis allows plants to capture energy from sunlight and store it in the chemical bonds of glucose, a sugar the plant can use for energy.` },
    { q:`Which organelle inside plant cells is the site of photosynthesis?`, ch:[`Mitochondria`,`Nucleus`,`Chloroplast`,`Ribosome`], ans:2, exp:`Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll, the green pigment that captures light energy, making them the site where photosynthesis takes place.` },
    { q:`What two raw materials does a plant need as inputs for photosynthesis, besides light energy?`, ch:[`Oxygen and glucose`,`Carbon dioxide and water`,`Nitrogen and water`,`Glucose and water`], ans:1, exp:`Plants take in carbon dioxide from the air and water from the soil, then use light energy to combine them into glucose during photosynthesis.` },
    { q:`What gas is released as a byproduct of photosynthesis, which humans and other animals need to breathe?`, ch:[`Carbon dioxide`,`Nitrogen`,`Oxygen`,`Hydrogen`], ans:2, exp:`During photosynthesis, plants release oxygen as a byproduct, which is essential for the survival of humans and most other living organisms.` },
    { q:`Which pigment gives most plants their green color and is essential for absorbing light energy during photosynthesis?`, ch:[`Melanin`,`Chlorophyll`,`Hemoglobin`,`Carotene`], ans:1, exp:`Chlorophyll absorbs light (mostly red and blue wavelengths, reflecting green) and uses that energy to power photosynthesis, giving plants their green color.` },
    { q:`What is the simplified overall word equation for photosynthesis?`, ch:[`Glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + energy`,`Carbon dioxide + water + light energy → glucose + oxygen`,`Oxygen + water → carbon dioxide + glucose`,`Glucose + carbon dioxide → water + oxygen`], ans:1, exp:`Photosynthesis combines carbon dioxide and water using light energy to produce glucose (food for the plant) and oxygen as a byproduct.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd009', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Cellular Respiration Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the main purpose of cellular respiration?`, ch:[`To convert light energy into glucose`,`To break down glucose and release usable energy in the form of ATP`,`To build new cell walls`,`To produce oxygen for the atmosphere`], ans:1, exp:`Cellular respiration breaks down glucose molecules to release stored chemical energy, which the cell captures and stores in a usable form called ATP.` },
    { q:`Where in the cell does most of cellular respiration take place?`, ch:[`Nucleus`,`Chloroplast`,`Mitochondria`,`Golgi apparatus`], ans:2, exp:`Mitochondria are the main site of cellular respiration, which is why they're often called the powerhouse of the cell.` },
    { q:`Which gas do cells need to take in for the most efficient form of cellular respiration to occur?`, ch:[`Carbon dioxide`,`Oxygen`,`Nitrogen`,`Hydrogen`], ans:1, exp:`Efficient cellular respiration requires oxygen, which is why this process is often called "aerobic" respiration.` },
    { q:`What two waste products are released as byproducts of aerobic cellular respiration?`, ch:[`Glucose and oxygen`,`Carbon dioxide and water`,`Nitrogen and ATP`,`Water and glucose`], ans:1, exp:`As cells break down glucose using oxygen, they release carbon dioxide and water as waste products, which is why we exhale carbon dioxide.` },
    { q:`What is the name of the energy-carrying molecule that cells produce during cellular respiration and use to power their activities?`, ch:[`DNA`,`Glucose`,`ATP`,`Chlorophyll`], ans:2, exp:`ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is the molecule cells use to store and transfer usable energy for nearly all cellular processes.` },
    { q:`How are photosynthesis and cellular respiration related to one another?`, ch:[`They are completely unrelated processes`,`Cellular respiration produces the glucose and oxygen that photosynthesis uses as inputs`,`Photosynthesis produces glucose and oxygen, which cellular respiration then uses as inputs to release energy`,`Both processes occur only in animal cells`], ans:2, exp:`Photosynthesis builds glucose and releases oxygen, while cellular respiration does the reverse — breaking down glucose using oxygen to release energy, producing carbon dioxide and water.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd010', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Enzymes 101: What They Do`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the main job of an enzyme in a biological reaction?`, ch:[`To act as a permanent building block of the cell`,`To speed up the rate of a chemical reaction`,`To slow down chemical reactions so cells don't overheat`,`To store genetic information`], ans:1, exp:`Enzymes are biological catalysts that speed up chemical reactions, allowing processes that would otherwise happen too slowly to sustain life.` },
    { q:`What term describes the molecule that an enzyme acts on during a reaction?`, ch:[`Product`,`Catalyst`,`Substrate`,`Cofactor`], ans:2, exp:`The substrate is the specific molecule that binds to an enzyme's active site and gets transformed during the reaction.` },
    { q:`What is the "active site" of an enzyme?`, ch:[`The part of the enzyme where the substrate binds and the reaction occurs`,`The location in the cell where enzymes are stored`,`A region of DNA that codes for enzymes`,`The part of the substrate that touches the cell membrane`], ans:0, exp:`The active site is a specially shaped region on the enzyme where the substrate fits and binds, allowing the reaction to take place.` },
    { q:`The idea that an enzyme's active site is shaped to fit a specific substrate, much like a key fits a specific lock, is known as the...`, ch:[`Concentration gradient model`,`Lock and key model`,`Osmosis model`,`Cell theory model`], ans:1, exp:`The lock-and-key model describes how an enzyme's active site has a specific shape that matches its substrate, explaining why enzymes are so selective about what they act on.` },
    { q:`What generally happens to most human enzymes if the temperature gets too high (for example, well above normal body temperature)?`, ch:[`They work even faster with no limit`,`They become denatured and lose their ability to function properly`,`They turn into a different type of molecule entirely`,`Nothing changes at all`], ans:1, exp:`Extreme heat can change an enzyme's shape (denature it), especially its active site, which prevents it from binding its substrate and doing its job.` },
    { q:`Do enzymes get used up or permanently changed after they help a reaction occur?`, ch:[`Yes, they are destroyed after every single reaction`,`No, enzymes are generally reusable and can catalyze many reactions`,`Yes, they turn directly into the product`,`Enzymes only work one time in their entire existence`], ans:1, exp:`Enzymes are not consumed by the reactions they speed up — after releasing the product, they can bind new substrate molecules and catalyze the reaction again.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd011', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Water & Why Life Needs It`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Why is a water molecule described as "polar"?`, ch:[`Because it has no charge at all`,`Because it has an uneven distribution of charge, with a slightly negative oxygen end and slightly positive hydrogen ends`,`Because it only exists at the North and South poles`,`Because it is made of only one type of atom`], ans:1, exp:`In a water molecule, oxygen pulls electrons more strongly than hydrogen does, giving the oxygen end a slight negative charge and the hydrogen ends a slight positive charge.` },
    { q:`What type of weak attraction forms between the slightly positive hydrogen of one water molecule and the slightly negative oxygen of a neighboring water molecule?`, ch:[`Covalent bond`,`Ionic bond`,`Hydrogen bond`,`Peptide bond`], ans:2, exp:`Hydrogen bonds are weak attractions between water molecules that give water many of its unique properties, like high surface tension and its ability to stay liquid across a wide temperature range.` },
    { q:`Why is water often called the "universal solvent"?`, ch:[`Because it cannot dissolve any substances`,`Because its polarity allows it to dissolve many different substances`,`Because it is the only liquid found on Earth`,`Because it has no chemical properties`], ans:1, exp:`Water's polar nature allows it to surround and dissolve many other polar or charged substances, making it an excellent solvent for the chemical reactions of life.` },
    { q:`What term describes water's tendency to stick to other water molecules, largely due to hydrogen bonding?`, ch:[`Adhesion`,`Cohesion`,`Diffusion`,`Osmosis`], ans:1, exp:`Cohesion refers to water molecules sticking to each other, which is responsible for phenomena like surface tension and water forming droplets.` },
    { q:`Water has a relatively high "specific heat," meaning it can absorb or release a lot of heat energy with only a small change in its own temperature. Why does this matter for living things?`, ch:[`It has no real importance for living organisms`,`It helps organisms and environments resist rapid, extreme temperature changes`,`It causes organisms to overheat quickly`,`It prevents water from ever freezing`], ans:1, exp:`Because water resists rapid temperature swings, it helps stabilize the internal temperature of organisms and moderates climates in bodies of water, supporting stable environments for life.` },
    { q:`What term describes water's ability to stick to other, different substances (like the walls of a thin tube or plant tissue)?`, ch:[`Cohesion`,`Adhesion`,`Polarity`,`Solvation`], ans:1, exp:`Adhesion is water's tendency to stick to other surfaces or substances, which, combined with cohesion, helps water travel upward through narrow tubes in plants.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd012', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Macromolecules: Carbs, Fats, Proteins, Nucleic Acids`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which class of macromolecule serves as the body's main and quickest source of energy, and includes sugars and starches?`, ch:[`Proteins`,`Carbohydrates`,`Lipids`,`Nucleic acids`], ans:1, exp:`Carbohydrates, such as sugars and starches, are the body's primary and fastest-accessed source of energy.` },
    { q:`Which class of macromolecule is made up of chains of amino acids and is used for building tissues, forming enzymes, and many other structural and functional roles?`, ch:[`Carbohydrates`,`Lipids`,`Proteins`,`Nucleic acids`], ans:2, exp:`Proteins are built from chains of amino acids and serve many roles in the body, including as enzymes, structural components, and signaling molecules.` },
    { q:`Which class of macromolecule includes fats and oils, and is used for long-term energy storage and insulation?`, ch:[`Lipids`,`Proteins`,`Carbohydrates`,`Nucleic acids`], ans:0, exp:`Lipids, including fats and oils, store energy efficiently for the long term and also help insulate and cushion the body.` },
    { q:`Which macromolecule class includes DNA and RNA, the molecules that store and transmit genetic information?`, ch:[`Carbohydrates`,`Proteins`,`Lipids`,`Nucleic acids`], ans:3, exp:`Nucleic acids, DNA and RNA, are built from repeating units called nucleotides and carry the genetic instructions for building and operating living organisms.` },
    { q:`Large macromolecules like proteins and carbohydrates are often built from smaller repeating units linked together into long chains. What are these smaller repeating units called?`, ch:[`Monomers`,`Isotopes`,`Organelles`,`Enzymes`], ans:0, exp:`Monomers are the small building-block molecules (like amino acids or simple sugars) that link together to form larger polymers such as proteins or starches.` },
    { q:`What are the specific monomer building blocks that link together to form nucleic acids like DNA?`, ch:[`Amino acids`,`Fatty acids`,`Nucleotides`,`Monosaccharides`], ans:2, exp:`Nucleotides are the individual building blocks of nucleic acids; each one contains a sugar, a phosphate group, and a nitrogen-containing base.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd013', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`DNA Structure Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What shape does a DNA molecule form?`, ch:[`A single straight strand`, `A double helix`, `A flat square`, `A branching tree`], ans:1, exp:`DNA is shaped like a twisted ladder called a double helix, made of two strands winding around each other.` },
    { q:`DNA is built from repeating units called what?`, ch:[`Nucleotides`, `Amino acids`, `Ribosomes`, `Enzymes`], ans:0, exp:`Nucleotides are the building blocks of DNA, each made of a sugar, a phosphate, and a nitrogen base.` },
    { q:`Which base pairs with adenine (A) in DNA?`, ch:[`Guanine`, `Cytosine`, `Thymine`, `Uracil`], ans:2, exp:`Adenine always pairs with thymine in DNA, held together by hydrogen bonds.` },
    { q:`Which base pairs with cytosine (C) in DNA?`, ch:[`Guanine`, `Adenine`, `Thymine`, `Uracil`], ans:0, exp:`Cytosine always pairs with guanine, one of the two fixed base-pairing rules in DNA.` },
    { q:`What forms the "backbone" of each DNA strand?`, ch:[`Nitrogen bases only`, `Amino acid chains`, `Alternating sugar and phosphate groups`, `Ribosomal proteins`], ans:2, exp:`Each DNA strand has a backbone made of sugar and phosphate molecules linked together, with bases attached to the sugars.` },
    { q:`Where is most of a human cell's DNA located?`, ch:[`In the cytoplasm`, `In the nucleus`, `In the cell membrane`, `In the ribosomes`], ans:1, exp:`Most DNA is stored safely inside the nucleus, tightly packaged so it fits and stays protected.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd014', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Genes, Alleles & Traits`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is a gene?`, ch:[`A whole chromosome`, `A section of DNA that codes for a specific trait`, `A type of protein`, `A cell organelle`], ans:1, exp:`A gene is a specific segment of DNA that carries instructions for a particular trait, like eye color.` },
    { q:`What is an allele?`, ch:[`A different version of a gene`, `The physical trait itself`, `A whole set of chromosomes`, `A type of mutation only`], ans:0, exp:`Alleles are different versions of the same gene, such as one version for brown eyes and another for blue eyes.` },
    { q:`What is a "trait" in genetics?`, ch:[`A strand of DNA`, `An observable characteristic, like hair color`, `A pair of chromosomes`, `A type of cell division`], ans:1, exp:`A trait is a characteristic you can observe or measure, such as height, eye color, or blood type.` },
    { q:`If an organism has two identical alleles for a gene, it is described as what?`, ch:[`Heterozygous`, `Recessive only`, `Homozygous`, `Dominant only`], ans:2, exp:`Homozygous means both alleles for that gene match, whether they are both dominant or both recessive.` },
    { q:`What is the difference between genotype and phenotype?`, ch:[`They mean the same thing`, `Genotype is the genetic makeup; phenotype is the physical expression of it`, `Phenotype is the genetic makeup; genotype is the physical expression`, `Genotype only applies to humans`], ans:1, exp:`Genotype refers to the actual alleles an organism carries, while phenotype is how those alleles show up physically.` },
    { q:`Where are genes physically located?`, ch:[`Floating freely in the cytoplasm`, `On chromosomes`, `Inside ribosomes`, `In the cell wall`], ans:1, exp:`Genes are arranged along chromosomes, which are long, organized strands of DNA.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd015', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Dominant vs Recessive Traits`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What happens when an organism has one dominant allele and one recessive allele for a trait?`, ch:[`The traits blend evenly`, `The dominant trait is usually expressed`, `The recessive trait is usually expressed`, `Neither trait appears`], ans:1, exp:`When a dominant allele is paired with a recessive one, the dominant allele's trait is typically the one that shows up.` },
    { q:`How is a dominant allele usually written in genetic notation?`, ch:[`As a lowercase letter`, `As a number`, `As an uppercase letter`, `As a symbol like %`], ans:2, exp:`By convention, dominant alleles are shown with a capital letter, like "B", while recessive alleles use lowercase, like "b".` },
    { q:`For a recessive trait to appear in an organism's phenotype, what must be true?`, ch:[`It needs just one recessive allele`, `It needs two recessive alleles`, `It needs one dominant and one recessive allele`, `It can never appear`], ans:1, exp:`Recessive traits only show up when an organism inherits two copies of the recessive allele, one from each parent.` },
    { q:`A person who carries one recessive allele for a trait but doesn't show it is often called what?`, ch:[`A mutant`, `A hybrid organism`, `A carrier`, `A dominant expresser`], ans:2, exp:`A carrier has a hidden recessive allele that isn't expressed but can still be passed on to offspring.` },
    { q:`Attached earlobes are a recessive trait in humans. If both of a child's parents have unattached (dominant) earlobes but are carriers, is it possible for the child to have attached earlobes?`, ch:[`No, it's impossible`, `Yes, if the child inherits a recessive allele from each parent`, `Only if one parent already has attached earlobes`, `Only in identical twins`], ans:1, exp:`Even if both parents show the dominant trait, they can each pass on a hidden recessive allele, allowing the recessive trait to appear in their child.` },
    { q:`Does "dominant" mean a trait is more common in a population?`, ch:[`Yes, dominant traits are always the most common`, `No, dominance is about how alleles interact, not how frequent they are`, `Yes, but only for physical traits`, `No, dominant traits are always rare`], ans:1, exp:`Dominant simply describes which allele gets expressed when paired with a recessive one, not how frequent that allele is in a population.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd016', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Punnett Squares 101`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the main purpose of a Punnett square?`, ch:[`To measure the size of chromosomes`, `To predict the possible genotypes of offspring from a genetic cross`, `To sequence DNA`, `To identify which organelle a gene is in`], ans:1, exp:`A Punnett square is a simple grid that shows all the possible allele combinations offspring could inherit from two parents.` },
    { q:`In a cross between two heterozygous parents (Aa x Aa), what genotype ratio is expected in the offspring?`, ch:[`1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa`, `All Aa`, `1 AA : 1 aa only`, `All aa`], ans:0, exp:`Crossing Aa x Aa gives a classic 1:2:1 genotype ratio: 25% AA, 50% Aa, and 25% aa.` },
    { q:`In a cross between two heterozygous parents (Aa x Aa) where A is dominant, what phenotype ratio is expected?`, ch:[`1 dominant : 3 recessive`, `2 dominant : 2 recessive`, `3 dominant : 1 recessive`, `All dominant`], ans:2, exp:`Since AA and Aa both show the dominant trait, 3 out of 4 offspring show the dominant phenotype and 1 out of 4 shows the recessive one.` },
    { q:`If you cross a homozygous recessive parent (aa) with a homozygous dominant parent (AA), what genotype will ALL the offspring have?`, ch:[`AA`, `aa`, `Aa`, `A mix of all three`], ans:2, exp:`Every offspring gets one A from one parent and one a from the other, so they will all be heterozygous (Aa).` },
    { q:`A heterozygous parent (Aa) is crossed with a homozygous recessive parent (aa). What is the probability an offspring will show the recessive phenotype?`, ch:[`0%`, `25%`, `50%`, `100%`], ans:2, exp:`This cross produces 50% Aa (dominant phenotype) and 50% aa (recessive phenotype), so there's a 50% chance of the recessive trait showing.` },
    { q:`Why are Punnett squares useful tools in genetics?`, ch:[`They guarantee exactly which trait an individual offspring will have`, `They show probabilities, helping predict likely outcomes of a genetic cross`, `They can only be used for plants`, `They replace the need for studying chromosomes`], ans:1, exp:`Punnett squares show probabilities and possible combinations, not certainties, which helps scientists predict likely outcomes across many offspring.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd017', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Chromosomes & Human Genetics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`How many chromosomes are in a typical human body cell?`, ch:[`23`, `46`, `92`, `44`], ans:1, exp:`Human body cells normally have 46 chromosomes, organized into 23 pairs.` },
    { q:`Of the 23 pairs of human chromosomes, how many pairs are autosomes (non-sex chromosomes)?`, ch:[`22`, `23`, `21`, `20`], ans:0, exp:`Humans have 22 pairs of autosomes plus 1 pair of sex chromosomes, totaling 23 pairs.` },
    { q:`Which combination of sex chromosomes typically results in a biological male?`, ch:[`XX`, `XY`, `YY`, `XO`], ans:1, exp:`A typical biological male has one X chromosome and one Y chromosome (XY).` },
    { q:`Where does a person's set of 46 chromosomes come from?`, ch:[`All 46 come from one parent`, `23 from each parent, one set from mom and one from dad`, `They form spontaneously after birth`, `46 from mom, 46 from dad, then reduced by half`], ans:1, exp:`Each parent contributes 23 chromosomes through their egg or sperm cell, combining to form the full set of 46.` },
    { q:`What is a karyotype?`, ch:[`A type of protein found in cells`, `A picture or map of an individual's complete set of chromosomes`, `A gene that controls height`, `A disease caused by too much DNA`], ans:1, exp:`A karyotype is an organized image of all of a person's chromosomes, arranged by size and pair, often used to spot chromosomal differences.` },
    { q:`Which cells in the human body normally contain only 23 chromosomes instead of 46?`, ch:[`Skin cells`, `Muscle cells`, `Egg and sperm cells (gametes)`, `Nerve cells`], ans:2, exp:`Gametes (eggs and sperm) carry half the usual number of chromosomes so that when they combine, the resulting cell has the full 46.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd018', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Mutations: What They Are`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is a mutation?`, ch:[`A change in the sequence of an organism's DNA`, `A type of cell that fights infection`, `The process of a cell dividing`, `A chromosome pairing with itself`], ans:0, exp:`A mutation is any change to the DNA sequence, which can happen naturally or be caused by outside factors.` },
    { q:`Which of the following can cause mutations?`, ch:[`Only aging`, `Exposure to UV light or certain chemicals, or errors during DNA copying`, `Eating too much sugar`, `Listening to loud music`], ans:1, exp:`Mutations can arise from environmental factors like UV radiation and chemicals, or from random mistakes made when DNA is copied.` },
    { q:`Are all mutations harmful?`, ch:[`Yes, every mutation causes disease`, `No, many mutations have no effect, and some can even be helpful`, `Yes, but only in animals`, `No mutations occur naturally`], ans:1, exp:`Most mutations are neutral and don't noticeably affect an organism, while a smaller number can be harmful or even beneficial.` },
    { q:`A mutation that occurs in a sperm or egg cell and can be passed to offspring is called what type of mutation?`, ch:[`Somatic mutation`, `Germline mutation`, `Silent mutation`, `Environmental mutation`], ans:1, exp:`Germline mutations occur in reproductive cells, so they can be inherited by the next generation.` },
    { q:`A mutation that occurs in a regular body cell, like a skin cell, and is NOT passed to offspring is called what?`, ch:[`Germline mutation`, `Inherited mutation`, `Somatic mutation`, `Chromosomal doubling`], ans:2, exp:`Somatic mutations affect only the individual body cells they occur in and are not passed on to children.` },
    { q:`Why are mutations important to the process of evolution?`, ch:[`They never affect populations`, `They are the ultimate source of new genetic variation in a population`, `They only remove genetic variation`, `They stop natural selection from occurring`], ans:1, exp:`Mutations introduce new versions of genes into a population, providing the raw genetic variation that natural selection can act on.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd019', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`From Gene to Protein (Intro)`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the basic flow of genetic information described by the "central dogma" of biology?`, ch:[`Protein to RNA to DNA`, `DNA to RNA to protein`, `RNA to protein to DNA`, `Protein to DNA to RNA`], ans:1, exp:`The central dogma describes how genetic information flows: DNA is used to make RNA, and RNA is used to make protein.` },
    { q:`What is the process of copying a gene's DNA sequence into an RNA molecule called?`, ch:[`Translation`, `Replication`, `Transcription`, `Mutation`], ans:2, exp:`Transcription is the process where the cell copies a DNA gene sequence into a molecule of messenger RNA (mRNA).` },
    { q:`What is the process of building a protein from an mRNA sequence called?`, ch:[`Translation`, `Transcription`, `Duplication`, `Fertilization`], ans:0, exp:`Translation is the process where the cell reads mRNA and links together amino acids to build a protein.` },
    { q:`Which cell structure is mainly responsible for building proteins during translation?`, ch:[`The nucleus`, `The mitochondria`, `The ribosome`, `The cell membrane`], ans:2, exp:`Ribosomes read mRNA instructions and assemble amino acids into proteins during translation.` },
    { q:`Why do genes matter so much for how an organism looks and functions?`, ch:[`Genes directly become the organism's traits with no other steps`, `Genes provide instructions for building proteins, which carry out most jobs in the body`, `Genes only affect eye color`, `Genes are only active before birth`], ans:1, exp:`Genes code for proteins, and proteins do most of the actual work in cells, from building structures to speeding up chemical reactions.` },
    { q:`What is mRNA (messenger RNA)?`, ch:[`A copy of a chromosome`, `A type of protein`, `A molecule that carries genetic instructions from DNA to the ribosome`, `A structure that stores mutations`], ans:2, exp:`mRNA acts like a messenger, carrying the copied instructions from a gene in the DNA out to the ribosome, where a protein is built.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd020', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Genetic Disorders 101`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`Sickle cell anemia is caused by what kind of change?`, ch:[`A missing chromosome`, `A mutation in the gene for hemoglobin`, `An extra copy of chromosome 21`, `A virus infecting red blood cells`], ans:1, exp:`Sickle cell anemia results from a mutation in the gene that codes for hemoglobin, changing the shape of red blood cells.` },
    { q:`Down syndrome is typically caused by what?`, ch:[`A single gene mutation`, `A missing X chromosome`, `An extra copy of chromosome 21`, `Exposure to sunlight`], ans:2, exp:`Down syndrome usually occurs when a person has three copies of chromosome 21 instead of the usual two, a condition called trisomy 21.` },
    { q:`Cystic fibrosis is inherited in what pattern?`, ch:[`Dominant, needing only one copy of the allele`, `Recessive, needing two copies of the allele`, `It is not inherited at all`, `Only passed from father to son`], ans:1, exp:`Cystic fibrosis is a recessive genetic disorder, meaning a person needs two copies of the mutated allele to be affected.` },
    { q:`Red-green color blindness is most commonly associated with which chromosome?`, ch:[`Chromosome 21`, `The Y chromosome`, `The X chromosome`, `Chromosome 1`], ans:2, exp:`The most common form of red-green color blindness is caused by a recessive gene located on the X chromosome, which is why it's more common in males.` },
    { q:`What is the key difference between a chromosomal disorder and a single-gene disorder?`, ch:[`There is no difference`, `A chromosomal disorder involves a whole chromosome or large piece of one; a single-gene disorder involves one specific gene`, `Single-gene disorders always affect more people`, `Chromosomal disorders can never be detected before birth`], ans:1, exp:`Chromosomal disorders involve changes in the number or structure of entire chromosomes, while single-gene disorders come from a change in just one gene.` },
    { q:`Why might two unaffected parents who are both carriers have a child with a recessive genetic disorder?`, ch:[`It is impossible for this to happen`, `Each parent can pass along their hidden recessive allele, and the child can inherit both`, `The disorder appears randomly regardless of genetics`, `Only the mother's genes matter for recessive disorders`], ans:1, exp:`Carrier parents each have one recessive allele hidden behind a dominant one, so there's a chance their child inherits the recessive allele from both, resulting in the disorder.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd021', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Twins & Heredity`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`How do identical twins typically form?`, ch:[`Two separate eggs are fertilized by two separate sperm`, `One fertilized egg splits into two embryos`, `One egg is fertilized by two sperm`, `Two eggs are fertilized by one sperm`], ans:1, exp:`Identical twins form when a single fertilized egg splits early in development, creating two embryos with the same genetic material.` },
    { q:`How do fraternal twins typically form?`, ch:[`One fertilized egg splits into two`, `Two separate eggs are each fertilized by a different sperm cell`, `A single sperm fertilizes two eggs`, `They always share identical DNA`], ans:1, exp:`Fraternal twins result from two different eggs being released and fertilized by two different sperm cells during the same pregnancy.` },
    { q:`Do identical twins share the exact same DNA?`, ch:[`No, their DNA is completely different`, `Yes, since they come from the same fertilized egg`, `Only their mitochondrial DNA matches`, `Only if they are the same biological sex`], ans:1, exp:`Because identical twins develop from one fertilized egg that split in two, they start out with essentially the same DNA.` },
    { q:`Are fraternal twins any more genetically similar to each other than regular siblings born at different times?`, ch:[`Yes, fraternal twins share 100% of their DNA`, `No, fraternal twins are genetically similar to regular siblings, just born at the same time`, `Fraternal twins share no DNA at all`, `Fraternal twins are always the same sex`], ans:1, exp:`Fraternal twins develop from two separate eggs and sperm, so genetically they're just like any other siblings, only they happen to be born together.` },
    { q:`Can identical twins be different biological sexes?`, ch:[`Yes, commonly`, `No, since they come from the same fertilized egg they are typically the same sex`, `Only if one twin has a mutation`, `Sex is unrelated to twin type`], ans:1, exp:`Since identical twins split from one fertilized egg with one set of sex chromosomes, they are almost always the same biological sex.` },
    { q:`Why are twin studies useful to scientists studying heredity?`, ch:[`Twins never share any traits, making comparisons easy`, `Comparing identical and fraternal twins helps researchers understand how much genetics versus environment shapes traits`, `Twins are the only people who inherit genes from parents`, `Twin studies focus solely on eye color`], ans:1, exp:`Because identical twins share all their DNA while fraternal twins share about the same amount as regular siblings, comparing them helps scientists tease apart the roles of genes and environment.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd022', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Intro to Evolution & Natural Selection`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which scientist is most famous for developing the theory of evolution by natural selection?`, ch:[`Gregor Mendel`, `Charles Darwin`, `Isaac Newton`, `Louis Pasteur`], ans:1, exp:`Charles Darwin is best known for developing and popularizing the theory of evolution by natural selection.` },
    { q:`What is required within a population for natural selection to act on it?`, ch:[`Every individual must be genetically identical`, `There must be variation in traits among individuals`, `The population must be very small`, `All individuals must reproduce at the same rate`], ans:1, exp:`Natural selection needs genetic variation to work with, so it can favor traits that help individuals survive and reproduce.` },
    { q:`In natural selection, which individuals are generally more likely to pass on their traits to the next generation?`, ch:[`Those least suited to their environment`, `Those best suited to survive and reproduce in their environment`, `Only the largest individuals, regardless of environment`, `Individuals are selected completely at random`], ans:1, exp:`Individuals whose traits help them survive and reproduce successfully in their environment tend to pass those traits on more often.` },
    { q:`What is an "adaptation" in the context of evolution?`, ch:[`A trait that helps an organism survive and reproduce in its environment`, `Any random change in DNA`, `A trait that always harms an organism`, `A skill learned during an organism's lifetime and then inherited`], ans:0, exp:`An adaptation is an inherited trait that improves an organism's chances of surviving and reproducing in a specific environment.` },
    { q:`Does natural selection act on individual organisms or on populations over time?`, ch:[`It only changes a single organism during its lifetime`, `It acts on individuals in the moment, but its effects on trait frequency show up in populations over generations`, `It has no effect on populations`, `It only applies to plants`], ans:1, exp:`Natural selection favors certain individuals in each generation, but its real impact on which traits become common is only visible across many generations in a population.` },
    { q:`Which phrase is commonly used to summarize the idea that better-suited individuals tend to survive and reproduce more successfully?`, ch:[`"Survival of the fittest"`, `"Use it or lose it"`, `"Nature versus nurture"`, `"Blending inheritance"`], ans:0, exp:`"Survival of the fittest" is a popular phrase describing how individuals best suited to their environment tend to survive and reproduce more.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd023', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Evidence for Evolution`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What can the fossil record show scientists about evolution?`, ch:[`Nothing useful, since fossils are too rare`, `How species have changed and appeared over long periods of time`, `Only what plants looked like`, `The exact thoughts of extinct animals`], ans:1, exp:`Fossils, especially when arranged from oldest to youngest layers, show a record of how species have changed and diversified over millions of years.` },
    { q:`What are homologous structures?`, ch:[`Body parts that look completely different and serve unrelated functions in different species`, `Similar body structures in different species that suggest a shared ancestor, even if used differently`, `Structures found only in bacteria`, `Random mutations with no evolutionary meaning`], ans:1, exp:`Homologous structures, like the bone arrangement in a human arm, a bat wing, and a whale flipper, share a similar underlying structure, suggesting they evolved from a common ancestor.` },
    { q:`How does comparing DNA between species provide evidence for evolution?`, ch:[`Species with more recent common ancestors tend to have more similar DNA sequences`, `All species have identical DNA regardless of relation`, `DNA comparisons cannot reveal anything about ancestry`, `DNA evidence only applies to identical twins`], ans:0, exp:`Species that share a more recent common ancestor tend to have more similar DNA, giving scientists a molecular way to trace evolutionary relationships.` },
    { q:`What does biogeography (the study of where species live) contribute as evidence for evolution?`, ch:[`It shows that species distributions have nothing to do with evolutionary history`, `It shows patterns, like related species found on nearby landmasses, that support shared ancestry and gradual change`, `It only studies ocean currents`, `It proves that all species originated in one exact location at the same time`], ans:1, exp:`The way related species tend to cluster in nearby or connected regions supports the idea that they evolved from common ancestors that spread out over time.` },
    { q:`What is a vestigial structure?`, ch:[`A brand-new structure that just evolved`, `A reduced or leftover structure that had a function in an ancestor but has little or no use today`, `A structure found only in fossils`, `A structure that only appears in plants`], ans:1, exp:`Vestigial structures, like the small pelvic bones in some whales, are remnants of features that were functional in ancestral species.` },
    { q:`Why do scientists consider multiple types of evidence (fossils, anatomy, DNA, biogeography) together when studying evolution?`, ch:[`Because any single type of evidence is completely useless`, `Because independent lines of evidence pointing to the same conclusion make the overall case for evolution stronger`, `Because they contradict each other and cancel out`, `Because only DNA evidence actually counts`], ans:1, exp:`When separate types of evidence, like fossils and DNA, all point to the same evolutionary relationships, it strengthens confidence in those conclusions.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd024', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Selective Breeding vs Natural Selection`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Who or what chooses which organisms reproduce in selective breeding?`, ch:[`The environment`, `Humans`, `Random chance only`, `Predators`], ans:1, exp:`In selective breeding, humans intentionally choose which organisms with desired traits get to reproduce.` },
    { q:`Who or what "chooses" which organisms are more likely to survive and reproduce in natural selection?`, ch:[`Humans, through direct choice`, `Environmental pressures, like predators, climate, and food availability`, `A government agency`, `Nothing; it is entirely random`], ans:1, exp:`In natural selection, the environment acts as the selecting force, favoring individuals whose traits help them survive and reproduce.` },
    { q:`Which of these is a classic example of selective breeding?`, ch:[`Peppered moths changing color due to pollution`, `Farmers breeding cows that produce more milk`, `Finches on different islands developing different beak shapes`, `Bacteria becoming resistant to antibiotics in the wild`], ans:1, exp:`Farmers choosing which cows to breed based on milk production is a human-directed example of selective breeding, also called artificial selection.` },
    { q:`Compared to natural selection, how does selective breeding typically affect the speed of trait changes in a population?`, ch:[`It usually happens much more slowly`, `It usually happens much faster because humans deliberately choose traits each generation`, `They always happen at the same speed`, `Selective breeding cannot change traits at all`], ans:1, exp:`Because humans directly and consistently pick which traits to favor, selective breeding can produce noticeable changes much faster than natural selection typically does.` },
    { q:`The many different breeds of dogs, all descended from wolves, are mainly the result of what process?`, ch:[`Natural selection acting alone in the wild`, `Selective breeding by humans over many generations`, `Random genetic drift with no human involvement`, `A single dramatic mutation`], ans:1, exp:`Humans selectively bred dogs over thousands of years for specific traits like size, temperament, and appearance, producing today's many breeds.` },
    { q:`What do selective breeding and natural selection have in common?`, ch:[`Both involve organisms with certain traits reproducing more than others, changing a population over time`, `Both always require human intervention`, `Both happen instantly with no generational change needed`, `Neither one changes the traits of a population`], ans:0, exp:`Whether it's humans or the environment doing the "selecting," both processes work by favoring certain traits to reproduce more, shifting a population's characteristics over generations.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd025', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Body Systems Overview`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which body system is primarily responsible for transporting oxygen and nutrients to cells throughout the body?`, ch:[`The circulatory system`,`The skeletal system`,`The integumentary system`,`The endocrine system`], ans:0, exp:`The circulatory system uses the heart and blood vessels to carry oxygen, nutrients, and other materials to every cell in the body.` },
    { q:`Which organ system includes the brain, spinal cord, and nerves?`, ch:[`The muscular system`,`The nervous system`,`The lymphatic system`,`The digestive system`], ans:1, exp:`The nervous system is made up of the brain, spinal cord, and a network of nerves that send signals throughout the body.` },
    { q:`What is the main job of the respiratory system?`, ch:[`Breaking down food for energy`,`Filtering waste from the blood`,`Exchanging oxygen and carbon dioxide with the air`,`Producing hormones`], ans:2, exp:`The respiratory system brings oxygen into the body and removes carbon dioxide through breathing.` },
    { q:`Which system provides the body's structural framework and protects internal organs?`, ch:[`The muscular system`,`The circulatory system`,`The nervous system`,`The skeletal system`], ans:3, exp:`The skeletal system is made of bones that support the body's shape and shield organs like the brain and heart.` },
    { q:`Which system is responsible for breaking down food into nutrients the body can use?`, ch:[`The digestive system`,`The respiratory system`,`The skeletal system`,`The nervous system`], ans:0, exp:`The digestive system processes food, breaking it into nutrients that can be absorbed and used for energy and growth.` },
    { q:`What do organ systems do when they work together in the body?`, ch:[`They operate completely independently`,`They cooperate to keep the whole body functioning`,`Only one system is active at a time`,`They compete for the same resources`], ans:1, exp:`Body systems work together as a team, each contributing a specific function so the whole body can stay healthy and balanced.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd026', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`The Skeletal System Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`About how many bones are in the adult human skeleton?`, ch:[`106`,`206`,`306`,`406`], ans:1, exp:`Adults typically have 206 bones, though babies are born with more that fuse together as they grow.` },
    { q:`What is the primary function of red bone marrow?`, ch:[`Storing fat`,`Producing blood cells`,`Connecting muscles to bone`,`Cushioning joints`], ans:1, exp:`Red bone marrow, found inside certain bones, is where the body produces red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.` },
    { q:`Which type of joint, found in the shoulder and hip, allows the widest range of motion?`, ch:[`Hinge joint`,`Fixed joint`,`Ball-and-socket joint`,`Pivot joint`], ans:2, exp:`Ball-and-socket joints let one bone rotate in almost any direction, which is why shoulders and hips are so flexible.` },
    { q:`What connective tissue attaches bone to bone at a joint?`, ch:[`Tendon`,`Cartilage`,`Ligament`,`Nerve`], ans:2, exp:`Ligaments are tough bands of tissue that connect one bone to another, helping stabilize joints.` },
    { q:`Which of these is a major function of the skeletal system besides support?`, ch:[`Digesting proteins`,`Storing minerals like calcium`,`Pumping blood`,`Filtering air`], ans:1, exp:`Bones act as a mineral reservoir, storing calcium and phosphorus that the body can draw on when needed.` },
    { q:`What is cartilage, commonly found at the ends of bones and in the nose and ears?`, ch:[`A type of muscle tissue`,`A flexible, smooth connective tissue`,`A hard mineral deposit`,`A type of nerve tissue`], ans:1, exp:`Cartilage is a flexible but firm tissue that cushions joints and gives shape to structures like the ear and nose.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd027', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`The Muscular System Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which type of muscle is attached to bones and lets you move voluntarily, like when you lift your arm?`, ch:[`Cardiac muscle`,`Smooth muscle`,`Skeletal muscle`,`Involuntary muscle`], ans:2, exp:`Skeletal muscles are attached to bones by tendons and contract under your conscious control to produce movement.` },
    { q:`Which muscle type makes up the walls of the heart and works automatically, without conscious control?`, ch:[`Cardiac muscle`,`Skeletal muscle`,`Smooth muscle only`,`Tendon tissue`], ans:0, exp:`Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart and contracts rhythmically on its own to pump blood.` },
    { q:`What tissue connects muscle to bone so that muscle contractions can move the skeleton?`, ch:[`Ligaments`,`Cartilage`,`Tendons`,`Nerves`], ans:2, exp:`Tendons are tough cords of tissue that anchor muscles to bones, transferring the force of a contraction into movement.` },
    { q:`Where is smooth muscle typically found in the body?`, ch:[`In the walls of organs like the stomach and intestines`,`Attached to the skeleton`,`Only in the heart`,`In the outer layer of skin`], ans:0, exp:`Smooth muscle lines internal organs such as the stomach, intestines, and blood vessels, working involuntarily to move substances through the body.` },
    { q:`When a muscle contracts to bend a joint, what usually happens to the opposing muscle on the other side?`, ch:[`It contracts at the same time with equal force`,`It relaxes and lengthens`,`It disappears`,`It turns into bone`], ans:1, exp:`Muscles often work in pairs; when one contracts to create movement, the opposing muscle relaxes to allow that motion.` },
    { q:`What is the main fuel source muscles use to power contraction during exercise?`, ch:[`Energy from food, primarily glucose and fat`,`Sunlight`,`Bone marrow`,`Oxygen alone with no other fuel`], ans:0, exp:`Muscles convert energy from the food we eat, especially carbohydrates and fats, into the fuel needed to contract.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd028', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`The Circulatory System Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`How many chambers does the human heart have?`, ch:[`Two`,`Three`,`Four`,`Six`], ans:2, exp:`The human heart has four chambers: two atria on top and two ventricles on the bottom.` },
    { q:`Which blood vessels carry blood away from the heart?`, ch:[`Veins`,`Arteries`,`Capillaries`,`Lymph vessels`], ans:1, exp:`Arteries carry oxygen-rich blood away from the heart to the rest of the body.` },
    { q:`What is the main function of red blood cells?`, ch:[`Fighting infection`,`Clotting blood`,`Carrying oxygen throughout the body`,`Producing hormones`], ans:2, exp:`Red blood cells contain hemoglobin, a protein that binds oxygen and delivers it to tissues throughout the body.` },
    { q:`Which tiny blood vessels allow oxygen and nutrients to pass from the blood into body tissues?`, ch:[`Arteries`,`Veins`,`Aorta`,`Capillaries`], ans:3, exp:`Capillaries are extremely thin, small vessels where the actual exchange of oxygen, nutrients, and waste happens between blood and tissue.` },
    { q:`What is the role of platelets in the blood?`, ch:[`Carrying oxygen`,`Helping blood clot to stop bleeding`,`Fighting off viruses`,`Digesting nutrients`], ans:1, exp:`Platelets are small cell fragments that clump together and help form clots to seal wounds and stop bleeding.` },
    { q:`Which blood vessels carry blood back toward the heart?`, ch:[`Veins`,`Arteries`,`Capillaries only`,`Bronchioles`], ans:0, exp:`Veins return oxygen-poor blood from the body back to the heart, often with the help of one-way valves.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd029', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`The Respiratory System Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which organ is primarily responsible for gas exchange in the respiratory system?`, ch:[`The heart`,`The lungs`,`The trachea`,`The diaphragm`], ans:1, exp:`The lungs contain millions of tiny air sacs called alveoli, where oxygen and carbon dioxide are exchanged with the blood.` },
    { q:`What is the name of the tube that carries air from the throat down toward the lungs?`, ch:[`Esophagus`,`Trachea`,`Bronchiole`,`Alveolus`], ans:1, exp:`The trachea, or windpipe, is the main airway that carries air from the throat into the lungs.` },
    { q:`What are the tiny air sacs in the lungs where oxygen enters the blood called?`, ch:[`Bronchi`,`Alveoli`,`Cilia`,`Larynx`], ans:1, exp:`Alveoli are the microscopic air sacs at the end of the airways where oxygen and carbon dioxide actually cross into and out of the bloodstream.` },
    { q:`Which muscle below the lungs contracts and flattens to help you inhale?`, ch:[`The diaphragm`,`The bicep`,`The cardiac muscle`,`The trapezius`], ans:0, exp:`The diaphragm is a dome-shaped muscle that contracts and flattens to expand the chest cavity, drawing air into the lungs.` },
    { q:`What gas does the body need to bring in through breathing, and what gas does it mainly get rid of?`, ch:[`Brings in nitrogen, gets rid of oxygen`,`Brings in oxygen, gets rid of carbon dioxide`,`Brings in carbon dioxide, gets rid of oxygen`,`Brings in hydrogen, gets rid of nitrogen`], ans:1, exp:`Breathing brings in oxygen that cells need for energy and removes carbon dioxide, a waste product of that process.` },
    { q:`Why do the airways contain tiny hair-like structures called cilia?`, ch:[`To produce sound for speaking`,`To trap and move dust and mucus out of the airways`,`To pump blood through the lungs`,`To store extra oxygen`], ans:1, exp:`Cilia line the airways and sweep mucus, dust, and germs upward and out, helping keep the lungs clean.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd030', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`The Digestive System Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Where does mechanical and chemical digestion of food first begin?`, ch:[`The stomach`,`The mouth`,`The small intestine`,`The large intestine`], ans:1, exp:`Digestion starts in the mouth, where chewing breaks food apart mechanically and saliva begins breaking down starches chemically.` },
    { q:`Which organ produces strong acid and enzymes to help break down food after it's swallowed?`, ch:[`The liver`,`The stomach`,`The esophagus`,`The pancreas`], ans:1, exp:`The stomach churns food and bathes it in acid and digestive enzymes, breaking it down further before it moves on.` },
    { q:`In which organ does most nutrient absorption into the bloodstream occur?`, ch:[`The large intestine`,`The stomach`,`The small intestine`,`The esophagus`], ans:2, exp:`The small intestine has a huge surface area lined with tiny finger-like structures that absorb most nutrients into the blood.` },
    { q:`What is the main role of the large intestine?`, ch:[`Absorbing most nutrients`,`Producing digestive acid`,`Absorbing water and forming solid waste`,`Breaking down proteins`], ans:2, exp:`The large intestine absorbs remaining water from digested material and compacts the leftover waste for elimination.` },
    { q:`Which organ produces bile, a substance that helps break down fats?`, ch:[`The liver`,`The kidney`,`The gallbladder`,`The pancreas`], ans:0, exp:`The liver produces bile, which is stored in the gallbladder and released to help digest and absorb fats.` },
    { q:`Which tube connects the mouth to the stomach and moves swallowed food downward?`, ch:[`The trachea`,`The small intestine`,`The esophagus`,`The pancreas`], ans:2, exp:`The esophagus is a muscular tube that uses wave-like contractions to push swallowed food down into the stomach.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd031', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`The Nervous System Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which two structures make up the central nervous system?`, ch:[`The brain and spinal cord`,`Nerves and muscles`,`The brain and nerves in the arms`,`The spinal cord and heart`], ans:0, exp:`The central nervous system is made up of the brain and spinal cord, which process information from the rest of the body.` },
    { q:`What is the basic functional cell of the nervous system that transmits signals?`, ch:[`Red blood cell`,`Neuron`,`Osteocyte`,`Platelet`], ans:1, exp:`Neurons are specialized cells that send electrical and chemical signals, forming the communication network of the nervous system.` },
    { q:`What is the small gap between two neurons where chemical signals pass from one cell to the next called?`, ch:[`Axon`,`Synapse`,`Dendrite`,`Cortex`], ans:1, exp:`A synapse is the junction where a neuron releases chemical messengers called neurotransmitters to communicate with the next neuron.` },
    { q:`Which part of the nervous system controls involuntary actions like heart rate and digestion?`, ch:[`The peripheral nervous system's sensory branch`,`The skeletal muscles`,`The autonomic nervous system`,`The cerebellum only`], ans:2, exp:`The autonomic nervous system manages automatic body functions such as heart rate, digestion, and breathing without conscious effort.` },
    { q:`What is a reflex, like pulling your hand away from something hot?`, ch:[`A slow, carefully planned response`,`A fast, automatic response that often bypasses conscious thought`,`A type of long-term memory`,`A response controlled entirely by the digestive system`], ans:1, exp:`Reflexes are quick, automatic responses to stimuli that often happen through the spinal cord before the brain fully processes the event, helping protect the body.` },
    { q:`Which part of the brain is primarily responsible for coordinating balance and voluntary muscle movement?`, ch:[`The cerebellum`,`The liver`,`The trachea`,`The pancreas`], ans:0, exp:`The cerebellum, located at the back of the brain, helps coordinate balance, posture, and smooth, precise muscle movements.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd032', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`The Immune System 101`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the general job of the immune system?`, ch:[`To pump blood throughout the body`,`To defend the body against harmful germs and other threats`,`To digest food into nutrients`,`To control body temperature only`], ans:1, exp:`The immune system protects the body by detecting and fighting off pathogens like bacteria, viruses, and other harmful invaders.` },
    { q:`Which white blood cells produce antibodies that target specific germs?`, ch:[`Red blood cells`,`Platelets`,`B cells`,`Bone cells`], ans:2, exp:`B cells are a type of white blood cell that produce antibodies, proteins that latch onto specific germs and help neutralize them.` },
    { q:`What is the immune system's "first line of defense," acting as a physical barrier against germs?`, ch:[`Antibodies`,`Skin and mucous membranes`,`White blood cells`,`Lymph nodes`], ans:1, exp:`Skin and mucous membranes physically block many germs from ever entering the body, making them the first barrier of defense.` },
    { q:`What is the general purpose of a fever during an infection?`, ch:[`It signals the body is completely healthy`,`It helps create an environment that makes it harder for some germs to survive`,`It has no known purpose`,`It stops the heart from beating too fast`], ans:1, exp:`A fever raises body temperature, which can slow the growth of some pathogens and help immune cells work more effectively.` },
    { q:`What are lymph nodes, and what role do they play in immunity?`, ch:[`Small bones that produce hormones`,`Small structures that filter lymph fluid and house immune cells that fight infection`,`Muscles that pump blood`,`Parts of the digestive tract`], ans:1, exp:`Lymph nodes filter lymph fluid and contain immune cells that help detect and respond to infections, which is why they can swell when you're sick.` },
    { q:`What does it mean when someone develops "immunity" to a specific disease after being infected once?`, ch:[`Their body can no longer fight any infections`,`Their immune system remembers the germ and can respond faster if exposed again`,`Their skin becomes thicker`,`Their blood type changes`], ans:1, exp:`After fighting an infection, the immune system often keeps memory cells that recognize the same germ quickly in the future, allowing a faster, stronger response.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd033', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Nutrition Basics: Macros & Micros`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which of the following is considered a macronutrient?`, ch:[`Vitamin C`,`Iron`,`Protein`,`Calcium`], ans:2, exp:`Protein, along with carbohydrates and fats, is a macronutrient because the body needs it in relatively large amounts for energy and building tissue.` },
    { q:`What is the primary role of carbohydrates in the diet?`, ch:[`Building antibodies`,`Providing the body's main quick source of energy`,`Carrying oxygen in the blood`,`Forming bone structure`], ans:1, exp:`Carbohydrates are broken down into glucose, which is the body's preferred and most readily available source of energy.` },
    { q:`Which nutrient is essential for building and repairing muscle tissue?`, ch:[`Protein`,`Fiber`,`Vitamin D`,`Sodium`], ans:0, exp:`Protein provides amino acids, the building blocks the body uses to grow and repair muscles and other tissues.` },
    { q:`Vitamins and minerals are needed in much smaller amounts than macronutrients, so they are known as what?`, ch:[`Empty calories`,`Micronutrients`,`Preservatives`,`Additives`], ans:1, exp:`Vitamins and minerals are called micronutrients because the body only needs small amounts, though they're still essential for health.` },
    { q:`Which mineral is especially important for building strong bones and teeth?`, ch:[`Calcium`,`Vitamin C`,`Fiber`,`Sodium`], ans:0, exp:`Calcium is a key mineral used to build and maintain strong bones and teeth throughout life.` },
    { q:`What is dietary fiber, and why is it important?`, ch:[`A type of fat that provides quick energy`,`An indigestible carbohydrate that supports healthy digestion`,`A vitamin found only in meat`,`A mineral needed for blood clotting`], ans:1, exp:`Fiber is a type of carbohydrate the body can't fully digest, but it helps keep the digestive system moving smoothly and supports gut health.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd034', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Vaccines & How They Work`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the basic idea behind how a vaccine protects you from a disease?`, ch:[`It cures the disease after you already have severe symptoms`,`It trains your immune system to recognize a germ before you're exposed to the real thing`,`It replaces your blood with antibodies from another person`,`It permanently removes the germ from the environment`], ans:1, exp:`Vaccines expose your immune system to a harmless piece or weakened form of a germ so it can learn to recognize and fight the real thing later.` },
    { q:`After vaccination, what does the immune system typically create that helps it respond faster if it meets the real germ later?`, ch:[`Extra red blood cells`,`Memory cells`,`New bones`,`Extra platelets`], ans:1, exp:`Vaccines prompt the immune system to produce memory cells, which "remember" the germ and allow a quicker, stronger response upon future exposure.` },
    { q:`Why do vaccines typically not cause the full-blown disease they protect against?`, ch:[`They contain no biological material at all`,`They use a weakened, inactivated, or partial form of the germ that can't cause serious illness in a healthy person`,`They are taken only after a person has recovered from the disease`,`They work by destroying the immune system`], ans:1, exp:`Vaccines are designed using weakened, killed, or partial versions of a germ, or instructions to make a harmless piece of it, so they train immunity without causing the actual disease.` },
    { q:`What term describes the community-level protection that happens when enough people are vaccinated, making it harder for a disease to spread?`, ch:[`Herd immunity`,`Passive immunity`,`Autoimmunity`,`Cross-reactivity`], ans:0, exp:`Herd immunity occurs when a high enough percentage of a population is immune, which slows or stops disease spread and helps protect those who can't be vaccinated.` },
    { q:`Which of these is a routine childhood vaccine used to prevent a serious disease?`, ch:[`Insulin`,`Measles vaccine`,`Ibuprofen`,`Antibiotic ointment`], ans:1, exp:`The measles vaccine is a standard part of childhood immunization schedules and has dramatically reduced measles cases worldwide.` },
    { q:`Why might a vaccine cause mild, short-term symptoms like a sore arm or slight fever?`, ch:[`Because it means the vaccine failed`,`Because it can be a normal sign that the immune system is actively responding and building protection`,`Because the vaccine is defective`,`Because it means the person is now contagious`], ans:1, exp:`Mild, temporary symptoms after vaccination often reflect the immune system actively engaging and building a protective response, not a problem with the vaccine.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd035', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Common Illnesses: Bacteria vs Viruses`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which of the following is a key difference between bacteria and viruses?`, ch:[`Bacteria are living cells that can reproduce on their own, while viruses need a host cell to reproduce`,`Viruses are always larger than bacteria`,`Bacteria cannot cause disease`,`Viruses are a type of bacteria`], ans:0, exp:`Bacteria are single-celled living organisms that can often reproduce independently, while viruses aren't considered fully alive on their own and must hijack a host cell's machinery to reproduce.` },
    { q:`Why don't antibiotics work against viral infections like the common cold?`, ch:[`Antibiotics only work at night`,`Antibiotics target processes specific to bacteria, which viruses don't have`,`Viruses are immune to all medicines`,`Antibiotics are too weak to enter the body`], ans:1, exp:`Antibiotics work by targeting structures or processes found in bacteria, such as their cell walls; viruses lack these features, so antibiotics have no effect on them.` },
    { q:`Which of these illnesses is typically caused by a virus rather than bacteria?`, ch:[`Strep throat`,`Influenza (the flu)`,`Bacterial pneumonia`,`Urinary tract infection`], ans:1, exp:`Influenza, or the flu, is caused by a virus, which is why antibiotics aren't an effective treatment for it.` },
    { q:`Which of these illnesses is commonly caused by bacteria and can be treated with antibiotics?`, ch:[`The common cold`,`Chickenpox`,`Strep throat`,`Influenza`], ans:2, exp:`Strep throat is caused by Streptococcus bacteria, so it can typically be treated effectively with an appropriate antibiotic.` },
    { q:`What is a practical reason doctors avoid prescribing antibiotics unnecessarily for viral illnesses?`, ch:[`Antibiotics are too expensive to produce`,`Overusing antibiotics can contribute to bacteria evolving resistance, making future infections harder to treat`,`Antibiotics make viral illnesses more contagious`,`There is no reason; it's just precaution`], ans:1, exp:`Overusing antibiotics gives bacteria more chances to develop resistance, which can make truly needed treatments less effective in the future.` },
    { q:`How do many viruses primarily spread from person to person, such as with a cold or the flu?`, ch:[`Only through direct blood transfusion`,`Through respiratory droplets from coughing, sneezing, or close contact`,`Only through contaminated water`,`They cannot spread between people`], ans:1, exp:`Many respiratory viruses spread through tiny droplets released when an infected person coughs, sneezes, or talks, or by touching contaminated surfaces.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd036', cat:'Life Sciences', title:`Healthy Habits & Preventive Care`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which of these is generally recommended as part of a healthy amount of sleep for teenagers?`, ch:[`3 to 4 hours per night`,`8 to 10 hours per night`,`Sleep is not important for teens`,`12 to 14 hours per night`], ans:1, exp:`Health experts generally recommend that teenagers get about 8 to 10 hours of sleep per night to support growth, learning, and overall health.` },
    { q:`Why is regular physical activity considered an important preventive health habit?`, ch:[`It has no effect on long-term health`,`It only benefits athletes`,`It helps support heart health, mood, and overall fitness`,`It weakens the immune system`], ans:2, exp:`Regular exercise supports cardiovascular health, strengthens muscles and bones, improves mood, and helps maintain a healthy body overall.` },
    { q:`What is the main purpose of a routine annual check-up with a doctor, even when you feel healthy?`, ch:[`It's only needed when you're already sick`,`To catch potential health issues early and track overall wellness`,`It replaces the need for vaccines`,`It is only for adults over 60`], ans:1, exp:`Routine check-ups help doctors monitor growth and health trends and catch potential problems early, often before symptoms even appear.` },
    { q:`Which simple habit is one of the most effective ways to prevent the spread of many common illnesses?`, ch:[`Avoiding all exercise`,`Washing your hands regularly with soap and water`,`Skipping meals`,`Sleeping less than 5 hours a night`], ans:1, exp:`Washing hands regularly helps remove germs before they can spread to your eyes, nose, mouth, or to other people.` },
    { q:`Why is staying adequately hydrated by drinking water considered an important daily health habit?`, ch:[`Water has no real function in the body`,`The body needs water to help regulate temperature, aid digestion, and support nearly every cell function`,`Only athletes need to stay hydrated`,`Hydration only matters in extremely hot weather`], ans:1, exp:`Water plays a role in nearly every body process, including regulating temperature, aiding digestion, and helping cells function properly, so staying hydrated matters every day.` },
    { q:`What is the benefit of routine dental check-ups as part of preventive care?`, ch:[`They are purely cosmetic and have no health benefit`,`They help catch cavities and gum problems early, before they become more serious`,`They replace the need for brushing teeth`,`They are only necessary after age 50`], ans:1, exp:`Regular dental visits allow a dentist to catch small issues like cavities early, which is easier and less invasive to treat than waiting until problems worsen.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd037', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Atoms 101: Protons, Neutrons, Electrons`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which subatomic particle carries a positive electric charge?`, ch:[`Electron`, `Proton`, `Neutron`, `Photon`], ans:1, exp:`Protons carry a positive charge and are found in the nucleus of the atom.` },
    { q:`Where are protons and neutrons located in an atom?`, ch:[`In the electron cloud`, `Spread evenly throughout the atom`, `In the nucleus`, `Outside the atom`], ans:2, exp:`Protons and neutrons are packed tightly together in the tiny, dense nucleus at the center of the atom.` },
    { q:`What is the charge of a neutron?`, ch:[`Positive`, `Negative`, `Neutral (no charge)`, `It varies`], ans:2, exp:`Neutrons have no electric charge, which is why they are called "neutral."` },
    { q:`What determines the identity of an element?`, ch:[`Number of neutrons`, `Number of electrons`, `Number of protons`, `Total mass of the atom`], ans:2, exp:`The number of protons, called the atomic number, is unique to each element and defines what element it is.` },
    { q:`In a neutral atom, how does the number of electrons compare to the number of protons?`, ch:[`Electrons are always double the protons`, `Electrons equal protons`, `Electrons are always fewer than protons`, `There is no relationship`], ans:1, exp:`A neutral atom has no overall charge, so it must have equal numbers of positively charged protons and negatively charged electrons.` },
    { q:`Which particle is responsible for forming chemical bonds between atoms?`, ch:[`Protons`, `Neutrons`, `The nucleus as a whole`, `Electrons`], ans:3, exp:`Electrons, especially the outermost ones, are shared or transferred between atoms to form chemical bonds.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd038', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`The Periodic Table: How It's Organized`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Elements on the periodic table are arranged in order of increasing what?`, ch:[`Atomic mass only`, `Atomic number`, `Alphabetical name`, `Discovery date`], ans:1, exp:`The modern periodic table lists elements in order of increasing atomic number, which is the number of protons.` },
    { q:`Who is most credited with creating an early version of the periodic table that grouped elements by similar properties?`, ch:[`Isaac Newton`, `Marie Curie`, `Dmitri Mendeleev`, `Albert Einstein`], ans:2, exp:`Dmitri Mendeleev arranged elements in a table in 1869 and even left gaps to predict undiscovered elements.` },
    { q:`Each box on the periodic table typically shows an element's symbol along with what other information?`, ch:[`Its melting point only`, `Its atomic number and atomic mass`, `Its color when burned`, `The year it was discovered`], ans:1, exp:`A standard periodic table entry includes the element symbol, name, atomic number, and average atomic mass.` },
    { q:`Why did Mendeleev leave some blank spaces in his original periodic table?`, ch:[`He ran out of room`, `Those spots were reserved for elements not yet discovered`, `He made a mistake`, `Those elements were considered unimportant`], ans:1, exp:`Mendeleev correctly predicted that certain elements existed but had not yet been discovered, leaving gaps for them.` },
    { q:`What is the one-, two-, or three-letter abbreviation for an element called?`, ch:[`Its formula`, `Its symbol`, `Its isotope`, `Its compound name`], ans:1, exp:`Each element has a unique chemical symbol, such as O for oxygen or Na for sodium.` },
    { q:`Roughly how many naturally occurring and man-made elements appear on a modern periodic table?`, ch:[`About 20`, `About 50`, `Around 118`, `Over 500`], ans:2, exp:`As of today, the periodic table contains 118 confirmed elements, from hydrogen to oganesson.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd039', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Periodic Trends Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`As you move from left to right across a period (row), atomic size generally does what?`, ch:[`Increases`, `Decreases`, `Stays the same`, `Changes randomly`], ans:1, exp:`Moving left to right, protons increase and pull the electron cloud in tighter, so atoms generally get smaller.` },
    { q:`As you move down a group (column) on the periodic table, atomic size generally does what?`, ch:[`Increases`, `Decreases`, `Stays exactly the same`, `Becomes zero`], ans:0, exp:`Moving down a group adds new electron shells, so atoms generally get larger.` },
    { q:`Which region of the periodic table generally has the highest electronegativity (strongest pull on electrons)?`, ch:[`Bottom-left`, `Top-right (excluding noble gases)`, `The very center`, `Electronegativity is the same everywhere`], ans:1, exp:`Electronegativity increases toward the top-right of the table, making elements like fluorine and oxygen strong electron attractors.` },
    { q:`What is ionization energy?`, ch:[`The energy released when two atoms bond`, `The energy needed to remove an electron from an atom`, `The energy stored in a neutron`, `The heat needed to melt a solid`], ans:1, exp:`Ionization energy is the amount of energy required to pull an electron away from a neutral atom.` },
    { q:`Generally, how does ionization energy change as you move across a period from left to right?`, ch:[`It decreases`, `It generally increases`, `It disappears`, `It has no pattern at all`], ans:1, exp:`Atoms on the right side hold their electrons more tightly due to a stronger nuclear pull, so it takes more energy to remove one.` },
    { q:`Which group of elements typically has the lowest ionization energy, making them easily give up an electron?`, ch:[`Noble gases`, `Halogens`, `Alkali metals`, `Transition metals`], ans:2, exp:`Alkali metals (Group 1) have just one loosely held outer electron, so they lose it easily and have low ionization energy.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd040', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Ionic Bonds Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`An ionic bond forms primarily through what process between atoms?`, ch:[`Sharing electrons equally`, `Transferring electrons from one atom to another`, `Combining neutrons`, `Merging nuclei together`], ans:1, exp:`In ionic bonding, one atom gives up one or more electrons to another atom, creating oppositely charged ions that attract each other.` },
    { q:`What is an ion?`, ch:[`An atom with an unusual number of neutrons`, `A molecule made of two identical atoms`, `An atom or group of atoms with an electric charge`, `A type of chemical bond`], ans:2, exp:`An ion is formed when an atom gains or loses electrons, giving it an overall positive or negative charge.` },
    { q:`When sodium (Na) reacts with chlorine (Cl) to form table salt, what happens to the sodium atom?`, ch:[`It gains an electron and becomes negatively charged`, `It loses an electron and becomes positively charged`, `It shares electrons equally with chlorine`, `It gains a proton`], ans:1, exp:`Sodium gives away its one outer electron to chlorine, becoming a positively charged Na+ ion.` },
    { q:`A positively charged ion is called a(n):`, ch:[`Anion`, `Cation`, `Isotope`, `Neutron`], ans:1, exp:`A cation is an ion that has lost electrons and carries a positive charge.` },
    { q:`Ionic compounds like table salt (NaCl) tend to form what kind of structure?`, ch:[`A loose gas cloud`, `A tightly bonded repeating crystal lattice`, `A single large molecule with no pattern`, `A liquid at room temperature`], ans:1, exp:`Oppositely charged ions arrange themselves into an organized, repeating crystal lattice held together by electrical attraction.` },
    { q:`Which pair of elements would most likely form an ionic bond?`, ch:[`Two nonmetals like oxygen and hydrogen`, `A metal like potassium and a nonmetal like fluorine`, `Two identical metal atoms`, `Two noble gas atoms`], ans:1, exp:`Ionic bonds typically form between a metal, which easily loses electrons, and a nonmetal, which readily gains them.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd041', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Covalent Bonds Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`A covalent bond forms when two atoms do what?`, ch:[`Transfer electrons completely`, `Share one or more pairs of electrons`, `Repel each other`, `Combine their nuclei`], ans:1, exp:`In covalent bonding, atoms share electron pairs so that both atoms can feel more stable, like having a "full" outer shell.` },
    { q:`Covalent bonds most commonly form between which types of atoms?`, ch:[`Two metals`, `A metal and a nonmetal`, `Two nonmetals`, `Two noble gases`], ans:2, exp:`Covalent bonding typically occurs between nonmetal atoms, which both tend to attract electrons rather than give them away.` },
    { q:`In a water molecule (H2O), how many electron pairs does the oxygen atom share in total with the two hydrogen atoms?`, ch:[`One`, `Two`, `Three`, `Four`], ans:1, exp:`Oxygen forms a single covalent bond (one shared pair) with each of the two hydrogen atoms, for a total of two shared pairs.` },
    { q:`What is a double covalent bond?`, ch:[`Two separate atoms bonded to a third atom`, `Two pairs of electrons shared between the same two atoms`, `A bond that only lasts twice as long`, `Two atoms sharing a proton`], ans:1, exp:`A double bond occurs when two atoms share two pairs of electrons instead of just one, as seen in molecules like O2.` },
    { q:`Compared to ionic compounds, molecular (covalent) compounds like sugar typically have:`, ch:[`Much higher melting points`, `Generally lower melting points`, `No melting point at all`, `Always higher electrical conductivity when dissolved`], ans:1, exp:`Covalent compounds are usually made of individual molecules held together by weaker forces between molecules, so they tend to melt at lower temperatures than ionic crystals.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is an example of a molecule held together by covalent bonds?`, ch:[`Table salt (NaCl)`, `Sodium metal (Na)`, `Carbon dioxide (CO2)`, `Iron (Fe)`], ans:2, exp:`Carbon dioxide is made of a carbon atom covalently bonded to two oxygen atoms, sharing electron pairs rather than transferring them.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd042', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`States of Matter`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`In which state of matter do particles have a fixed shape and a fixed volume?`, ch:[`Gas`, `Liquid`, `Solid`, `Plasma`], ans:2, exp:`In a solid, particles are packed tightly in fixed positions, giving it a definite shape and volume.` },
    { q:`Which state of matter takes the shape of its container but keeps a constant volume?`, ch:[`Solid`, `Liquid`, `Gas`, `None of these`], ans:1, exp:`A liquid's particles can move past each other, so it flows to fit its container, but it still has a fixed volume.` },
    { q:`What is the process called when a solid changes directly into a gas without becoming a liquid first?`, ch:[`Condensation`, `Sublimation`, `Freezing`, `Evaporation`], ans:1, exp:`Sublimation is when a solid, like dry ice, turns straight into a gas, skipping the liquid stage.` },
    { q:`What happens to the particles in a substance as it is heated and changes from solid to liquid to gas?`, ch:[`They move slower and get closer together`, `They move faster and spread farther apart`, `They stop moving entirely`, `They lose mass`], ans:1, exp:`Adding heat energy makes particles move faster and increases the space between them, which is why gases are the most spread out.` },
    { q:`What is the term for a liquid turning into a gas?`, ch:[`Melting`, `Deposition`, `Vaporization (evaporation)`, `Freezing`], ans:2, exp:`Vaporization, often called evaporation, is the change from liquid to gas as particles gain enough energy to escape the liquid.` },
    { q:`What is plasma, often called the fourth state of matter?`, ch:[`A very cold solid`, `A liquid with extra minerals`, `A gas so hot that its atoms lose electrons and become charged`, `A mixture of solid and liquid`], ans:2, exp:`Plasma forms when a gas is heated so much that electrons are stripped away, creating a mix of charged particles, like in stars or lightning.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd043', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Mixtures vs Pure Substances`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`A pure substance is made up of what?`, ch:[`Two or more materials combined in any ratio`, `Only one type of atom or molecule throughout`, `Only liquids`, `Anything found in nature`], ans:1, exp:`A pure substance, like distilled water or gold, has a consistent, uniform composition throughout.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is an example of a mixture rather than a pure substance?`, ch:[`Pure oxygen gas (O2)`, `Distilled water`, `Salad`, `A gold bar`], ans:2, exp:`A salad contains many different ingredients that are physically combined but not chemically bonded, making it a mixture.` },
    { q:`What distinguishes a homogeneous mixture from a heterogeneous mixture?`, ch:[`Homogeneous mixtures have visibly different parts, heterogeneous do not`, `Homogeneous mixtures look uniform throughout, heterogeneous mixtures have visibly distinct parts`, `Heterogeneous mixtures are always gases`, `There is no real difference`], ans:1, exp:`Homogeneous mixtures, like saltwater, look the same throughout, while heterogeneous mixtures, like sand and gravel, have visible differences from spot to spot.` },
    { q:`Which of these is the best example of a homogeneous mixture?`, ch:[`Oil and water`, `A bowl of trail mix`, `Sweetened iced tea (fully dissolved)`, `A pile of mixed coins`], ans:2, exp:`Once sugar is fully dissolved in tea, the mixture looks uniform throughout, making it homogeneous.` },
    { q:`How can the components of a mixture typically be separated?`, ch:[`Only through a nuclear reaction`, `They cannot be separated`, `Through physical methods like filtering, evaporating, or sorting`, `By creating a new element`], ans:2, exp:`Because mixtures are combined physically rather than chemically, they can usually be separated using physical techniques such as filtration or evaporation.` },
    { q:`Why can't a pure substance be separated by simple physical methods like filtering?`, ch:[`Because it has only one type of particle uniformly making it up`, `Because it is always a gas`, `Because it has no mass`, `Because it is always man-made`], ans:0, exp:`Since a pure substance is made of just one kind of atom or molecule, there is nothing different to physically separate out.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd044', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Elements, Compounds & Molecules`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is an element?`, ch:[`A substance made of two or more different atoms chemically combined`, `A pure substance made of only one type of atom`, `Any mixture of two liquids`, `A substance that cannot be found on the periodic table`], ans:1, exp:`An element, like carbon or oxygen, consists of only one kind of atom and cannot be broken down into simpler substances by chemical means.` },
    { q:`What is a compound?`, ch:[`Two or more different elements chemically bonded together in a fixed ratio`, `Any two substances mixed together`, `A single atom by itself`, `A type of subatomic particle`], ans:0, exp:`A compound forms when two or more different elements chemically combine in a set ratio, such as water (H2O), which always has two hydrogens per oxygen.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is a molecule?`, ch:[`A single, isolated proton`, `Two or more atoms bonded together`, `An empty container`, `A random pile of unrelated atoms`], ans:1, exp:`A molecule is formed whenever two or more atoms bond together, whether they are the same element (like O2) or different elements (like H2O).` },
    { q:`Which of these is an example of an element, not a compound?`, ch:[`Carbon dioxide (CO2)`, `Table salt (NaCl)`, `Oxygen gas (O2)`, `Water (H2O)`], ans:2, exp:`Oxygen gas is made of only one type of atom, oxygen, bonded together, so it is an element rather than a compound.` },
    { q:`Can all compounds be classified as molecules?`, ch:[`No, compounds are never molecules`, `Yes, since compounds involve two or more atoms bonded together, they are also molecules`, `Only compounds that are gases count as molecules`, `Only compounds with metals count as molecules`], ans:1, exp:`Since a compound always has atoms bonded together, it fits the definition of a molecule too — though not all molecules are compounds (like O2).` },
    { q:`Why can't a compound be separated into its elements using simple physical methods like stirring or filtering?`, ch:[`Because the atoms are chemically bonded and require a chemical reaction to separate`, `Because compounds don't actually contain atoms`, `Because compounds are always gases`, `Because it is against the law of conservation of mass`], ans:0, exp:`Since the atoms in a compound are held together by strong chemical bonds, breaking them apart requires a chemical reaction, not just physical mixing or sorting.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd045', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Reading the Periodic Table: Groups & Periods`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is a vertical column on the periodic table called?`, ch:[`A period`, `A group (or family)`, `A shell`, `A row`], ans:1, exp:`Vertical columns are called groups or families, and elements within a group tend to share similar chemical properties.` },
    { q:`What is a horizontal row on the periodic table called?`, ch:[`A period`, `A group`, `A family`, `An orbital`], ans:0, exp:`Horizontal rows are called periods, and elements in the same period have the same number of electron shells.` },
    { q:`Elements in the same group tend to have similar chemical properties mainly because they have the same:`, ch:[`Atomic mass`, `Number of neutrons`, `Number of valence (outermost) electrons`, `Color`], ans:2, exp:`Elements in a group share the same number of valence electrons, which largely determines how an element reacts chemically.` },
    { q:`What are the elements in Group 18, on the far right of the periodic table, commonly called?`, ch:[`Alkali metals`, `Halogens`, `Noble gases`, `Transition metals`], ans:2, exp:`Group 18 elements, like helium and neon, are called noble gases because they have full outer shells and rarely react with other elements.` },
    { q:`What are the elements in Group 1 (excluding hydrogen) commonly called?`, ch:[`Alkali metals`, `Alkaline earth metals`, `Halogens`, `Noble gases`], ans:0, exp:`Group 1 elements, such as lithium and sodium, are called alkali metals and are known for being highly reactive with just one valence electron.` },
    { q:`As you move across a period from left to right, what generally happens to the number of protons?`, ch:[`It decreases by one each time`, `It stays exactly the same`, `It increases by one for each successive element`, `It resets to zero`], ans:2, exp:`Each element to the right has one more proton than the element before it, which is why atomic number increases across a period.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd046', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Isotopes & Atomic Mass Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is an isotope?`, ch:[`An atom with a different number of protons than usual`, `A version of an element with a different number of neutrons`, `An atom that has lost an electron`, `A completely different element`], ans:1, exp:`Isotopes are atoms of the same element (same number of protons) that have different numbers of neutrons.` },
    { q:`Carbon-12 and Carbon-14 are both isotopes of carbon. What is different between them?`, ch:[`Carbon-14 has more protons`, `Carbon-14 has more electrons in a neutral atom`, `Carbon-14 has more neutrons`, `They are actually different elements`], ans:2, exp:`The number after the element name refers to the mass number (protons + neutrons), so carbon-14 has two more neutrons than carbon-12.` },
    { q:`The atomic mass listed on the periodic table for an element is best described as:`, ch:[`The mass of the most common isotope only`, `A weighted average of the masses of all naturally occurring isotopes`, `Always a whole number`, `The number of protons plus electrons`], ans:1, exp:`Since elements exist as a mix of isotopes in nature, the periodic table reports a weighted average based on how common each isotope is.` },
    { q:`If an atom has 6 protons and 8 neutrons, what is its mass number?`, ch:[`6`, `8`, `14`, `2`], ans:2, exp:`Mass number equals protons plus neutrons, so 6 protons + 8 neutrons = 14.` },
    { q:`Why do isotopes of the same element behave almost identically in chemical reactions?`, ch:[`Because they have the same number of protons and electrons, which control chemical behavior`, `Because they have the same number of neutrons`, `Because they always have the same mass`, `Isotopes cannot undergo chemical reactions`], ans:0, exp:`Chemical behavior depends mainly on protons and electrons, and isotopes of an element share the same number of both; only the neutron count differs.` },
    { q:`Some isotopes are unstable and break down over time, releasing radiation. What term describes this process?`, ch:[`Sublimation`, `Radioactive decay`, `Ionization`, `Neutralization`], ans:1, exp:`Unstable isotopes lose energy through radioactive decay, a process used in fields like medical imaging and cancer treatment.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd047', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Metals, Nonmetals & Metalloids`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Where are most metals located on the periodic table?`, ch:[`On the far right`, `In a thin zigzag line down the middle`, `On the left and in the middle`, `Only in the bottom row`], ans:2, exp:`The majority of elements, located on the left side and in the middle of the table, are classified as metals.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is a typical physical property of metals?`, ch:[`Brittle and dull`, `Poor conductors of electricity`, `Shiny and good conductors of heat and electricity`, `Always gases at room temperature`], ans:2, exp:`Metals are generally shiny (lustrous), malleable, and conduct heat and electricity well.` },
    { q:`Where are nonmetals mostly found on the periodic table?`, ch:[`Upper right side`, `Lower left side`, `Directly in the center`, `Evenly spread throughout`], ans:0, exp:`Most nonmetals are clustered in the upper right portion of the periodic table (with hydrogen as an exception).` },
    { q:`Which of these best describes metalloids?`, ch:[`Elements that are always liquid at room temperature`, `Elements with properties in between metals and nonmetals`, `Elements found only in group 18`, `Elements that never conduct electricity`], ans:1, exp:`Metalloids, such as silicon and boron, sit along the zigzag line on the periodic table and share some properties of both metals and nonmetals.` },
    { q:`Silicon, a well-known metalloid, is especially valued in the electronics industry because it is:`, ch:[`A perfect insulator`, `A perfect conductor like copper`, `A semiconductor, conducting electricity under certain conditions`, `Radioactive`], ans:2, exp:`Silicon's semiconducting properties, partway between a conductor and an insulator, make it essential for computer chips.` },
    { q:`Which property is typical of nonmetals compared to metals?`, ch:[`They are usually malleable and can be hammered into sheets`, `They tend to be brittle in solid form and poor conductors of electricity`, `They are always shiny`, `They are always found as pure elements in nature`], ans:1, exp:`Unlike metals, solid nonmetals tend to be brittle rather than malleable, and they generally do not conduct electricity well.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd048', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Intro to the Mole Concept`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`In chemistry, a "mole" is best described as:`, ch:[`A small burrowing animal`, `A unit representing a specific number of particles, like a chemist's "dozen"`, `A type of chemical bond`, `A unit of temperature`], ans:1, exp:`Just like "a dozen" always means 12, "a mole" always means a specific, huge number of particles, useful for counting atoms or molecules.` },
    { q:`Approximately how many particles are in one mole of a substance?`, ch:[`12`, `1,000`, `6.022 x 10^23`, `1,000,000`], ans:2, exp:`This huge number, called Avogadro's number, lets chemists count incredibly tiny particles like atoms by weighing out measurable amounts.` },
    { q:`Why is the mole a useful unit in chemistry?`, ch:[`It lets chemists work with a countable number of atoms or molecules using a measurable mass`, `It only applies to liquids`, `It measures temperature changes during reactions`, `It replaces the need for the periodic table`], ans:0, exp:`Atoms and molecules are far too small and numerous to count individually, so the mole connects a measurable mass to a specific particle count.` },
    { q:`The molar mass of an element, found using the periodic table, tells you:`, ch:[`The mass of a single atom in kilograms`, `The mass, in grams, of one mole of that element`, `The number of neutrons in the atom`, `The charge of the element's ion`], ans:1, exp:`Molar mass is expressed in grams per mole, and it matches the atomic mass number shown on the periodic table.` },
    { q:`The number of particles in a mole is named after which scientist?`, ch:[`Dmitri Mendeleev`, `Marie Curie`, `Amedeo Avogadro`, `John Dalton`], ans:2, exp:`This value is called Avogadro's number in honor of Amedeo Avogadro, whose work helped chemists relate particle counts to measurable quantities.` },
    { q:`If you have one mole of carbon atoms, roughly how many grams would that sample weigh?`, ch:[`About 1 gram`, `About 12 grams`, `About 100 grams`, `About 1,000 grams`], ans:1, exp:`Carbon's atomic mass is about 12, so one mole of carbon atoms weighs approximately 12 grams.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd049', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Chemical vs Physical Changes`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which of these is an example of a physical change?`, ch:[`Burning paper`,`Digesting food`,`Tearing a piece of paper`,`Rusting a nail`], ans:2, exp:`Tearing paper changes its shape and size, but it's still made of the same paper — no new substance is formed.` },
    { q:`What happens during a chemical change that does NOT happen during a physical change?`, ch:[`The state of matter always changes`,`One or more new substances with different properties are formed`,`The mass of the object always increases`,`The object always changes color`], ans:1, exp:`The defining feature of a chemical change is that new substances form, with different properties than what you started with.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is generally considered a clue that a chemical reaction has occurred?`, ch:[`The substance changes shape`,`Bubbles of gas form when two liquids are mixed`,`A solid is crushed into powder`,`A liquid is poured into a different container`], ans:1, exp:`Gas bubbles forming (when not just boiling) often signal that a new gaseous substance was created — a classic sign of a chemical change.` },
    { q:`Ice melting into liquid water is best classified as a...?`, ch:[`Chemical change, because water molecules break apart`,`Physical change, because the substance is still H2O`,`Chemical change, because energy is absorbed`,`Physical change, but only if the ice is very cold`], ans:1, exp:`Melting only changes the state of water from solid to liquid — the molecules themselves (H2O) stay the same.` },
    { q:`When wood burns in a campfire, it is classified as a chemical change because...`, ch:[`The wood gets smaller`,`New substances like ash, carbon dioxide, and water vapor are produced`,`The wood changes from a solid to a gas`,`The temperature of the wood increases`], ans:1, exp:`Burning wood produces entirely new substances (ash, CO2, water vapor) that have different properties than wood — that's what makes it chemical.` },
    { q:`If you dissolve table salt in water and then let the water evaporate, what do you observe, and what does it tell you?`, ch:[`The salt disappears forever, showing a chemical change occurred`,`The salt crystals reappear, showing that dissolving was a physical change`,`A new gas is produced, showing a chemical change occurred`,`The water turns into a solid, showing the salt reacted with it`], ans:1, exp:`Since the salt can be recovered unchanged after evaporation, no new substance was formed — dissolving salt in water is a physical change.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd050', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Balancing Chemical Equations 101`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`Why must chemical equations be balanced?`, ch:[`To make the reaction happen faster`,`To satisfy the law of conservation of mass — atoms can't be created or destroyed`,`To show which reactant is in excess`,`To make the equation look neater`], ans:1, exp:`Balancing ensures the same number of each type of atom appears on both sides, matching the law of conservation of mass.` },
    { q:`What is the correct coefficient needed to balance this equation: 2H2 + O2 → __H2O?`, ch:[`1`,`2`,`3`,`4`], ans:1, exp:`2H2 + O2 → 2H2O gives 4 hydrogen atoms and 2 oxygen atoms on both sides, so the correct coefficient is 2.` },
    { q:`Balance the equation: N2 + H2 → NH3. What are the correct coefficients (in order N2, H2, NH3)?`, ch:[`1, 1, 1`,`1, 3, 2`,`2, 3, 2`,`1, 2, 3`], ans:1, exp:`N2 + 3H2 → 2NH3 balances 2 nitrogen atoms and 6 hydrogen atoms on each side.` },
    { q:`In the balanced equation CH4 + 2O2 → CO2 + 2H2O, how many total oxygen atoms are on the reactant side?`, ch:[`2`,`3`,`4`,`6`], ans:2, exp:`2O2 means 2 molecules of O2, and each O2 has 2 oxygen atoms, so that's 4 oxygen atoms total.` },
    { q:`Which equation is correctly balanced?`, ch:[`Fe + O2 → Fe2O3`,`4Fe + 3O2 → 2Fe2O3`,`2Fe + O2 → Fe2O3`,`Fe + 3O2 → 2Fe2O3`], ans:1, exp:`4Fe + 3O2 → 2Fe2O3 gives 4 iron atoms and 6 oxygen atoms on both sides — everything checks out.` },
    { q:`If a balanced equation shows 2Na + Cl2 → 2NaCl, how many chlorine atoms are needed to react completely with 4 sodium atoms?`, ch:[`2`,`4`,`6`,`8`], ans:1, exp:`The ratio is 2 Na to 1 Cl2 (which has 2 Cl atoms). Doubling the sodium to 4 atoms means you need 2 Cl2 molecules, which is 4 chlorine atoms.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd051', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Types of Chemical Reactions`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`In a synthesis (combination) reaction, what generally happens?`, ch:[`A single compound breaks down into two or more simpler substances`,`Two or more substances combine to form a single new compound`,`One element replaces another in a compound`,`Two compounds exchange parts`], ans:1, exp:`Synthesis reactions build a bigger, more complex compound out of two or more simpler starting materials.` },
    { q:`2H2O → 2H2 + O2 is an example of what type of reaction?`, ch:[`Synthesis`,`Decomposition`,`Single replacement`,`Combustion`], ans:1, exp:`Here, one compound (water) breaks apart into two simpler substances (hydrogen and oxygen gas) — that's decomposition.` },
    { q:`Which equation represents a single-replacement reaction?`, ch:[`NaCl + AgNO3 → AgCl + NaNO3`,`Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl2 + H2`,`2H2 + O2 → 2H2O`,`CaCO3 → CaO + CO2`], ans:1, exp:`Zinc replaces the hydrogen in hydrochloric acid, so a single element swaps places with another element in a compound.` },
    { q:`In a double-replacement reaction, what typically occurs?`, ch:[`Two elements combine to form a compound`,`A compound decomposes into elements`,`The positive ions (cations) of two compounds swap partners`,`A hydrocarbon reacts with oxygen`], ans:2, exp:`In double-replacement reactions, two compounds trade their ions, forming two new compounds.` },
    { q:`Burning propane (C3H8) in a grill, producing CO2 and H2O, is an example of what reaction type?`, ch:[`Decomposition`,`Double replacement`,`Combustion`,`Synthesis`], ans:2, exp:`Combustion reactions involve a fuel reacting with oxygen to release energy, typically producing carbon dioxide and water.` },
    { q:`CaCO3 → CaO + CO2 is best classified as a...?`, ch:[`Synthesis reaction`,`Decomposition reaction`,`Single-replacement reaction`,`Combustion reaction`], ans:1, exp:`One compound (calcium carbonate) breaks down into two simpler substances — that's a decomposition reaction.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd052', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Acids & Bases Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What ion do acids release when dissolved in water?`, ch:[`Hydroxide ions (OH-)`,`Hydrogen ions (H+)`,`Sodium ions (Na+)`,`Chloride ions (Cl-)`], ans:1, exp:`Acids are defined by their ability to release hydrogen ions (H+) when dissolved in water.` },
    { q:`Which of these is a common household base?`, ch:[`Vinegar`,`Lemon juice`,`Baking soda (sodium bicarbonate)`,`Orange juice`], ans:2, exp:`Baking soda is a mild base, while vinegar, lemon juice, and orange juice are all acidic.` },
    { q:`What happens when you mix an acid and a base together?`, ch:[`They explode`,`They undergo a neutralization reaction, forming water and a salt`,`They always turn into a gas`,`Nothing happens; acids and bases don't react`], ans:1, exp:`Acids and bases react in a neutralization reaction, which typically produces water and a salt.` },
    { q:`Which taste is commonly associated with acids?`, ch:[`Bitter`,`Sour`,`Sweet`,`Salty`], ans:1, exp:`Acids like the citric acid in lemons taste sour — that's a classic (though not lab-safe) way to identify them.` },
    { q:`Bases often feel ___ to the touch (though you should never touch chemicals to test this in a lab!).`, ch:[`Sticky`,`Slippery`,`Gritty`,`Dry`], ans:1, exp:`Bases tend to feel slippery, similar to soap, which is actually a mild base.` },
    { q:`Which of these is an example of a strong acid found in your stomach that helps digest food?`, ch:[`Sodium hydroxide`,`Hydrochloric acid`,`Ammonia`,`Sodium bicarbonate`], ans:1, exp:`Your stomach produces hydrochloric acid (HCl) to help break down food and kill harmful bacteria.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd053', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`The pH Scale`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`The pH scale typically ranges from what values?`, ch:[`-10 to 10`,`0 to 14`,`1 to 100`,`0 to 7`], ans:1, exp:`The standard pH scale runs from 0 (very acidic) to 14 (very basic).` },
    { q:`A solution with a pH of 7 is considered...?`, ch:[`Strongly acidic`,`Neutral`,`Strongly basic`,`Radioactive`], ans:1, exp:`A pH of exactly 7, like pure water, is considered neutral — neither acidic nor basic.` },
    { q:`The lower the pH number (below 7), the more ___ the solution is.`, ch:[`Basic`,`Neutral`,`Acidic`,`Dense`], ans:2, exp:`As pH numbers decrease below 7, solutions become more acidic.` },
    { q:`Which pH value represents the most basic (alkaline) solution?`, ch:[`2`,`6`,`7`,`13`], ans:3, exp:`Higher pH values, closer to 14, indicate stronger bases, so 13 is the most basic option listed.` },
    { q:`Pure water has a pH of about...?`, ch:[`0`,`7`,`10`,`14`], ans:1, exp:`Pure water sits right in the middle of the pH scale at 7, making it neutral.` },
    { q:`A tool that uses color changes to estimate pH is called...?`, ch:[`A thermometer`,`A barometer`,`Litmus paper or pH paper`,`A graduated cylinder`], ans:2, exp:`Litmus and pH paper change color depending on how acidic or basic a solution is, giving a quick pH estimate.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd054', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Solutions: Solute, Solvent & Concentration`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`In a solution, the substance that gets dissolved is called the...?`, ch:[`Solvent`,`Solute`,`Precipitate`,`Catalyst`], ans:1, exp:`The solute is the substance being dissolved, like sugar stirred into tea.` },
    { q:`In a solution, the substance present in the greatest amount that does the dissolving is called the...?`, ch:[`Solute`,`Solvent`,`Suspension`,`Colloid`], ans:1, exp:`The solvent is the substance doing the dissolving and is usually present in the larger amount, like water in salt water.` },
    { q:`In salt water, which is the solvent?`, ch:[`Salt`,`Water`,`Both equally`,`Neither`], ans:1, exp:`Water dissolves the salt and is present in a much greater amount, making it the solvent.` },
    { q:`A solution that has a relatively large amount of solute dissolved in it is described as...?`, ch:[`Dilute`,`Concentrated`,`Saturated only`,`Neutral`], ans:1, exp:`A concentrated solution has a high ratio of solute to solvent, compared to a dilute solution which has less solute.` },
    { q:`A solution is "saturated" when...?`, ch:[`No solute has been added yet`,`The solvent has evaporated completely`,`It has dissolved the maximum amount of solute possible at that temperature`,`It has turned a different color`], ans:2, exp:`A saturated solution has reached its limit — no more solute will dissolve at that temperature without extra solute settling out.` },
    { q:`Which of the following generally increases how much solid solute can dissolve in water?`, ch:[`Lowering the temperature of the water`,`Raising the temperature of the water`,`Using less water`,`Stirring less`], ans:1, exp:`For most solids, warmer water can dissolve more solute than cold water, which is why sugar dissolves faster in hot tea.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd055', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Conservation of Mass`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`The Law of Conservation of Mass states that...?`, ch:[`Mass can be created but not destroyed`,`Mass can be destroyed but not created`,`Mass is neither created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction`,`Mass always increases during a reaction`], ans:2, exp:`In a chemical reaction, the total mass of the reactants always equals the total mass of the products — mass is conserved.` },
    { q:`If 10 grams of reactants undergo a chemical reaction in a sealed container, how much mass will the products have?`, ch:[`Less than 10 grams`,`Exactly 10 grams`,`More than 10 grams`,`It depends on the color of the products`], ans:1, exp:`Since the container is sealed and no matter enters or leaves, the total mass stays exactly the same before and after.` },
    { q:`Who is the scientist most credited with formulating the Law of Conservation of Mass?`, ch:[`Isaac Newton`,`Antoine Lavoisier`,`Charles Darwin`,`Marie Curie`], ans:1, exp:`Antoine Lavoisier, often called the "father of modern chemistry," is credited with establishing this law through careful experiments.` },
    { q:`If you burn a piece of paper in an open room, it seems like mass disappears. What actually happens?`, ch:[`Mass is truly destroyed`,`The gases produced (like CO2 and water vapor) escape into the air, carrying the mass with them`,`The paper turns into pure energy`,`The scale is broken`], ans:1, exp:`The mass isn't lost — it leaves as invisible gases, so if you captured everything produced, the total mass would match the paper you started with.` },
    { q:`Why must chemical equations be balanced according to conservation of mass?`, ch:[`So the reaction looks symmetrical`,`So the number of each type of atom is equal on both sides of the equation`,`So the reaction happens faster`,`So fewer chemicals are needed`], ans:1, exp:`Balancing equations ensures atoms aren't created or destroyed on paper, matching what actually happens in a real reaction.` },
    { q:`In a sealed, rigid container, the total mass before a chemical reaction compared to after should be...?`, ch:[`Always greater before`,`Always greater after`,`The same`,`Impossible to know`], ans:2, exp:`With nothing entering or leaving the sealed container, the total mass must stay the same throughout the reaction.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd056', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Endothermic vs Exothermic Reactions`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`An exothermic reaction is one that...?`, ch:[`Absorbs energy from its surroundings`,`Releases energy to its surroundings`,`Never involves heat`,`Only occurs at very low temperatures`], ans:1, exp:`Exothermic reactions release energy, often as heat, into their surroundings, which is why they can feel warm.` },
    { q:`Which of these is a classic example of an exothermic reaction?`, ch:[`Melting ice`,`Photosynthesis`,`Combustion (burning)`,`Cooking an egg with ice`], ans:2, exp:`Combustion, like burning wood or gasoline, releases a large amount of heat and light energy, making it exothermic.` },
    { q:`An endothermic reaction absorbs energy, which often causes the surrounding temperature to...?`, ch:[`Increase`,`Decrease`,`Stay exactly the same`,`Become radioactive`], ans:1, exp:`Since endothermic reactions pull energy in from their surroundings, the surrounding area often feels cooler.` },
    { q:`Instant cold packs used for sports injuries typically work through what kind of reaction?`, ch:[`Exothermic`,`Endothermic`,`Nuclear`,`Catalytic only`], ans:1, exp:`Cold packs use an endothermic reaction that absorbs heat from the surroundings, making the pack feel cold.` },
    { q:`In an exothermic reaction, if you touched the reaction container (safely, in a controlled lab setting), it would likely feel...?`, ch:[`Cold`,`Warm or hot`,`No different`,`Wet`], ans:1, exp:`Because exothermic reactions release energy as heat, the container often warms up during the reaction.` },
    { q:`Photosynthesis, where plants absorb sunlight energy to build sugar molecules, is best classified as...?`, ch:[`Exothermic`,`Endothermic`,`Neutral`,`A physical change`], ans:1, exp:`Photosynthesis requires plants to absorb energy from sunlight to drive the reaction, making it endothermic.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd057', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Density Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Density is defined as...?`, ch:[`Mass divided by volume`,`Volume divided by mass`,`Mass multiplied by volume`,`Weight divided by height`], ans:0, exp:`Density tells you how much mass is packed into a given volume, calculated as mass divided by volume.` },
    { q:`If an object is less dense than water, it will...?`, ch:[`Sink`,`Float`,`Dissolve`,`Explode`], ans:1, exp:`Objects less dense than the liquid they're placed in will float on top of it, like wood on water.` },
    { q:`Two objects have the same volume, but Object A has more mass than Object B. Which has a greater density?`, ch:[`Object A`,`Object B`,`They have equal density`,`Cannot be determined`], ans:0, exp:`With equal volumes, the object with more mass packed into that space has the higher density — that's Object A.` },
    { q:`What is a common unit used to express density?`, ch:[`Meters per second`,`Grams per cubic centimeter (g/cm3)`,`Newtons`,`Degrees Celsius`], ans:1, exp:`Density is commonly measured in grams per cubic centimeter (g/cm3) or grams per milliliter (g/mL) for liquids.` },
    { q:`Why does oil typically float on top of water?`, ch:[`Oil is denser than water`,`Oil is less dense than water`,`Oil evaporates faster`,`Oil is a solid at room temperature`], ans:1, exp:`Oil has a lower density than water, so it rises to the top when the two are mixed and left to settle.` },
    { q:`If you have 10 mL of a substance with a mass of 20 grams, what is its density?`, ch:[`0.5 g/mL`,`2 g/mL`,`10 g/mL`,`200 g/mL`], ans:1, exp:`Density equals mass divided by volume, so 20 grams divided by 10 mL gives a density of 2 g/mL.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd058', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Gas Laws: The Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`According to Boyle's Law, when the pressure on a gas increases at constant temperature, its volume...?`, ch:[`Increases`,`Decreases`,`Stays the same`,`Becomes zero`], ans:1, exp:`Boyle's Law describes an inverse relationship — as pressure goes up, the volume of a gas goes down, and vice versa.` },
    { q:`According to Charles's Law, when the temperature of a gas increases at constant pressure, its volume...?`, ch:[`Increases`,`Decreases`,`Stays the same`,`Turns to liquid`], ans:0, exp:`Charles's Law shows a direct relationship — heating a gas at constant pressure makes it expand and take up more volume.` },
    { q:`Which of the following best describes the relationship in Boyle's Law?`, ch:[`Direct relationship between pressure and volume`,`Inverse relationship between pressure and volume`,`No relationship at all`,`Direct relationship between pressure and temperature`], ans:1, exp:`Boyle's Law describes pressure and volume moving in opposite directions when temperature is held constant.` },
    { q:`Why does a sealed bag of chips seem to puff up when you take it to a higher altitude (lower outside pressure)?`, ch:[`The gas inside the bag heats up`,`The lower outside air pressure allows the gas inside to expand`,`The chips absorb more air`,`The bag material shrinks`], ans:1, exp:`With less pressure pushing in from outside, the gas trapped inside the bag expands, causing it to puff up — a real-world example of Boyle's Law.` },
    { q:`If you heat a balloon (without popping it), what generally happens to its volume, according to Charles's Law?`, ch:[`It shrinks`,`It stays exactly the same`,`It expands`,`It turns into a liquid`], ans:2, exp:`Heating the gas inside the balloon at roughly constant pressure causes it to expand, increasing the balloon's volume.` },
    { q:`The combined gas law relates which three properties of a gas?`, ch:[`Mass, color, and texture`,`Pressure, volume, and temperature`,`Density, taste, and smell`,`Speed, distance, and time`], ans:1, exp:`The combined gas law brings together pressure, volume, and temperature to describe how a gas behaves when more than one of these changes at once.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd059', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Catalysts 101`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What does a catalyst do in a chemical reaction?`, ch:[`Increases the amount of product formed beyond what's possible`,`Speeds up the reaction without being consumed`,`Slows down the reaction`,`Changes the products that are formed`], ans:1, exp:`Catalysts speed up how quickly a reaction happens, but they come out of the reaction unchanged and ready to be used again.` },
    { q:`Which statement about catalysts is TRUE?`, ch:[`Catalysts are permanently used up in the reaction`,`Catalysts are not consumed and can be used again`,`Catalysts always increase the mass of the products`,`Catalysts only work at freezing temperatures`], ans:1, exp:`A key feature of catalysts is that they participate in the reaction but are regenerated at the end, so they aren't used up.` },
    { q:`Enzymes found in your body are examples of...?`, ch:[`Inhibitors`,`Biological catalysts`,`Solvents`,`Acids only`], ans:1, exp:`Enzymes are proteins that act as biological catalysts, speeding up chemical reactions like digestion inside your body.` },
    { q:`A catalyst speeds up a reaction by...?`, ch:[`Increasing the temperature of the surroundings`,`Lowering the activation energy needed for the reaction`,`Adding more reactant molecules`,`Removing product molecules`], ans:1, exp:`Catalysts provide an easier pathway for the reaction, lowering the energy barrier (activation energy) that needs to be overcome.` },
    { q:`Catalytic converters in cars help speed up reactions that convert harmful gases into less harmful ones. This is an example of a catalyst used in...?`, ch:[`Biology only`,`Everyday technology and environmental science`,`Cooking only`,`Nothing important`], ans:1, exp:`Catalytic converters use metal catalysts to help clean up exhaust gases, showing how catalysts matter well beyond the science classroom.` },
    { q:`Which of these substances would NOT typically be considered a catalyst?`, ch:[`An enzyme in saliva that breaks down starch`,`Platinum in a catalytic converter`,`A reactant that gets fully consumed and turned into product`,`An enzyme that helps yeast ferment sugar`], ans:2, exp:`A true catalyst isn't consumed by the reaction — a reactant that gets used up and turned into product is not acting as a catalyst.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd060', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Everyday Chemistry: Kitchen Reactions`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`When baking soda (a base) reacts with vinegar (an acid), the fizzing bubbles you see are mostly...?`, ch:[`Oxygen gas`,`Carbon dioxide gas`,`Hydrogen gas`,`Water vapor only`], ans:1, exp:`The acid-base reaction between vinegar and baking soda produces carbon dioxide gas, which creates the fizzy bubbles.` },
    { q:`What causes bread dough to rise when baking soda or yeast is used?`, ch:[`The dough absorbs water and swells`,`Gas bubbles (like CO2) form and get trapped in the dough`,`The dough melts slightly`,`The oven's heat expands the flour`], ans:1, exp:`Gas produced by yeast or baking soda gets trapped in the stretchy dough, creating pockets that make the bread rise and become fluffy.` },
    { q:`Why does a cut apple turn brown after being left out in the air?`, ch:[`It freezes`,`A chemical reaction with oxygen in the air (oxidation) changes compounds in the apple`,`Bacteria instantly grows on it`,`It absorbs food coloring from the air`], ans:1, exp:`Exposed compounds in the apple react with oxygen in the air, a process called oxidation, which produces the brown color.` },
    { q:`When you cook an egg, the clear egg white turns solid and white. This is an example of...?`, ch:[`A physical change only, with no new substances formed`,`A chemical change, as heat causes proteins to permanently change structure`,`Freezing`,`Dissolving`], ans:1, exp:`Heat permanently changes the structure of the proteins in egg whites, which is why you can't turn a cooked egg back into a raw one — that's a chemical change.` },
    { q:`Adding lemon juice (acidic) to milk can cause it to curdle. What is happening chemically?`, ch:[`The acid causes milk proteins to change structure and clump together`,`The milk freezes instantly`,`The lemon juice evaporates the milk`,`Nothing chemical happens; it's just a color change`], ans:0, exp:`The acid in lemon juice changes the shape of milk proteins, causing them to clump together and form curds.` },
    { q:`Why do onions make your eyes water when you cut them?`, ch:[`They release a cold gas that irritates your skin`,`They release a chemical compound that reacts with moisture in your eyes to form an irritating substance`,`They contain a strong acid that splashes into your eyes`,`They release pure water vapor`], ans:1, exp:`Cutting onions releases a sulfur-containing compound that reacts with the water in your eyes, forming a mild irritant that triggers tears.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd061', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Speed, Velocity & Acceleration`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`A car travels 100 miles in 2 hours. What is its average speed?`, ch:[`40 mph`,`50 mph`,`100 mph`,`200 mph`], ans:1, exp:`Average speed is total distance divided by total time: 100 miles ÷ 2 hours = 50 mph.` },
    { q:`What's the key difference between speed and velocity?`, ch:[`Velocity has direction, speed doesn't`,`Speed has direction, velocity doesn't`,`They are exactly the same thing`,`Speed is only used for cars`], ans:0, exp:`Speed is just how fast something moves, while velocity also tells you which direction it's moving — that makes velocity a vector.` },
    { q:`A runner sprints 400m around a track and ends up back at the exact starting point. What is the runner's displacement?`, ch:[`400m`,`100m`,`0m`,`200m`], ans:2, exp:`Displacement measures the straight-line change in position. Since the runner ends where they started, the displacement is zero, even though they covered real distance.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is a vector quantity (has both magnitude and direction)?`, ch:[`Distance`,`Time`,`Speed`,`Velocity`], ans:3, exp:`Velocity includes direction, which makes it a vector. Distance, time, and speed are all scalars — they only have magnitude.` },
    { q:`A car speeds up from 0 to 60 mph in 6 seconds. On average, about how much speed does it gain each second?`, ch:[`6 mph per second`,`60 mph per second`,`10 mph per second`,`360 mph per second`], ans:2, exp:`Divide the total change in speed by the time it took: 60 mph ÷ 6 s = 10 mph gained each second — that's the average acceleration.` },
    { q:`A ball is tossed straight up and reaches the very top of its path before falling back down. What is its instantaneous velocity at that highest point?`, ch:[`Maximum speed`,`Equal to its launch speed`,`Negative`,`Zero`], ans:3, exp:`At the peak of its flight, the ball briefly stops moving upward before gravity pulls it back down, so its velocity at that instant is zero.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd062', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Newton's First Law`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which statement correctly describes Newton's First Law of Motion?`, ch:[`An object in motion stays in motion unless acted on by a net force`,`Force equals mass times acceleration`,`For every action there's an equal and opposite reaction`,`Objects fall at the same rate regardless of mass`], ans:0, exp:`Newton's First Law says objects keep doing what they're already doing — staying still or moving at constant velocity — unless an unbalanced force changes that.` },
    { q:`Newton's First Law is also commonly known as the law of:`, ch:[`Momentum`,`Friction`,`Inertia`,`Gravity`], ans:2, exp:`Inertia is an object's tendency to resist changes in its motion, which is exactly what the First Law describes.` },
    { q:`When a car stops suddenly, passengers lurch forward. Why does this happen?`, ch:[`The seatbelt causes it`,`The car pushes them forward`,`Gravity pulls them forward`,`Their bodies want to keep moving forward due to inertia`], ans:3, exp:`The passengers' bodies were already moving forward with the car, and inertia makes them want to keep moving that way even after the car stops.` },
    { q:`Which of these objects has the most inertia?`, ch:[`A tennis ball`,`A bowling ball`,`A ping pong ball`,`A feather`], ans:1, exp:`Inertia increases with mass, and a bowling ball has far more mass than the other objects listed, so it resists changes in motion the most.` },
    { q:`For a moving object's velocity to change, what must happen?`, ch:[`Nothing — it changes on its own over time`,`A balanced set of forces must act on it`,`An unbalanced (net) force must act on it`,`Time alone is enough`], ans:2, exp:`Balanced forces cancel out and cause no change in motion. It takes an unbalanced, or net, force to speed up, slow down, or change an object's direction.` },
    { q:`A book sits perfectly still on a table. Why doesn't it move?`, ch:[`It has no inertia`,`There is no gravity acting on it`,`The table pushes harder than gravity pulls`,`The forces acting on it are balanced`], ans:3, exp:`Gravity pulls the book down while the table pushes up with an equal and opposite support force, so the forces balance and the book stays put.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd063', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Newton's Second Law`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`Newton's Second Law of Motion is expressed with which formula?`, ch:[`F = ma`,`E = mc²`,`p = mv`,`W = Fd`], ans:0, exp:`Newton's Second Law states that force equals mass times acceleration (F = ma), linking how hard something is pushed to how quickly it speeds up.` },
    { q:`A 2 kg object experiences a net force of 10 N. What is its acceleration?`, ch:[`2 m/s²`,`5 m/s²`,`10 m/s²`,`20 m/s²`], ans:1, exp:`Rearranging F = ma gives a = F ÷ m, so 10 N ÷ 2 kg = 5 m/s².` },
    { q:`If you double an object's mass while the net force applied stays the same, what happens to its acceleration?`, ch:[`It doubles`,`It stays the same`,`It's cut in half`,`It quadruples`], ans:2, exp:`Since a = F ÷ m, doubling the mass while force stays constant cuts the acceleration in half — more mass means more resistance to speeding up.` },
    { q:`If you double the net force on an object while its mass stays the same, what happens to its acceleration?`, ch:[`It stays the same`,`It doubles`,`It's cut in half`,`It becomes zero`], ans:1, exp:`Since a = F ÷ m, doubling the force while mass is unchanged doubles the acceleration — force and acceleration are directly proportional.` },
    { q:`What is the SI (metric) unit of force, named after the scientist behind these laws?`, ch:[`Joule`,`Watt`,`Newton`,`Pascal`], ans:2, exp:`Force is measured in newtons (N). A joule measures energy, a watt measures power, and a pascal measures pressure.` },
    { q:`A 5 kg cart needs to accelerate at 4 m/s². How much net force is required?`, ch:[`9 N`,`1.25 N`,`0.8 N`,`20 N`], ans:3, exp:`Using F = ma: 5 kg × 4 m/s² = 20 N of net force.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd064', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Newton's Third Law`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Newton's Third Law of Motion states that:`, ch:[`For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction`,`Objects at rest stay at rest`,`Force equals mass times acceleration`,`Energy cannot be created or destroyed`], ans:0, exp:`Newton's Third Law says forces always come in pairs — whenever one object pushes or pulls on another, that second object pushes or pulls back just as hard, in the opposite direction.` },
    { q:`You jump off a small boat onto a dock, and the boat drifts backward. Why?`, ch:[`The water pushes it`,`The dock pushes it`,`You pushed on the boat and it pushed back on you with equal force`,`The boat has no inertia`], ans:2, exp:`As you push off the boat to move forward, the boat pushes back on you with an equal and opposite force, sending it drifting the other way.` },
    { q:`How does a rocket manage to launch itself into space?`, ch:[`By pulling itself up with wings`,`By expelling gas downward, which pushes the rocket upward`,`By pushing against the air only`,`By using gravity to lift off`], ans:1, exp:`The rocket engine blasts hot gas downward, and by Newton's Third Law, the gas pushes back on the rocket with equal force, propelling it upward — this even works in the vacuum of space.` },
    { q:`Action-reaction force pairs described by Newton's Third Law always:`, ch:[`Act on the same object`,`Only occur in outer space`,`Act on two different objects`,`Cancel each other out completely`], ans:2, exp:`One force in the pair acts on one object, and the other force acts on a different object, so the two forces never cancel out on the same object.` },
    { q:`You push on a solid wall with 50 N of force. How much force does the wall push back on you with?`, ch:[`50 N`,`0 N`,`100 N`,`25 N`], ans:0, exp:`By Newton's Third Law, the wall pushes back on you with a force equal in size and opposite in direction — 50 N.` },
    { q:`Which situation best illustrates Newton's Third Law?`, ch:[`A ball rolling to a stop due to friction`,`An apple falling from a tree`,`A car accelerating on a highway`,`A swimmer pushing water backward to move forward`], ans:3, exp:`The swimmer pushes water backward, and the water pushes the swimmer forward with an equal and opposite force — a clear action-reaction pair.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd065', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Gravity & Weight vs Mass`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the key difference between mass and weight?`, ch:[`Mass is measured in newtons and weight in kilograms`,`Mass is the amount of matter in an object; weight is the force of gravity acting on that mass`,`They are exactly the same thing`,`Weight never changes but mass does`], ans:1, exp:`Mass stays the same no matter where you are, but weight is a force that depends on the local strength of gravity pulling on that mass.` },
    { q:`An astronaut's mass on the Moon compared to on Earth is:`, ch:[`Greater`,`Less`,`The same`,`Zero`], ans:2, exp:`Mass depends only on the amount of matter in the astronaut's body, which doesn't change just by traveling to the Moon.` },
    { q:`An astronaut's weight on the Moon compared to on Earth is:`, ch:[`Much less`,`The same`,`Greater`,`Impossible to measure`], ans:0, exp:`The Moon's gravity is much weaker than Earth's, so the same mass experiences a much smaller gravitational force, meaning less weight.` },
    { q:`Which unit is commonly used to measure weight, since weight is actually a force?`, ch:[`Kilogram`,`Newton`,`Liter`,`Meter`], ans:1, exp:`Because weight is a force, it's properly measured in newtons, while kilograms measure mass.` },
    { q:`Why do the Moon and Earth pull on objects with different gravitational strength?`, ch:[`The Moon has much less mass than Earth`,`The Moon is farther from the Sun`,`The Moon has no atmosphere`,`The Moon spins faster than Earth`], ans:0, exp:`Gravitational pull depends on mass, and since the Moon has far less mass than Earth, its gravitational pull on objects is much weaker.` },
    { q:`If you weigh 120 lbs on Earth, roughly how much would you weigh on the Moon, where gravity is about 1/6th as strong?`, ch:[`120 lbs`,`60 lbs`,`20 lbs`,`720 lbs`], ans:2, exp:`Since Moon gravity is about 1/6th of Earth's, your weight there would be roughly 120 ÷ 6 = 20 lbs, even though your mass hasn't changed.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd066', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Work & Energy Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`In physics, "work" is done on an object when:`, ch:[`You think hard about a problem`,`A force causes an object to move in the direction of the force`,`An object simply exists`,`Time passes`], ans:1, exp:`Physics work has a strict definition: a force must actually move an object some distance in the direction of that force.` },
    { q:`Which formula correctly calculates work?`, ch:[`W = F × d`,`W = m × a`,`W = m × v`,`W = F ÷ d`], ans:0, exp:`Work equals the force applied multiplied by the distance the object moves in the direction of that force.` },
    { q:`You push a box with 20 N of force and it slides 5 meters in the direction of your push. How much work did you do?`, ch:[`4 J`,`15 J`,`25 J`,`100 J`], ans:3, exp:`Using W = F × d: 20 N × 5 m = 100 joules of work.` },
    { q:`What is the SI unit used to measure both work and energy?`, ch:[`Newton`,`Watt`,`Joule`,`Pascal`], ans:2, exp:`Work and energy are both measured in joules (J), since doing work on an object transfers energy to it.` },
    { q:`You push as hard as you can against a solid brick wall, but it doesn't budge at all. How much work have you done on the wall?`, ch:[`Zero`,`A lot`,`A little`,`It depends on how hard you push`], ans:0, exp:`Since work requires movement in the direction of the force, and the wall doesn't move, no work is done on it — no matter how hard you push.` },
    { q:`Power is best described as:`, ch:[`The total energy in a system`,`The rate at which work is done`,`The amount of force applied`,`The mass of an object`], ans:1, exp:`Power measures how quickly work gets done or energy gets transferred — doing the same work faster means using more power.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd067', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Kinetic vs Potential Energy`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Kinetic energy is the energy an object has due to its:`, ch:[`Position or stored condition`,`Heat`,`Motion`,`Sound`], ans:2, exp:`Kinetic energy is specifically the energy of motion — the faster and more massive an object is, the more kinetic energy it has.` },
    { q:`Potential energy is best described as:`, ch:[`Energy that has been destroyed`,`Stored energy due to an object's position or condition`,`Energy of motion`,`Energy only found in batteries`], ans:1, exp:`Potential energy is stored energy that depends on position or configuration, like a ball held up high or a stretched rubber band.` },
    { q:`A ball is held high above the ground and is not moving. What kind of energy does it mostly have?`, ch:[`Kinetic energy`,`Sound energy`,`No energy`,`Potential energy`], ans:3, exp:`Since the ball is at rest but positioned high above the ground, it has gravitational potential energy due to its height.` },
    { q:`As the held ball is released and falls, its potential energy mostly converts into:`, ch:[`Heat only`,`Kinetic energy`,`Nuclear energy`,`Nothing — it just disappears`], ans:1, exp:`As the ball falls and speeds up, its stored potential energy transforms into kinetic energy, the energy of motion.` },
    { q:`Which two factors most directly affect an object's gravitational potential energy?`, ch:[`Only its color`,`Its height and mass`,`Its speed only`,`Its temperature`], ans:1, exp:`Gravitational potential energy depends on how much mass an object has and how high above the ground it's positioned.` },
    { q:`A roller coaster car sits at the very top of the first big hill, not yet moving. At that point it has the ___ potential energy and the ___ kinetic energy of the whole ride.`, ch:[`Most; least`,`Least; most`,`Equal; equal`,`Zero; maximum`], ans:0, exp:`At the top of the hill, the car is at its highest point (maximum potential energy) and barely moving (minimum kinetic energy) for that ride.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd068', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Simple Machines 101`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which of these is NOT one of the six classic simple machines?`, ch:[`Lever`,`Pulley`,`Engine`,`Wedge`], ans:2, exp:`An engine is a complex machine with many moving parts. The six simple machines are the lever, pulley, wheel and axle, inclined plane, wedge, and screw.` },
    { q:`A simple machine makes work easier mainly by:`, ch:[`Eliminating the need for force entirely`,`Changing the amount, direction, or distance of the force needed`,`Creating energy from nothing`,`Increasing friction`], ans:1, exp:`Simple machines don't create energy — they let you trade off force, distance, or direction to make a task easier to perform.` },
    { q:`A seesaw is a classic example of which simple machine?`, ch:[`Pulley`,`Screw`,`Lever`,`Wedge`], ans:2, exp:`A seesaw pivots around a central point called a fulcrum, which is the defining feature of a lever.` },
    { q:`Which simple machine is essentially a spiral-shaped inclined plane wrapped around a cylinder?`, ch:[`Wheel and axle`,`Screw`,`Wedge`,`Lever`], ans:1, exp:`A screw is an inclined plane wound around a central shaft, which is why turning it a little moves it forward a lot with less force.` },
    { q:`A ramp used to roll a heavy barrel up into a truck is an example of a(n):`, ch:[`Pulley`,`Wheel and axle`,`Inclined plane`,`Screw`], ans:2, exp:`A ramp is an inclined plane — it lets you move a load to a higher position using less force over a longer distance.` },
    { q:`When you use a simple machine to reduce the force needed for a task, what do you generally give up in exchange?`, ch:[`Less force for more distance moved (or vice versa)`,`Nothing at all`,`More energy for less work`,`Speed for color`], ans:0, exp:`Simple machines don't create free energy — using less force typically means applying it over a longer distance, and vice versa.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd069', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Electric Circuits: The Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`In a simple electric circuit, current will only flow if:`, ch:[`The circuit is open`,`The circuit is closed, forming a complete loop`,`The battery is disconnected`,`There is no resistance anywhere`], ans:1, exp:`Electric current needs an unbroken, closed path to flow through — if the loop is open anywhere, the current stops.` },
    { q:`What is the SI unit used to measure electric current?`, ch:[`Volt`,`Ohm`,`Watt`,`Ampere`], ans:3, exp:`Electric current is measured in amperes (amps). Volts measure voltage, ohms measure resistance, and watts measure power.` },
    { q:`Which of these best describes voltage in a circuit?`, ch:[`The rate of flow of charge`,`The total energy used by the circuit`,`The opposition to current flow`,`The electrical "push," or potential difference, that drives current through a circuit`], ans:3, exp:`Voltage is the "push" supplied by a source like a battery — it's the potential difference that drives charges through the circuit.` },
    { q:`Ohm's Law, relating voltage (V), current (I), and resistance (R), is written as:`, ch:[`V = IR`,`F = ma`,`P = IV`,`E = mc²`], ans:0, exp:`Ohm's Law states that voltage equals current multiplied by resistance (V = IR), a foundational relationship in circuits.` },
    { q:`In a series circuit with several light bulbs, what happens if one bulb burns out?`, ch:[`All the other bulbs go out too, because the circuit is broken`,`Only that bulb goes out; the others stay lit`,`Nothing changes`,`The circuit becomes a parallel circuit`], ans:0, exp:`A series circuit has only one path for current, so if that path is broken by a burnt-out bulb, current stops flowing to every bulb.` },
    { q:`In a parallel circuit, what happens if the bulb in one branch burns out?`, ch:[`The whole circuit stops working`,`The current reverses direction`,`The other branches keep working because they have separate paths`,`The voltage drops to zero everywhere`], ans:2, exp:`Parallel circuits give current multiple independent paths, so if one branch breaks, current can still flow through the other branches.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd070', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Waves 101: Amplitude, Wavelength & Frequency`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`Wavelength is best defined as:`, ch:[`The height of a wave`,`The distance between two consecutive identical points on a wave, like crest to crest`,`The number of waves passing a point per second`,`The speed of the wave`], ans:1, exp:`Wavelength measures the physical length of one complete wave cycle, such as the distance from one crest to the next.` },
    { q:`Frequency is measured in units called:`, ch:[`Meters`,`Seconds`,`Newtons`,`Hertz`], ans:3, exp:`Frequency counts how many wave cycles pass a point each second, and this rate is measured in hertz (Hz).` },
    { q:`Which wave property is most directly related to how much energy the wave carries?`, ch:[`Amplitude`,`Wavelength`,`Frequency`,`Speed`], ans:0, exp:`Amplitude describes how big or intense a wave's disturbance is, and larger amplitude generally means the wave carries more energy.` },
    { q:`If a wave's frequency increases while its speed stays constant, what must happen to its wavelength?`, ch:[`Increase`,`Stay the same`,`Decrease`,`Become zero`], ans:2, exp:`Since wave speed equals frequency times wavelength (v = fλ), if speed stays fixed and frequency goes up, wavelength must go down.` },
    { q:`The wave equation relating speed (v), frequency (f), and wavelength (λ) is:`, ch:[`v = f + λ`,`v = f × λ`,`v = f − λ`,`v = f ÷ λ`], ans:1, exp:`Wave speed equals frequency multiplied by wavelength — this simple relationship connects all three key wave properties.` },
    { q:`A wave has a frequency of 5 Hz. How many complete cycles does it complete in 2 seconds?`, ch:[`1`,`5`,`2`,`10`], ans:3, exp:`Frequency tells you cycles per second, so 5 Hz × 2 seconds = 10 complete cycles.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd071', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Sound Waves Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Sound is a type of wave that requires:`, ch:[`Nothing — it can travel through empty space`,`Light to carry it`,`A medium, like air, water, or a solid, to travel through`,`Only electricity`], ans:2, exp:`Sound waves are vibrations that need particles to bump into each other, so they can't travel through the vacuum of empty space.` },
    { q:`Sound generally travels fastest through which of these?`, ch:[`Solids`,`Gases like air`,`A vacuum`,`Liquids only`], ans:0, exp:`In solids, particles are packed tightly together, so vibrations pass from particle to particle quickly, making sound travel fastest there.` },
    { q:`The pitch of a sound is most closely related to its:`, ch:[`Amplitude`,`Speed`,`Volume`,`Frequency`], ans:3, exp:`Higher frequency sound waves are perceived as higher pitch, while lower frequency waves sound lower in pitch.` },
    { q:`The loudness of a sound is most closely related to its:`, ch:[`Frequency`,`Amplitude`,`Wavelength`,`Speed`], ans:1, exp:`Amplitude reflects how big the sound wave's vibrations are, and bigger vibrations are perceived as louder sound.` },
    { q:`Sound cannot travel through which of the following?`, ch:[`Air`,`Water`,`A vacuum, or empty space`,`Steel`], ans:2, exp:`Since sound needs particles of matter to carry its vibrations, it cannot travel through a vacuum, which has no particles at all.` },
    { q:`An echo occurs because sound waves:`, ch:[`Reflect off a surface and return to the listener`,`Speed up over distance`,`Disappear and then regenerate`,`Travel through a vacuum`], ans:0, exp:`An echo happens when sound waves bounce off a hard surface, like a wall or canyon, and travel back to reach your ears again.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd072', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Light & the Electromagnetic Spectrum Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which of these best describes visible light's place in the electromagnetic spectrum?`, ch:[`It's the fastest of all electromagnetic waves`,`It's just one small band within a much larger spectrum of electromagnetic waves`,`It's the only kind of light humans use`,`It has the longest wavelength of all electromagnetic waves`], ans:1, exp:`Visible light is only a small slice of the full electromagnetic spectrum, which also includes radio waves, infrared, ultraviolet, X-rays, and gamma rays.` },
    { q:`Which list correctly orders these electromagnetic waves from longest wavelength to shortest?`, ch:[`X-rays, gamma rays, visible light, radio waves`,`Visible light, radio waves, gamma rays, X-rays`,`Gamma rays, X-rays, visible light, radio waves`,`Radio waves, visible light, X-rays, gamma rays`], ans:3, exp:`Radio waves have the longest wavelengths, followed by visible light, then X-rays, with gamma rays having the shortest wavelengths of all.` },
    { q:`All electromagnetic waves travel through a vacuum at the same:`, ch:[`Speed — the speed of light`,`Wavelength`,`Frequency`,`Amplitude`], ans:0, exp:`Every type of electromagnetic wave, from radio to gamma rays, travels through empty space at the same speed: the speed of light.` },
    { q:`Comparing electromagnetic waves, which type generally carries more energy — one with a short wavelength or one with a long wavelength?`, ch:[`Long wavelength waves always have more energy`,`They always have equal energy`,`Short wavelength waves have more energy`,`Energy doesn't relate to wavelength at all`], ans:2, exp:`Shorter wavelength electromagnetic waves have higher frequency, and higher frequency waves carry more energy, which is why X-rays and gamma rays are so energetic.` },
    { q:`Which of these is NOT part of the electromagnetic spectrum?`, ch:[`Microwaves`,`Sound waves`,`Infrared`,`Ultraviolet`], ans:1, exp:`Sound waves are mechanical vibrations that need a medium to travel through, unlike electromagnetic waves, which can travel through empty space.` },
    { q:`White light splits into a rainbow of colors when it passes through a prism. Why does this happen?`, ch:[`The prism adds color to the light`,`All the colors travel at different constant speeds forever`,`Light loses energy inside the prism`,`Different wavelengths, or colors, bend by different amounts as they refract inside the prism`], ans:3, exp:`White light is actually a mix of all visible colors, and since each color's wavelength bends by a slightly different amount inside the prism, they spread out into a visible rainbow.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd073', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`What Is Psychology?`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which of these best defines psychology?`, ch:[`The study of the brain's anatomy only`, `The scientific study of behavior and mental processes`, `The treatment of mental illness using medication`, `The philosophical study of the meaning of life`], ans:1, exp:`Psychology is the scientific study of behavior (what we do) and mental processes (what we think and feel).` },
    { q:`Who is credited with founding the first formal psychology laboratory in 1879?`, ch:[`Sigmund Freud`, `B.F. Skinner`, `Wilhelm Wundt`, `Jean Piaget`], ans:2, exp:`Wilhelm Wundt opened the first psychology lab in Leipzig, Germany, in 1879, marking psychology's start as a formal science.` },
    { q:`What is a key difference between a psychologist and a psychiatrist?`, ch:[`Psychologists study animals, psychiatrists study humans`, `A psychiatrist is a medical doctor who can prescribe medication; most psychologists cannot`, `Psychiatrists only work in schools`, `There is no real difference`], ans:1, exp:`Psychiatrists complete medical school and can prescribe medication, while most psychologists have a doctorate in psychology and focus on therapy, testing, or research.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is a recognized subfield of psychology?`, ch:[`Developmental psychology`, `Geology`, `Astrophysics`, `Metallurgy`], ans:0, exp:`Developmental psychology studies how people change and grow across the lifespan; it's one of many subfields, alongside clinical, cognitive, and social psychology.` },
    { q:`Psychologists often describe their goals as being able to describe, explain, predict, and what else about behavior?`, ch:[`Ignore it`, `Change or influence it`, `Memorize it`, `Deny it`], ans:1, exp:`A major goal of psychology is not just understanding behavior but also finding ways to help change it for the better, such as in therapy.` },
    { q:`Which early perspective in psychology emphasized the influence of the unconscious mind on behavior?`, ch:[`Behaviorism`, `Cognitive perspective`, `Psychodynamic perspective`, `Humanistic perspective`], ans:2, exp:`The psychodynamic perspective, pioneered by Sigmund Freud, focuses on how unconscious thoughts and childhood experiences shape behavior.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd074', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`The Brain: Basic Structure & Function`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the basic building block (cell) of the nervous system?`, ch:[`Neuron`, `Red blood cell`, `Osteocyte`, `Muscle fiber`], ans:0, exp:`Neurons are specialized cells that send and receive electrical and chemical signals, forming the basis of all nervous system activity.` },
    { q:`Which lobe of the brain is most associated with decision-making, planning, and personality?`, ch:[`Occipital lobe`, `Temporal lobe`, `Frontal lobe`, `Parietal lobe`], ans:2, exp:`The frontal lobe, located at the front of the brain, handles higher-level functions like planning, judgment, and personality.` },
    { q:`Which lobe primarily processes visual information?`, ch:[`Parietal lobe`, `Frontal lobe`, `Temporal lobe`, `Occipital lobe`], ans:3, exp:`The occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, is dedicated mainly to processing what we see.` },
    { q:`Which brain structure is mainly responsible for balance and coordinating smooth, precise movement?`, ch:[`Cerebellum`, `Hippocampus`, `Amygdala`, `Corpus callosum`], ans:0, exp:`The cerebellum, tucked under the back of the brain, fine-tunes movement and helps keep us balanced and coordinated.` },
    { q:`The brainstem is critical for controlling which of the following?`, ch:[`Long-term memory storage`, `Abstract reasoning`, `Basic life functions like breathing and heart rate`, `Language comprehension`], ans:2, exp:`The brainstem connects the brain to the spinal cord and regulates automatic, life-sustaining functions such as breathing and heartbeat.` },
    { q:`What is the tiny gap between two neurons, across which chemical messages travel, called?`, ch:[`Axon`, `Synapse`, `Dendrite`, `Myelin sheath`], ans:1, exp:`The synapse is the small gap between neurons where chemical messengers (neurotransmitters) cross to pass along a signal.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd075', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`How Memory Works (Intro)`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`Psychologists describe memory as involving three basic processes. Which set is correct?`, ch:[`Encoding, storage, retrieval`, `Reading, writing, erasing`, `Sensing, guessing, forgetting`, `Input, output, delete`], ans:0, exp:`Memory works by encoding (taking in information), storing it, and retrieving it later when needed.` },
    { q:`Without rehearsal, information in short-term (working) memory typically lasts about how long?`, ch:[`Several hours`, `About 15 to 30 seconds`, `One full day`, `Permanently`], ans:1, exp:`Short-term memory has a limited duration—information fades in roughly 15 to 30 seconds unless it's actively rehearsed.` },
    { q:`Which brain structure plays a key role in forming new long-term memories?`, ch:[`Cerebellum`, `Occipital lobe`, `Hippocampus`, `Spinal cord`], ans:2, exp:`The hippocampus helps convert short-term experiences into lasting long-term memories.` },
    { q:`Which is the correct order from briefest to longest-lasting memory system?`, ch:[`Long-term, short-term, sensory`, `Short-term, sensory, long-term`, `Sensory, short-term, long-term`, `Long-term, sensory, short-term`], ans:2, exp:`Information first enters sensory memory (a fraction of a second), then short-term memory (seconds), and finally can move into long-term memory (potentially a lifetime).` },
    { q:`Repeating a phone number over and over to keep it in mind is an example of what?`, ch:[`Extinction`, `Rehearsal`, `Shaping`, `Generalization`], ans:1, exp:`Rehearsal is repeating information to keep it active in short-term memory and help transfer it into long-term storage.` },
    { q:`Knowing how to ride a bike without consciously thinking about the steps is an example of which type of memory?`, ch:[`Episodic memory`, `Sensory memory`, `Procedural memory`, `Working memory`], ans:2, exp:`Procedural memory stores skills and how-to knowledge, like riding a bike, that we can perform automatically.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd076', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Classical Conditioning Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which scientist is famous for discovering classical conditioning through his experiments with dogs?`, ch:[`B.F. Skinner`, `Ivan Pavlov`, `Wilhelm Wundt`, `Jean Piaget`], ans:1, exp:`Ivan Pavlov noticed that dogs could learn to associate a neutral sound with food, laying the foundation for classical conditioning.` },
    { q:`In classical conditioning, what is an unconditioned stimulus (UCS)?`, ch:[`A stimulus that only triggers a response after learning`, `A stimulus that naturally and automatically triggers a response without prior learning`, `A response that has been trained away`, `A random, meaningless sound`], ans:1, exp:`An unconditioned stimulus, like food, naturally causes a response (salivation) without any learning required.` },
    { q:`In Pavlov's experiment, after being repeatedly paired with food, the bell became what?`, ch:[`An unconditioned stimulus`, `An unconditioned response`, `A conditioned stimulus`, `A conditioned response`], ans:2, exp:`Through repeated pairing with food, the previously neutral bell became a conditioned stimulus that alone could trigger salivation.` },
    { q:`What is a conditioned response (CR)?`, ch:[`A natural reflex present at birth`, `A learned response to a previously neutral stimulus`, `The stimulus that starts the learning process`, `A punishment used to stop behavior`], ans:1, exp:`A conditioned response is the learned reaction (like salivating to a bell) that occurs after conditioning has taken place.` },
    { q:`If the bell is rung many times without ever being paired with food again, and the dog gradually stops salivating to the bell, this process is called what?`, ch:[`Acquisition`, `Generalization`, `Extinction`, `Shaping`], ans:2, exp:`Extinction occurs when the conditioned stimulus is repeatedly presented without the unconditioned stimulus, causing the learned response to fade.` },
    { q:`A student feels anxious every time they hear their school's morning bell because it always precedes a pop quiz. In this scenario, what is the school bell?`, ch:[`The unconditioned stimulus`, `The unconditioned response`, `The conditioned stimulus`, `Negative reinforcement`], ans:2, exp:`The bell started as neutral but, after being paired with quizzes, became a conditioned stimulus that now triggers anxiety on its own.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd077', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Operant Conditioning Basics`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which psychologist is most closely associated with operant conditioning?`, ch:[`Ivan Pavlov`, `Sigmund Freud`, `B.F. Skinner`, `Erik Erikson`], ans:2, exp:`B.F. Skinner studied how consequences (rewards and punishments) shape voluntary behavior, developing the theory of operant conditioning.` },
    { q:`Giving a dog a treat every time it sits on command, which increases the sitting behavior, is an example of what?`, ch:[`Positive reinforcement`, `Negative reinforcement`, `Positive punishment`, `Negative punishment`], ans:0, exp:`Positive reinforcement means adding a pleasant stimulus (the treat) to increase the likelihood of a behavior happening again.` },
    { q:`A driver puts on their seatbelt to stop the annoying beeping sound in the car. This is an example of what?`, ch:[`Positive punishment`, `Negative reinforcement`, `Positive reinforcement`, `Extinction`], ans:1, exp:`Negative reinforcement increases a behavior by removing something unpleasant, like buckling up to stop the beeping.` },
    { q:`A teenager loses phone privileges for staying out past curfew, and as a result they start coming home on time. This is an example of what?`, ch:[`Negative reinforcement`, `Positive reinforcement`, `Negative punishment`, `Shaping`], ans:2, exp:`Negative punishment decreases a behavior by taking away something desirable, like phone privileges.` },
    { q:`Spraying a cat with water when it scratches the furniture, which decreases the scratching, is an example of what?`, ch:[`Positive punishment`, `Negative punishment`, `Positive reinforcement`, `Negative reinforcement`], ans:0, exp:`Positive punishment decreases a behavior by adding an unpleasant consequence, such as a spray of water.` },
    { q:`Gradually rewarding a dolphin for movements that get closer and closer to a full jump through a hoop is called what?`, ch:[`Extinction`, `Generalization`, `Shaping`, `Discrimination`], ans:2, exp:`Shaping involves reinforcing small steps, or successive approximations, that gradually lead to the final desired behavior.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd078', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Motivation: What Drives Us`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`In Maslow's hierarchy of needs, which needs must generally be met first, at the base of the pyramid?`, ch:[`Self-actualization needs`, `Esteem needs`, `Physiological needs like food and water`, `Belongingness needs`], ans:2, exp:`Maslow placed basic survival needs, such as food, water, and sleep, at the bottom of the pyramid as the foundation for all other needs.` },
    { q:`What is intrinsic motivation?`, ch:[`Doing something to earn money or a prize`, `Doing something because it's personally satisfying or enjoyable`, `Doing something only to avoid punishment`, `Doing something because a friend forces you to`], ans:1, exp:`Intrinsic motivation comes from within—we engage in an activity because we find it interesting or rewarding on its own.` },
    { q:`What is extrinsic motivation?`, ch:[`Motivation driven by internal enjoyment`, `Motivation driven by curiosity alone`, `Motivation driven by outside rewards or consequences, like grades or money`, `Motivation that never changes over time`], ans:2, exp:`Extrinsic motivation comes from external factors, such as rewards, grades, or avoiding punishment, rather than internal enjoyment.` },
    { q:`Drive-reduction theory suggests that behavior is motivated by the desire to do what?`, ch:[`Increase internal tension`, `Reduce internal tension and restore balance (homeostasis)`, `Copy other people's behavior`, `Avoid all social interaction`], ans:1, exp:`Drive-reduction theory proposes that unmet biological needs create tension, and we're motivated to act in ways that reduce that tension and return the body to balance.` },
    { q:`At the top of Maslow's hierarchy of needs is which of the following?`, ch:[`Safety needs`, `Self-actualization`, `Physiological needs`, `Esteem needs`], ans:1, exp:`Self-actualization, or reaching one's full potential, sits at the very top of Maslow's pyramid.` },
    { q:`A student who studies biology purely because they find the subject fascinating, not because of a grade, is displaying what kind of motivation?`, ch:[`Extrinsic motivation`, `Negative motivation`, `Intrinsic motivation`, `Unconscious motivation`], ans:2, exp:`Because the enjoyment comes from within the student rather than from an external reward, this is intrinsic motivation.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd079', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Emotions 101`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Which of these best describes an emotion?`, ch:[`A purely physical reflex with no thoughts involved`, `A complex reaction involving physical arousal, feelings, thoughts, and outward behavior`, `A permanent personality trait`, `A random, meaningless brain event`], ans:1, exp:`Emotions combine physiological arousal, subjective feelings, cognitive interpretation, and behavioral expression all at once.` },
    { q:`Which researcher is well known for identifying a set of universally recognized basic emotions and facial expressions?`, ch:[`B.F. Skinner`, `Paul Ekman`, `Ivan Pavlov`, `Wilhelm Wundt`], ans:1, exp:`Paul Ekman's research found that certain basic emotions, like happiness and fear, are expressed through similar facial expressions across many different cultures.` },
    { q:`Which brain structure is especially important for processing fear and other emotional responses?`, ch:[`Cerebellum`, `Occipital lobe`, `Amygdala`, `Spinal cord`], ans:2, exp:`The amygdala is a small, almond-shaped structure deep in the brain that plays a central role in processing fear and other strong emotions.` },
    { q:`Research suggests that which facial expression is recognized as signaling happiness across nearly all cultures studied?`, ch:[`A furrowed brow`, `A smile`, `A clenched jaw`, `Squinted eyes only`], ans:1, exp:`Smiling is one of the facial expressions found to be widely recognized as a sign of happiness across many different cultures.` },
    { q:`Your heart racing and palms sweating right before a big test is an example of which component of emotion?`, ch:[`The cognitive component`, `The behavioral component`, `The physiological component`, `The linguistic component`], ans:2, exp:`A racing heart and sweaty palms are physical, bodily reactions—this is the physiological component of an emotional response.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is one of Paul Ekman's originally proposed basic, universal emotions?`, ch:[`Jealousy`, `Nostalgia`, `Fear`, `Pride`], ans:2, exp:`Fear is one of Ekman's classic basic emotions, along with happiness, sadness, anger, surprise, and disgust.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd080', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Sleep & Why We Need It`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the circadian rhythm?`, ch:[`A type of sleep disorder`, `The body's internal clock that regulates the sleep-wake cycle over roughly 24 hours`, `A stage of deep sleep`, `A hormone released only during exercise`], ans:1, exp:`The circadian rhythm is an internal biological clock that cycles roughly every 24 hours, helping regulate when we feel sleepy or alert.` },
    { q:`During which stage of sleep does most vivid dreaming occur, along with rapid eye movement?`, ch:[`NREM stage 1`, `NREM stage 3 (deep sleep)`, `REM sleep`, `The moment you first fall asleep`], ans:2, exp:`REM (rapid eye movement) sleep is the stage most associated with vivid dreaming, along with quick eye movements beneath closed eyelids.` },
    { q:`How many hours of sleep do most sleep experts recommend for teenagers each night?`, ch:[`3 to 4 hours`, `5 to 6 hours`, `8 to 10 hours`, `12 to 14 hours`], ans:2, exp:`Most sleep experts recommend that teenagers get about 8 to 10 hours of sleep per night to support growth, learning, and mood.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is a common effect of not getting enough sleep?`, ch:[`Improved memory and focus`, `Impaired concentration, memory, and mood`, `Faster reaction times`, `Stronger immune system`], ans:1, exp:`Sleep deprivation typically hurts concentration, memory, and emotional regulation rather than helping them.` },
    { q:`Deep sleep (NREM stage 3) is especially important for which of the following?`, ch:[`Vivid dreaming`, `Physical restoration and growth`, `Processing new vocabulary words`, `Digesting food`], ans:1, exp:`Deep NREM sleep is when the body does much of its physical repair work, including tissue growth and muscle recovery.` },
    { q:`Getting good sleep is especially important for which cognitive process?`, ch:[`Forgetting information faster`, `Consolidating and strengthening memories`, `Preventing all learning`, `Increasing short-term memory capacity only`], ans:1, exp:`Sleep helps the brain consolidate memories, transferring and strengthening information learned during the day.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd081', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Stress & the Body`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`The "fight-or-flight" response is triggered by which part of the nervous system in reaction to a perceived threat?`, ch:[`The parasympathetic nervous system`, `The sympathetic nervous system`, `The digestive system`, `The occipital lobe`], ans:1, exp:`The sympathetic nervous system activates the fight-or-flight response, preparing the body to confront or flee from a threat.` },
    { q:`Which of these hormones is commonly released in the body during a stress response?`, ch:[`Insulin`, `Melatonin`, `Cortisol`, `Estrogen`], ans:2, exp:`Cortisol, often called a stress hormone, is released by the body to help mobilize energy during stressful situations.` },
    { q:`Hans Selye's General Adaptation Syndrome describes the body's stress response in which three stages?`, ch:[`Denial, bargaining, acceptance`, `Alarm, resistance, exhaustion`, `Input, processing, output`, `Fight, flight, freeze`], ans:1, exp:`Selye's model describes stress unfolding in three stages: alarm (initial reaction), resistance (coping), and exhaustion (resources depleted).` },
    { q:`Which of these is a common effect of prolonged, chronic stress on the body?`, ch:[`A stronger immune system`, `Improved digestion`, `A weakened immune system`, `No effect on physical health`], ans:2, exp:`Long-term chronic stress can suppress the immune system, making the body more vulnerable to illness.` },
    { q:`In the General Adaptation Syndrome, during which stage has the body's ability to cope become depleted?`, ch:[`Alarm stage`, `Resistance stage`, `Exhaustion stage`, `Recovery stage`], ans:2, exp:`The exhaustion stage occurs when the body has used up its resources trying to cope with prolonged stress, leaving it more vulnerable.` },
    { q:`What term describes positive stress that can actually motivate and energize a person, such as the excitement before a big game?`, ch:[`Distress`, `Eustress`, `Homeostasis`, `Extinction`], ans:1, exp:`Eustress is a type of stress that feels exciting and motivating rather than overwhelming, unlike negative distress.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd082', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Nature vs Nurture`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`In the nature vs. nurture debate, what does "nature" refer to?`, ch:[`Environmental experiences and upbringing`, `Genetic and biological inheritance`, `The school a person attends`, `The friends a person chooses`], ans:1, exp:`"Nature" refers to the traits and tendencies we inherit genetically from our biological parents.` },
    { q:`In the nature vs. nurture debate, what does "nurture" refer to?`, ch:[`Inherited genes`, `Biological instincts present at birth`, `Environmental influences and life experiences`, `Physical appearance only`], ans:2, exp:`"Nurture" refers to the environmental factors—like upbringing, culture, and experiences—that shape who we become.` },
    { q:`Why do psychologists often study twins, especially identical twins raised apart, when researching nature vs. nurture?`, ch:[`Twins are easier to find than other participants`, `To help separate the relative contributions of genetics and environment`, `Because twins always have identical personalities`, `Twin studies have nothing to do with nature vs. nurture`], ans:1, exp:`Comparing identical twins raised in different environments helps researchers estimate how much of a trait comes from genes versus surroundings.` },
    { q:`What is the modern, widely accepted view among psychologists regarding nature and nurture?`, ch:[`Only nature matters; nurture has no real effect`, `Only nurture matters; genes are irrelevant`, `Nature and nurture typically interact together to shape development`, `Nature and nurture cancel each other out`], ans:2, exp:`Most psychologists today agree that traits and behaviors usually result from a complex interaction between genes and environment, not one factor alone.` },
    { q:`Approximately what percentage of their DNA do identical twins share?`, ch:[`50%`, `75%`, `90%`, `100%`], ans:3, exp:`Identical twins develop from a single fertilized egg that splits, so they share essentially 100% of their DNA.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is generally considered a better example of "nurture" than "nature"?`, ch:[`Eye color`, `Natural height potential`, `The specific language a person speaks`, `Blood type`], ans:2, exp:`The particular language someone speaks is learned from their environment and upbringing, making it a clear example of nurture rather than nature.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd083', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Intro to Human Development`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What does developmental psychology study?`, ch:[`Only how children learn to read`, `How people change physically, cognitively, and socially across the entire lifespan`, `Only mental illness in adults`, `Only the structure of the brain`], ans:1, exp:`Developmental psychology examines how people grow and change—physically, mentally, and socially—from infancy through old age.` },
    { q:`Which psychologist is best known for a theory describing stages of children's cognitive development?`, ch:[`Erik Erikson`, `B.F. Skinner`, `Jean Piaget`, `Ivan Pavlov`], ans:2, exp:`Jean Piaget proposed that children move through distinct stages of cognitive development as their thinking abilities mature.` },
    { q:`In Piaget's theory, during which stage do infants primarily learn about the world through their senses and physical actions?`, ch:[`Formal operational stage`, `Concrete operational stage`, `Preoperational stage`, `Sensorimotor stage`], ans:3, exp:`The sensorimotor stage, occurring roughly from birth to age 2, is when infants explore and learn through touching, looking, and moving.` },
    { q:`Which psychologist proposed a theory of psychosocial development spanning stages from infancy to late adulthood?`, ch:[`Erik Erikson`, `Wilhelm Wundt`, `Paul Ekman`, `Jean Piaget`], ans:0, exp:`Erik Erikson developed a theory describing key social and emotional challenges people face at different stages across the entire lifespan.` },
    { q:`According to Erikson, adolescents primarily wrestle with which developmental challenge?`, ch:[`Trust vs. mistrust`, `Identity vs. role confusion`, `Generativity vs. stagnation`, `Integrity vs. despair`], ans:1, exp:`Erikson believed adolescence centers on figuring out "who am I?"—a struggle he called identity vs. role confusion.` },
    { q:`According to Piaget, the formal operational stage, which involves abstract and logical reasoning, typically begins around what age?`, ch:[`Birth to age 2`, `Ages 2 to 7`, `Ages 7 to 11`, `Around age 12 and older`], ans:3, exp:`Piaget proposed that abstract, hypothetical reasoning typically emerges starting around age 12, in the formal operational stage.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd084', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Intro to Personality`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`How do psychologists typically define personality?`, ch:[`A person's IQ score`, `An individual's characteristic and relatively stable patterns of thoughts, feelings, and behaviors`, `A person's job title`, `A random and constantly changing set of behaviors`], ans:1, exp:`Personality refers to the fairly stable and consistent patterns of thinking, feeling, and behaving that make each person unique.` },
    { q:`The "Big Five" personality trait model includes Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and which other trait?`, ch:[`Intelligence`, `Neuroticism`, `Popularity`, `Athleticism`], ans:1, exp:`Neuroticism, which reflects a tendency toward emotional instability or anxiety, is the fifth trait in the Big Five model.` },
    { q:`Someone who is highly organized, disciplined, and reliable would score high on which Big Five trait?`, ch:[`Openness`, `Extraversion`, `Conscientiousness`, `Agreeableness`], ans:2, exp:`Conscientiousness describes traits like organization, dependability, and self-discipline.` },
    { q:`A person who is outgoing, energized by social interaction, and enjoys being around others would score high on which trait?`, ch:[`Neuroticism`, `Extraversion`, `Agreeableness`, `Openness`], ans:1, exp:`Extraversion reflects sociability and a tendency to gain energy from being around other people.` },
    { q:`Trait theories of personality generally propose what?`, ch:[`Personality is made up of relatively stable characteristics that differ from person to person`, `Personality changes completely every single day`, `Everyone has the exact same personality`, `Personality has nothing to do with behavior`], ans:0, exp:`Trait theories suggest personality can be described using a set of stable characteristics, or traits, that vary in degree from person to person.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is NOT one of the Big Five personality traits?`, ch:[`Openness`, `Agreeableness`, `Intelligence`, `Conscientiousness`], ans:2, exp:`Intelligence is not one of the Big Five traits; the five are Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism.` },
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd085', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`What Is Sociology?`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Sociology is best described as the study of what?`, ch:[`The human body's organ systems`,`Society, social relationships, and how groups of people behave`,`The chemical composition of living things`,`Ancient civilizations and archaeology`], ans:1, exp:`Sociology focuses on how people interact in groups, communities, and society as a whole.` },
    { q:`Which of these questions would a sociologist most likely ask?`, ch:[`How does a virus replicate inside a cell?`,`Why do teenagers in different neighborhoods have different graduation rates?`,`What is the boiling point of water at sea level?`,`How many bones are in the human hand?`], ans:1, exp:`Sociologists study patterns in how social factors like neighborhood or income shape people's lives and outcomes.` },
    { q:`A "society" in sociological terms refers to what?`, ch:[`A single person's personal beliefs`,`A group of people who share a culture, territory, and way of life`,`Only formal organizations like governments`,`A random collection of strangers`], ans:1, exp:`A society is a large group of people who live together and share customs, laws, and a common way of life.` },
    { q:`Why is sociology useful for someone interested in a healthcare career?`, ch:[`It teaches the names of every muscle in the body`,`It has no real connection to patient care`,`It helps explain how social factors like poverty or community affect people's health`,`It replaces the need to study biology`], ans:2, exp:`Understanding social factors helps future healthcare workers see the bigger picture behind why patients are healthy or unhealthy, not just their symptoms.` },
    { q:`Which term describes the different levels sociologists study, from small groups to whole societies?`, ch:[`Micro and macro perspectives`,`Photosynthesis levels`,`Genetic sequencing`,`Atomic structure`], ans:0, exp:`Sociologists often look at small-scale interactions (micro, like a family) and large-scale patterns (macro, like a whole country's economy).` },
    { q:`Sociology differs from psychology mainly because sociology focuses more on...`, ch:[`Individual brain chemistry`,`Groups, institutions, and social patterns rather than just the individual mind`,`Personal childhood memories only`,`Medications and their side effects`], ans:1, exp:`While psychology often looks inward at individual minds, sociology looks outward at groups, communities, and social structures.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd086', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Culture & Socialization Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What does "culture" refer to?`, ch:[`Only the food a group of people eats`,`The shared beliefs, values, customs, and behaviors of a group`,`A person's genetic makeup`,`The weather patterns of a region`], ans:1, exp:`Culture includes the traditions, values, language, and behaviors that a group of people share and pass down.` },
    { q:`What is "socialization"?`, ch:[`A medical procedure`,`The process of learning the norms and values of your society`,`A type of weather forecast`,`The study of rocks and minerals`], ans:1, exp:`Socialization is how people, especially children, learn the rules, values, and expected behaviors of their culture, often from family, school, and friends.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is typically considered a "norm"?`, ch:[`Shaking hands when meeting someone in many cultures`,`A person's blood type`,`The number of planets in the solar system`,`A random coincidence`], ans:0, exp:`Norms are the unwritten (or written) rules that guide expected behavior within a culture, like greeting customs.` },
    { q:`Which of these is an example of a "value" rather than a norm?`, ch:[`Waiting in line at a store`,`Believing that honesty is important`,`Saying "please" and "thank you"`,`Taking off shoes before entering a home`], ans:1, exp:`Values are broad beliefs about what is good or important, while norms are the specific behaviors that reflect those values.` },
    { q:`Which of these best describes "cultural relativism"?`, ch:[`Believing your own culture is superior to all others`,`Trying to understand another culture on its own terms rather than judging it by your own culture's standards`,`Ignoring cultural differences completely`,`Only studying cultures from textbooks`], ans:1, exp:`Cultural relativism means approaching other cultures with an open mind instead of assuming your own culture's way is the "right" one.` },
    { q:`Which agent of socialization typically has the earliest and one of the strongest influences on a child?`, ch:[`Social media`,`Family`,`Political campaigns`,`Sports leagues`], ans:1, exp:`Family is usually the first and one of the most powerful sources of a child's early values, language, and behaviors.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd087', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Social Groups & Roles`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`In sociology, what is a "social group"?`, ch:[`Any random gathering of strangers at a bus stop`,`Two or more people who interact and share a sense of belonging or identity`,`A single individual's personal opinions`,`A type of building`], ans:1, exp:`A social group involves people who interact regularly and feel connected, like a family, sports team, or club.` },
    { q:`What is a "primary group"?`, ch:[`A large, impersonal group like all employees of a company`,`A small, close-knit group with strong personal bonds, like family or close friends`,`A group you only interact with online`,`A group defined only by law`], ans:1, exp:`Primary groups are small and intimate, involving close, long-term relationships such as family and best friends.` },
    { q:`What is a "secondary group"?`, ch:[`A group based purely on emotional closeness`,`A larger, more impersonal group formed around a specific goal or activity, like a classroom or workplace`,`A group of exactly two people`,`Your immediate family only`], ans:1, exp:`Secondary groups are typically larger and more goal-oriented, such as coworkers or classmates, with less emotional closeness than primary groups.` },
    { q:`What does a "social role" refer to?`, ch:[`A person's permanent personality traits`,`The expected behaviors associated with a particular social position, like "student" or "doctor"`,`A type of government position only`,`A person's physical appearance`], ans:1, exp:`A social role is the set of expected behaviors that comes with a certain position in society, such as being a student, sibling, or employee.` },
    { q:`What is "role conflict"?`, ch:[`When someone forgets their role entirely`,`When the demands of two or more roles a person holds clash with each other`,`When a group has no assigned roles`,`When two strangers disagree about politics`], ans:1, exp:`Role conflict happens when the expectations of different roles, like being a student and a part-time employee, compete for a person's time or energy.` },
    { q:`A hospital volunteer who is also a full-time high school student is an example of holding what?`, ch:[`A single fixed identity`,`Multiple social roles at once`,`No social role at all`,`Only a primary group membership`], ans:1, exp:`People often hold several roles simultaneously, such as student, volunteer, sibling, and friend, each with its own expectations.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd088', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Understanding Stereotypes & Bias`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is a "stereotype"?`, ch:[`A carefully researched scientific fact about a group`,`An oversimplified and widely held belief about a group of people that may not reflect individuals accurately`,`A legal rule about how to treat people`,`A type of personality test`], ans:1, exp:`Stereotypes are generalized, often inaccurate assumptions applied to an entire group, which can unfairly label individuals within it.` },
    { q:`What is "implicit bias"?`, ch:[`A bias someone openly and intentionally states`,`Unconscious attitudes or assumptions that can affect how someone perceives or treats others without them realizing it`,`A rule written into a company's official policy`,`A type of medical diagnosis`], ans:1, exp:`Implicit bias refers to automatic, often unconscious attitudes that can shape our reactions to people, even when we don't intend to be biased.` },
    { q:`Why can stereotypes be harmful in a healthcare setting?`, ch:[`They have no real effect on patient care`,`They can lead providers to make inaccurate assumptions about a patient's symptoms or needs based on group membership`,`They always improve communication with patients`,`They are required by medical guidelines`], ans:1, exp:`If a provider assumes something about a patient based on a stereotype instead of listening to that individual, it can lead to misunderstandings or worse care.` },
    { q:`What is "prejudice"?`, ch:[`A neutral fact about a group`,`A preconceived opinion or feeling about a person or group, often negative, not based on actual experience or reason`,`A type of medical treatment`,`A law protecting minority groups`], ans:1, exp:`Prejudice is an unfair, preformed judgment about someone, usually based on their group membership rather than who they actually are.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is an example of challenging a stereotype?`, ch:[`Assuming all members of a group act the same way`,`Getting to know someone as an individual instead of relying on assumptions about their group`,`Avoiding people who are different from you`,`Repeating a generalization you heard without checking if it's true`], ans:1, exp:`Getting to know people as individuals helps break down inaccurate group-based assumptions.` },
    { q:`What is the difference between "prejudice" and "discrimination"?`, ch:[`They mean exactly the same thing`,`Prejudice is a belief or attitude, while discrimination is an unfair action taken based on that belief`,`Discrimination is always legal, prejudice is always illegal`,`Prejudice only applies to healthcare, discrimination only applies to schools`], ans:1, exp:`Prejudice happens in someone's mind as a biased attitude, while discrimination is when that bias leads to unfair treatment or actions.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd089', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Social Institutions 101`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is a "social institution"?`, ch:[`A single person's private opinion`,`An established, organized system of practices that meets basic needs of society, like family, education, or healthcare`,`A type of building only`,`A random social gathering`], ans:1, exp:`Social institutions are stable, organized structures like family, education, government, and healthcare that help society function.` },
    { q:`Which of these is considered a social institution?`, ch:[`A person's favorite hobby`,`The healthcare system`,`A single conversation between friends`,`An individual's mood`], ans:1, exp:`The healthcare system is a major social institution because it's an organized structure that provides an essential need across society.` },
    { q:`What is the main role of the "education" institution in society?`, ch:[`To provide entertainment only`,`To pass on knowledge, skills, and cultural values to new generations`,`To set the price of goods`,`To enforce traffic laws`], ans:1, exp:`Education systems exist to teach knowledge and skills and to help socialize young people into their culture.` },
    { q:`Why is "family" considered a core social institution?`, ch:[`Because it has no influence on individuals`,`Because it plays a key role in socializing children and providing support and care`,`Because it is only found in some cultures and not others`,`Because it is identical to government`], ans:1, exp:`Family is one of the most fundamental institutions because it usually provides the first socialization, care, and support a person receives.` },
    { q:`Which of these best describes the "economy" as a social institution?`, ch:[`A random collection of shopping malls`,`The system through which a society produces, distributes, and consumes goods and services`,`A type of religious practice`,`A single company's internal rules`], ans:1, exp:`The economy is the organized system societies use to produce and exchange goods, services, and resources.` },
    { q:`How can weaknesses in social institutions, like underfunded schools or limited healthcare access, affect communities?`, ch:[`They have no measurable effect on people's lives`,`They can contribute to unequal opportunities and outcomes for people in those communities`,`They only affect government officials`,`They automatically fix themselves over time`], ans:1, exp:`When institutions like schools or healthcare systems are under-resourced, the people who depend on them can face fewer opportunities and worse outcomes.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd090', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Media Literacy Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is "media literacy"?`, ch:[`The ability to memorize news headlines`,`The ability to access, analyze, evaluate, and create media thoughtfully`,`Owning a lot of social media accounts`,`A rule that bans watching the news`], ans:1, exp:`Media literacy is the skill of critically thinking about the messages, sources, and purposes behind the media we consume.` },
    { q:`Why is it important to check the source of a health-related article before believing it?`, ch:[`Sources never matter for health information`,`Some sources are more reliable and evidence-based than others, and inaccurate information can be harmful`,`All websites are equally trustworthy`,`Only newspapers can be trusted`], ans:1, exp:`Not all sources verify their information carefully, so checking credibility helps you avoid spreading or believing false health claims.` },
    { q:`What is "misinformation"?`, ch:[`Information that is deliberately created to deceive people`,`False or inaccurate information that is spread, regardless of intent to deceive`,`Only information found in printed books`,`A synonym for opinion pieces`], ans:1, exp:`Misinformation is incorrect information shared, even if the person sharing it didn't mean to mislead anyone.` },
    { q:`What distinguishes "disinformation" from "misinformation"?`, ch:[`They are identical terms`,`Disinformation is deliberately created and spread to deceive, while misinformation may be spread unintentionally`,`Disinformation is always true`,`Misinformation only appears in advertisements`], ans:1, exp:`Disinformation involves intentional deception, while misinformation can be false information shared by mistake.` },
    { q:`Which of these is a good media literacy habit?`, ch:[`Believing the first headline you see without checking further`,`Cross-checking a surprising claim with more than one credible source`,`Sharing articles without reading past the headline`,`Assuming all social media posts are fact-checked`], ans:1, exp:`Verifying claims across multiple trustworthy sources helps you avoid being misled by inaccurate or exaggerated information.` },
    { q:`Why might understanding media literacy be helpful for a future healthcare professional?`, ch:[`It has no connection to patient interactions`,`Patients often encounter health information online, and providers may need to help them evaluate what's accurate`,`It only matters for journalists`,`It replaces the need for medical training`], ans:1, exp:`Patients frequently bring in information from the internet, so healthcare workers benefit from helping guide them toward reliable, evidence-based sources.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd091', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Health Disparities 101`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is a "health disparity"?`, ch:[`A difference in health outcomes that occurs purely by random chance`,`A preventable difference in health outcomes that is closely linked to social, economic, or environmental disadvantage`,`Any difference in height between two people`,`A type of medical test`], ans:1, exp:`Health disparities are differences in health that are avoidable and often tied to factors like income, location, or access to care.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is an example of a "social determinant of health"?`, ch:[`A person's eye color`,`Access to safe housing and nutritious food`,`The season of the year someone was born`,`A person's favorite subject in school`], ans:1, exp:`Social determinants of health are the conditions in which people live and work, like housing, food access, and education, that shape their health.` },
    { q:`Why might someone living in a "food desert" (an area with limited access to affordable, healthy food) face greater health risks?`, ch:[`Food deserts have no connection to health outcomes`,`Limited access to nutritious food can contribute to poor diet-related health conditions over time`,`They are guaranteed to have better health than others`,`Food deserts only exist in fictional stories`], ans:1, exp:`When healthy food options are hard to access, people may rely more on less nutritious options, which can contribute to health problems over time.` },
    { q:`Which factor is commonly linked to health disparities in the United States?`, ch:[`Unequal access to healthcare based on income or insurance status`,`Everyone in the country has identical access to healthcare regardless of income`,`Health disparities do not exist in developed countries`,`Only genetics determine all health outcomes`], ans:0, exp:`Access to healthcare, often tied to income and insurance, is a major factor contributing to unequal health outcomes among different groups.` },
    { q:`What does "healthcare access" refer to?`, ch:[`Only whether a hospital exists in a country`,`The ability of people to actually obtain needed medical services, including affordability, availability, and location`,`A type of medical degree`,`The number of doctors on television`], ans:1, exp:`Healthcare access includes whether people can realistically get the care they need, considering cost, distance, insurance, and availability.` },
    { q:`Why is understanding health disparities important for future healthcare workers?`, ch:[`It has no impact on how they should care for patients`,`It helps them understand why some patients face greater barriers to good health and how to address those barriers`,`It only matters for hospital administrators`,`It is only relevant in other countries`], ans:1, exp:`Recognizing the social factors behind health disparities helps future providers give more thoughtful, effective care to all patients.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd092', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Communication Skills Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is "active listening"?`, ch:[`Waiting for your turn to speak while thinking about something else`,`Fully concentrating on, understanding, and responding thoughtfully to what someone is saying`,`Interrupting someone to share your own opinion`,`Reading a text message while someone talks to you`], ans:1, exp:`Active listening means giving your full attention to the speaker and showing that you understand and care about what they're saying.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is an example of "nonverbal communication"?`, ch:[`The exact words in an email`,`Eye contact and facial expressions`,`A written report`,`A phone number`], ans:1, exp:`Nonverbal communication includes body language, facial expressions, tone of voice, and eye contact, not just spoken or written words.` },
    { q:`Why is clear communication especially important between a healthcare provider and a patient?`, ch:[`It has little effect on patient understanding or trust`,`It helps ensure patients understand their diagnosis, treatment, and instructions correctly`,`It is only important for insurance paperwork`,`Patients never need explanations`], ans:1, exp:`Clear communication helps patients truly understand their health situation, which supports better decisions and outcomes.` },
    { q:`What is an "open-ended question"?`, ch:[`A question that can only be answered with "yes" or "no"`,`A question that invites a detailed response rather than a simple answer`,`A question with no possible answer`,`A question about closing a conversation`], ans:1, exp:`Open-ended questions, like "Can you describe how you're feeling?", encourage someone to share more detail than a yes/no question would.` },
    { q:`Which behavior would likely make someone feel unheard during a conversation?`, ch:[`Nodding and making eye contact while they speak`,`Frequently checking your phone while they are talking`,`Asking a follow-up question about what they said`,`Repeating back what you understood to confirm it`], ans:1, exp:`Being distracted, like checking your phone, signals that you're not fully paying attention, which can make the other person feel dismissed.` },
    { q:`What does "paraphrasing" mean in communication?`, ch:[`Repeating someone's exact words verbatim`,`Restating what someone said in your own words to show understanding`,`Ignoring what someone said and changing the subject`,`Writing a formal essay`], ans:1, exp:`Paraphrasing involves reflecting back the speaker's message in your own words, which confirms you understood them correctly.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd093', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Empathy & Bedside Manner Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is "empathy"?`, ch:[`Feeling sorry for someone from a distance`,`The ability to understand and share the feelings of another person`,`Agreeing with everything someone says`,`Ignoring other people's emotions`], ans:1, exp:`Empathy means putting yourself in someone else's shoes and genuinely trying to understand what they're feeling.` },
    { q:`What is the difference between "empathy" and "sympathy"?`, ch:[`They mean exactly the same thing`,`Empathy involves understanding and sharing someone's feelings, while sympathy is more about feeling sorry for them`,`Sympathy always requires more emotional connection than empathy`,`Empathy is only used in medical settings`], ans:1, exp:`Empathy involves connecting with and understanding another person's emotions, while sympathy is more like feeling pity or concern from the outside.` },
    { q:`What does "bedside manner" refer to in healthcare?`, ch:[`The furniture arrangement in a hospital room`,`How a healthcare provider interacts with and treats patients, including kindness and communication`,`A doctor's medical test results`,`The schedule of hospital visiting hours`], ans:1, exp:`Bedside manner describes the way a provider communicates with and treats patients, including warmth, respect, and clear explanations.` },
    { q:`Which response shows good empathetic communication to someone who is nervous before a medical procedure?`, ch:[`"Just relax, it's not a big deal."`,`"I understand this feels scary. Let's talk through what will happen so you feel more prepared."`,`Ignoring their concerns and moving on quickly`,`Telling them their fear is unreasonable`], ans:1, exp:`Acknowledging the person's feelings and offering support shows genuine empathy, rather than dismissing their concerns.` },
    { q:`Why does empathy matter in healthcare settings?`, ch:[`It has no effect on patient trust or comfort`,`It helps patients feel heard, respected, and more comfortable sharing important information`,`It slows down every appointment unnecessarily`,`It is only useful for pediatric patients`], ans:1, exp:`When patients feel understood, they're often more comfortable being open and honest, which helps providers give better care.` },
    { q:`Which of these is an example of showing empathy through nonverbal communication?`, ch:[`Crossing your arms and avoiding eye contact`,`Sitting at the patient's eye level and maintaining a warm, attentive expression`,`Checking your watch repeatedly`,`Speaking in a rushed, dismissive tone`], ans:1, exp:`Body language like sitting at eye level and having a warm expression can communicate care and attentiveness without words.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd094', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Ethics 101: Right, Wrong & Gray Areas`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is "ethics"?`, ch:[`A set of laws enforced by police`,`The study of principles about what is right and wrong or good and bad behavior`,`A branch of biology`,`A type of medical test`], ans:1, exp:`Ethics is the study of moral principles that guide what people consider right or wrong behavior.` },
    { q:`What is the key difference between "ethics" and "law"?`, ch:[`They are always exactly the same thing`,`Laws are official rules enforced by government, while ethics involves broader moral principles that may not always be legally required`,`Ethics only applies to doctors`,`Laws never relate to morality`], ans:1, exp:`Laws are formal rules set by governments, while ethics is about moral principles, some of which are reflected in law and some of which are not.` },
    { q:`What is an "ethical dilemma"?`, ch:[`A situation with one clearly correct answer`,`A situation where a person must choose between two or more options that each involve competing values or principles`,`A type of scientific experiment`,`A legal contract`], ans:1, exp:`An ethical dilemma occurs when there's no easy answer because different values or responsibilities conflict with each other.` },
    { q:`In healthcare, what does "patient confidentiality" mean?`, ch:[`Sharing a patient's medical information with anyone who asks`,`Keeping a patient's private health information protected and only sharing it with appropriate permission`,`Publishing patient records publicly for research`,`Telling a patient's family everything without asking`], ans:1, exp:`Confidentiality means protecting a patient's private health information and only sharing it when appropriate and permitted.` },
    { q:`Which of the following best illustrates an ethical "gray area"?`, ch:[`Deciding whether stealing is generally wrong`,`Deciding how to allocate a limited medical resource fairly among several patients who each need it`,`Choosing what color scrubs to wear`,`Calculating a patient's height in centimeters`], ans:1, exp:`Situations involving competing needs and no perfect solution, like limited resources, are classic examples of ethical gray areas.` },
    { q:`Why is it valuable for future healthcare workers to think through ethical scenarios early on?`, ch:[`Ethics has no relevance to real patient care`,`It helps them build the reasoning skills needed to navigate difficult, real-world decisions with care and fairness`,`It guarantees there will never be difficult decisions in their career`,`It replaces the need for scientific knowledge`], ans:1, exp:`Practicing ethical reasoning helps future healthcare professionals thoughtfully navigate the complex, real decisions they'll eventually face.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd095', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Intro to Public Health`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the main focus of "public health," as opposed to individual medical treatment?`, ch:[`Treating one patient's illness at a time in a clinic`,`Protecting and improving the health of entire populations and communities`,`Performing surgery`,`Selling health insurance plans`], ans:1, exp:`Public health focuses on preventing disease and promoting health across whole populations, rather than treating individual patients one at a time.` },
    { q:`Which of these is an example of a public health initiative?`, ch:[`A single doctor prescribing medication to one patient`,`A community-wide vaccination campaign`,`An individual's personal diet plan`,`A private consultation about one patient's allergy`], ans:1, exp:`Public health efforts, like vaccination campaigns, aim to protect and improve the health of large groups of people at once.` },
    { q:`What does "prevention" mean in a public health context?`, ch:[`Waiting until people get sick before doing anything`,`Taking action to stop health problems before they occur or worsen`,`Treating diseases only with surgery`,`A type of medical insurance`], ans:1, exp:`Prevention focuses on stopping illness or injury before it happens, such as through education, vaccines, or safety regulations.` },
    { q:`Why do public health officials track disease outbreaks across a population?`, ch:[`To identify patterns and respond quickly to protect community health`,`Tracking outbreaks has no practical use`,`Only to fill out paperwork`,`Because it is required for hospital billing`], ans:0, exp:`Monitoring outbreaks helps public health officials understand how a disease is spreading so they can respond effectively and protect people.` },
    { q:`Which of these best describes "epidemiology" in simple terms?`, ch:[`The study of ancient languages`,`The study of how diseases spread and affect populations`,`The study of building construction`,`The study of individual patient billing`], ans:1, exp:`Epidemiology is the study of patterns, causes, and effects of health and disease within populations.` },
    { q:`Which of these is a public health strategy aimed at improving community well-being rather than treating one patient?`, ch:[`Prescribing a specific patient's medication dosage`,`Creating clean water and sanitation systems for a city`,`Diagnosing one patient's broken bone`,`Scheduling a single patient's follow-up appointment`], ans:1, exp:`Building clean water and sanitation systems protects the health of an entire community, which is a classic public health goal.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd096', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Cultural Competence in Healthcare`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is "cultural competence" in a healthcare setting?`, ch:[`Speaking only one language fluently`,`The ability to understand, respect, and effectively interact with people from different cultural backgrounds`,`Following the same approach for every patient regardless of their background`,`A type of medical certification exam`], ans:1, exp:`Cultural competence means being aware of and respectful toward the diverse backgrounds, beliefs, and needs of different patients.` },
    { q:`Why might cultural competence improve the quality of patient care?`, ch:[`It has no real impact on patient outcomes`,`It helps providers understand patients' values and beliefs, building trust and improving communication`,`It only matters for patients who speak a foreign language`,`It slows down every single interaction unnecessarily`], ans:1, exp:`When providers understand a patient's cultural background, they can communicate more effectively and build trust, which improves care.` },
    { q:`What role can a "medical interpreter" play in healthcare?`, ch:[`They have no official role in patient care`,`They help bridge language differences so patients and providers can communicate clearly and accurately`,`They replace the need for a doctor entirely`,`They only translate written documents, never spoken conversations`], ans:1, exp:`Medical interpreters help ensure patients who speak different languages can accurately understand and be understood by their healthcare providers.` },
    { q:`Which approach reflects culturally competent care?`, ch:[`Assuming every patient from a certain background wants the exact same treatment`,`Asking patients about their individual preferences, beliefs, and needs rather than making assumptions`,`Ignoring a patient's cultural or religious concerns entirely`,`Refusing to adjust communication styles for any patient`], ans:1, exp:`Culturally competent care means treating each patient as an individual and asking about their specific needs rather than stereotyping.` },
    { q:`Why is it important for healthcare providers to be aware that some patients may have cultural or religious beliefs affecting their medical decisions?`, ch:[`Because beliefs never influence real medical decisions`,`Because understanding these beliefs helps providers offer respectful, patient-centered care that honors the patient's values`,`Because it allows providers to ignore evidence-based medicine`,`Because all patients share identical beliefs`], ans:1, exp:`Respecting a patient's beliefs while providing care helps build trust and ensures the patient feels heard and respected in their treatment decisions.` },
    { q:`Which of these is a simple way future healthcare workers can start building cultural competence today?`, ch:[`Avoiding contact with people from different backgrounds`,`Learning about and genuinely listening to people whose experiences and backgrounds differ from their own`,`Assuming their own cultural perspective is the only correct one`,`Waiting until medical school to think about it for the first time`], ans:1, exp:`Actively learning about and listening to people with different backgrounds now helps build the awareness and respect needed for culturally competent care later.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd097', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Time Management Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is "time blocking"?`, ch:[`Scheduling specific blocks of time for specific tasks or subjects`,`Refusing to schedule anything and working whenever you feel like it`,`Memorizing your class schedule by heart`,`Blocking your phone permanently so you never use it again`], ans:0, exp:`Time blocking means assigning a specific chunk of your day to a specific task, which helps you actually get to everything instead of letting one thing eat your whole afternoon.` },
    { q:`According to the "two-minute rule," what should you do with a task that takes less than two minutes to complete?`, ch:[`Save it for the end of the week`,`Add it to a long-term goal list`,`Do it right away instead of putting it off`,`Ask someone else to do it for you`], ans:2, exp:`Quick tasks are more efficient to knock out immediately than to track, remember, and revisit later — that mental tracking often takes longer than the task itself.` },
    { q:`Why is multitasking (like texting while studying) usually less effective than focusing on one task at a time?`, ch:[`It uses more paper`,`Switching between tasks costs time and mental energy, so both tasks suffer`,`It makes your handwriting worse`,`Multitasking is actually always more efficient`], ans:1, exp:`Your brain isn't truly doing two things at once — it's rapidly switching, and each switch costs focus and time, which lowers the quality of your work.` },
    { q:`Which of these is a common time-wasting trap for students trying to study?`, ch:[`Turning off phone notifications`,`Studying in a quiet room`,`Keeping social media notifications on during study time`,`Using a timer to track focus sessions`], ans:2, exp:`Notifications repeatedly pull your attention away, and it can take several minutes to fully refocus after each interruption.` },
    { q:`The Pomodoro Technique typically involves what pattern?`, ch:[`Studying for 3 hours straight with no breaks`,`25 minutes of focused work followed by a 5-minute break`,`Studying only late at night`,`One long break followed by a short study session`], ans:1, exp:`Short focused sprints with built-in breaks help you stay sharp and avoid burning out during a long study session.` },
    { q:`A tool that helps you sort tasks by both urgency and importance (like "urgent and important" vs. "not urgent, not important") is called what?`, ch:[`The Eisenhower Matrix`,`The Pomodoro Technique`,`Spaced repetition`,`The two-minute rule`], ans:0, exp:`The Eisenhower Matrix helps you decide what to do first, what to schedule for later, and what to skip, based on how urgent and important each task really is.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd098', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Effective Study Techniques`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Why is highlighting an entire paragraph in a textbook generally a weak study strategy on its own?`, ch:[`It permanently damages the pages`,`It makes reading feel too fast`,`It creates the illusion of learning without requiring you to actively process or recall the information`,`Highlighters are expensive`], ans:2, exp:`Highlighting feels productive, but it's passive — your brain isn't being forced to retrieve or apply the information, so it doesn't stick as well as more active methods.` },
    { q:`What does it mean to use "interleaving" while studying?`, ch:[`Studying one topic for many hours in a row`,`Mixing different topics or problem types within a single study session`,`Copying notes word for word`,`Only studying right before a test`], ans:1, exp:`Interleaving mixes related topics together, which trains your brain to recognize which method or concept to use — a skill you also need on real tests.` },
    { q:`Which strategy best describes "the Feynman Technique"?`, ch:[`Rereading your notes silently five times`,`Explaining a concept in simple terms as if teaching it to someone else`,`Listening to a lecture on double speed`,`Memorizing definitions without context`], ans:1, exp:`If you can explain an idea simply and clearly, it shows you truly understand it — and teaching also strengthens your own memory of the material.` },
    { q:`Why is studying in a completely silent, unchanging environment every single time not always ideal?`, ch:[`Silence damages your hearing over time`,`It's against most study guides`,`Varying your study location can actually help you recall information in more situations, like on test day`, `It guarantees you'll fall asleep`], ans:2, exp:`Practicing in different settings helps your brain build more flexible memory cues, so you're not thrown off if the test environment feels different from your usual spot.` },
    { q:`What is a key benefit of practicing with sample problems instead of just rereading solved examples?`, ch:[`It takes less time overall`,`It requires you to actively retrieve and apply knowledge, which builds stronger understanding`,`It guarantees a perfect score`,`It replaces the need to learn the material at all`], ans:1, exp:`Actually attempting problems forces your brain to retrieve and use information, which builds deeper, more durable understanding than passively reviewing answers.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is considered a passive study technique, generally less effective when used alone?`, ch:[`Self-testing with flashcards`,`Teaching a concept to a friend`,`Rereading the textbook chapter without stopping to check understanding`,`Solving practice problems from memory`], ans:2, exp:`Simply rereading text lets your eyes move over words without truly checking whether you understand or remember them, so it's one of the weaker study methods by itself.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd099', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Active Recall & Spaced Repetition`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is "active recall"?`, ch:[`Rereading your notes multiple times in a row`,`Testing yourself on information without looking at your notes first`,`Listening to a recorded lecture again`,`Organizing your notes by color`], ans:1, exp:`Active recall means retrieving information from memory on your own, which strengthens the neural pathways for that information far more than just looking it over.` },
    { q:`Why does spaced repetition generally lead to better long-term retention than cramming the night before a test?`, ch:[`It takes exactly the same amount of total time as cramming`,`Spreading review sessions out over time forces your brain to re-retrieve information as it starts to fade, which strengthens memory`,`Cramming is scientifically proven to be more effective for exams`,`Spaced repetition only works for math, not science`], ans:1, exp:`Each time you recall something just as you're starting to forget it, that memory gets reinforced — this repeated "struggle to remember" is what makes spaced repetition so powerful.` },
    { q:`A student uses flashcards but reviews the whole deck equally every day, regardless of which cards they already know well. What could improve this approach?`, ch:[`Throwing away all the flashcards`,`Reviewing cards they already know more often than the ones they're struggling with`,`Using a system that reviews difficult cards more frequently and easy ones less often`,`Only studying the deck once a week`], ans:2, exp:`Spaced repetition systems space out review based on how well you know each item, so your time goes toward the material that actually still needs work.` },
    { q:`What is the "forgetting curve" a concept used to describe?`, ch:[`How handwriting gets messier over time`,`The natural decline in memory of new information over time if it isn't reviewed`,`The order in which students finish a test`,`How grades typically drop during senior year`], ans:1, exp:`The forgetting curve shows that without review, we lose a large portion of new information within days — which is exactly why spaced review sessions matter.` },
    { q:`Which activity is the best example of active recall while studying biology vocabulary?`, ch:[`Reading the glossary from start to finish`,`Covering the definitions and trying to say them from memory before checking`,`Copying each term and definition three times`,`Highlighting all the bolded vocabulary words`], ans:1, exp:`Trying to produce the answer yourself before checking is what makes this active recall — it tests your memory rather than just exposing you to the words again.` },
    { q:`Why might a student who crams the night before a test do okay on that test but struggle in a cumulative final exam later?`, ch:[`Cumulative finals are always unfairly written`,`Cramming builds permanent long-term memory just as well as spaced study`,`Information crammed in short-term memory tends to fade quickly without spaced review, so it's not available months later`,`Final exams never cover earlier material`], ans:2, exp:`Cramming can get information into short-term memory for a day or two, but without spaced review it fades fast, leaving little for a later cumulative test.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd100', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Test-Taking Strategies`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`When taking a timed multiple-choice test, what is generally a smart first step?`, ch:[`Answer every question in the exact order it appears, no matter how long each one takes`,`Skim through and answer the questions you're confident about first, then return to harder ones`,`Immediately guess on every question`,`Spend all your remaining time on the hardest question first`], ans:1, exp:`Answering easier questions first builds momentum, banks points quickly, and leaves more time to think carefully about the tougher ones.` },
    { q:`On a multiple-choice question where you're unsure of the answer, what's a good strategy?`, ch:[`Pick the longest answer choice automatically`,`Skip it and leave it blank forever`,`Eliminate answers you know are wrong to improve your odds among the remaining choices`,`Always choose the same letter for every guess`], ans:2, exp:`Ruling out clearly incorrect choices increases the probability that your guess among the remaining options is correct.` },
    { q:`Why is it important to read test instructions carefully before starting?`, ch:[`Instructions never matter for the actual score`,`They might specify things like point values, time limits, or whether you can go back to previous sections`,`It's just a formality with no real content`,`Only the first question's instructions matter`], ans:1, exp:`Instructions often contain details — like scoring rules or section time limits — that can change how you should approach the test.` },
    { q:`What is a helpful strategy for tackling a long reading passage followed by questions?`, ch:[`Skip the passage and guess on all questions`,`Read the questions first to know what to look for, or skim the passage for structure before diving into detail`,`Memorize the entire passage word for word`,`Only read the last paragraph of the passage`], ans:1, exp:`Previewing questions or skimming for structure helps you focus your careful reading on the parts of the passage that actually matter for answering.` },
    { q:`If you finish a test with extra time remaining, what should you do?`, ch:[`Turn it in immediately without looking back`,`Review your answers, especially ones you were unsure about, and check for careless errors`,`Change all your answers to a different letter`,`Start doodling since there's nothing else useful to do`], ans:1, exp:`Using leftover time to double-check flagged questions and look for simple mistakes, like a misread number, can catch easy points you might otherwise lose.` },
    { q:`Why is it usually a bad idea to change an answer at the last second just because you have a sudden "feeling"?`, ch:[`Changing answers is against the rules on most tests`,`Research on test-taking generally shows first instincts are correct more often than gut-driven last-second changes made without a clear reason`,`It's fine, first instincts are always wrong`,`Answer changes are never allowed to be erased`], ans:1, exp:`Studies on test-taking behavior show that changes made with solid reasoning (like realizing you misread the question) tend to help, but ones based purely on nervous second-guessing often hurt more than they help.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd101', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Note-Taking Methods`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the main idea behind the Cornell Note-Taking System?`, ch:[`Writing everything the teacher says word for word`,`Dividing your page into sections for notes, cues/questions, and a summary`,`Taking notes only in bullet points with no structure`,`Recording the entire class instead of writing anything`], ans:1, exp:`The Cornell method splits your page into a notes column, a cue column for key questions or terms, and a summary section, which makes review much easier later.` },
    { q:`Why is it usually better to write notes in your own words rather than copying the textbook or slides exactly?`, ch:[`Copying exactly is always faster and just as effective`,`Putting ideas in your own words forces you to actually process and understand the material, not just transcribe it`,`Teachers require notes to be in your own words`,`Exact copies are illegal to use`], ans:1, exp:`Rephrasing information requires your brain to understand it well enough to restate it, which builds much stronger understanding than simple transcription.` },
    { q:`What is a "mind map" useful for?`, ch:[`Listing information in strict chronological order only`,`Visually showing how different ideas or concepts connect to a central topic`,`Replacing the need to study entirely`,`Writing out full sentences for every idea`], ans:1, exp:`Mind maps use branches and connections to show relationships between ideas, which can help you see the bigger picture of how concepts link together.` },
    { q:`Which of these is a downside of trying to write down every single word a teacher says during a lecture?`, ch:[`It's technically impossible to write anything at all`,`You may miss the chance to actually think about and understand what's being said in the moment`,`It guarantees better grades every time`,`Teachers will always object to it`], ans:1, exp:`Focusing purely on transcription can turn note-taking into a passive typing exercise, leaving less mental energy to actually process and understand the content.` },
    { q:`In the "outline method" of note-taking, how is information typically organized?`, ch:[`Randomly scattered across the page`,`In a hierarchy with main topics and indented subpoints beneath them`,`Only as a single paragraph with no breaks`,`As a set of unrelated flashcards`], ans:1, exp:`The outline method uses indentation to show which ideas are main points and which are supporting details, making the structure of the material easy to see at a glance.` },
    { q:`Why is reviewing and reorganizing your notes shortly after class (like the same day) considered a good habit?`, ch:[`It has no real effect on memory`,`It helps reinforce the material while it's still fresh and lets you fill in gaps or fix unclear parts`,`It's only useful the night before a final exam`,`Notes should never be touched again after class`], ans:1, exp:`Reviewing soon after class catches confusing spots while the lecture is still fresh in your memory, and reinforces the material before it starts to fade.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd102', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Goal Setting for Students`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What does the "S" in a SMART goal stand for?`, ch:[`Simple`,`Specific`,`Short`,`Silent`], ans:1, exp:`A specific goal clearly states exactly what you want to accomplish, instead of something vague like "do better in school."` },
    { q:`Which of these is an example of a measurable goal?`, ch:[`"I want to get better at chemistry"`,`"I will raise my chemistry quiz average from a 75% to an 85% this semester"`,`"I like chemistry class"`,`"Chemistry is important"`], ans:1, exp:`This goal includes a specific number you can track, so you'll know exactly whether you've achieved it.` },
    { q:`Why is it helpful to break a large goal, like "improve my grades," into smaller weekly goals?`, ch:[`Smaller goals are easier to track progress on and feel less overwhelming`,`Large goals are technically against school rules`,`Breaking goals down always doubles the amount of total work`,`It has no real benefit compared to one big goal`], ans:0, exp:`Smaller, concrete steps make progress visible and manageable, which keeps you motivated instead of feeling stuck facing one huge, vague task.` },
    { q:`What is the difference between a "process goal" and an "outcome goal"?`, ch:[`There is no real difference between them`,`A process goal focuses on the actions you'll take, while an outcome goal focuses on the final result`,`Process goals only apply to sports`,`Outcome goals are always easier to control than process goals`], ans:1, exp:`A process goal, like "study 30 minutes every night," focuses on actions you fully control, while an outcome goal, like "get an A," depends partly on factors outside your control.` },
    { q:`Why is setting a deadline (the "T" for time-bound) important for a goal?`, ch:[`Deadlines make goals impossible to achieve`,`It has no impact on whether a goal gets completed`,`A deadline creates urgency and helps you plan backward to figure out what to do and when`,`Deadlines are only useful for teachers, not students`], ans:2, exp:`Without a deadline, a goal can easily get pushed off indefinitely — a clear timeframe helps you plan concrete steps and stay accountable.` },
    { q:`A student sets the goal "I will do well this year." What is the main problem with this goal?`, ch:[`It's too specific and measurable`,`It's vague and doesn't define what "well" means or by when, making it hard to track progress`,`It already includes a clear deadline`,`It's impossible to have any goals about school`], ans:1, exp:`Without specifics, it's unclear what success looks like or how to measure progress, which makes the goal hard to actually work toward.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd103', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Managing Test Anxiety`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is a common physical symptom of test anxiety?`, ch:[`Improved memory recall`,`Racing heart, sweaty palms, or an upset stomach before or during a test`,`Falling asleep instantly`,`Increased appetite only`], ans:1, exp:`Test anxiety often triggers the body's stress response, which can cause physical symptoms like a racing heart or stomach discomfort.` },
    { q:`Why can slow, deep breathing help before or during a stressful test?`, ch:[`It has no physiological effect at all`,`It activates the body's relaxation response, which can help calm a racing heart and clear your thinking`,`It uses up time you need for answering questions`,`It only works if done for over an hour`], ans:1, exp:`Slow breathing helps shift your nervous system out of "fight or flight" mode, which can lower physical stress symptoms and help you think more clearly.` },
    { q:`Which of these is generally the most effective long-term way to reduce test anxiety?`, ch:[`Avoiding studying so you don't think about the test`,`Consistent preparation and practice well before the test, so you walk in feeling ready`,`Staying up all night before the test to cram`,`Skipping the test entirely`], ans:1, exp:`Feeling genuinely prepared, built through consistent study over time, is one of the strongest ways to reduce anxiety because it replaces uncertainty with confidence.` },
    { q:`What is a helpful strategy if your mind goes blank during a test?`, ch:[`Give up on the entire test immediately`,`Panic and rush through every remaining question`,`Pause, take a slow breath, and move to a different question before coming back`,`Erase everything you've written so far`], ans:2, exp:`Stepping away from a question that's causing you to freeze and returning to it later, after calming down, can help your brain reset and recall the information.` },
    { q:`How can negative self-talk, like "I'm going to fail this," affect test performance?`, ch:[`It has no impact on how you actually perform`,`It can increase anxiety and distract you from focusing on the actual questions`,`It always improves focus and performance`,`It only affects performance in math classes`], ans:1, exp:`Negative self-talk can spike anxiety and pull your attention away from the test itself, making it harder to think clearly and recall what you've studied.` },
    { q:`Why is it recommended to avoid comparing your progress to classmates right before a test?`, ch:[`Comparison always boosts confidence`,`It's required by most schools`,`Comparing yourself to others can increase stress and distract from focusing on your own preparation`,`It has no effect on anxiety levels`], ans:2, exp:`Focusing on someone else's pace or confidence can spike unnecessary stress — your own preparation and mindset are what actually matter for your performance.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd104', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Building a Study Schedule`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Why is it generally more effective to study a little bit each day rather than one long session right before a test?`, ch:[`Long single sessions are always more effective`,`Distributed practice over several days helps information stick better in long-term memory`,`Daily studying takes more total hours than cramming`,`It doesn't matter how you space out your studying`], ans:1, exp:`Spreading study sessions across multiple days, known as distributed practice, gives your brain repeated chances to reinforce memory, leading to better retention than one marathon session.` },
    { q:`When building a weekly study schedule, why is it useful to first block out fixed commitments (like classes, sports, or a job)?`, ch:[`Fixed commitments should be ignored when planning`,`It helps you see realistically how much free time you actually have for studying`,`It has no effect on planning`,`It guarantees you'll have no time to study at all`], ans:1, exp:`Seeing your fixed obligations first gives you an honest picture of your available time, so your study plan is realistic instead of overly ambitious.` },
    { q:`What is a good reason to schedule your hardest or least favorite subject earlier in a study session rather than last?`, ch:[`Hard subjects should always be skipped entirely`,`Your focus and energy are often higher earlier on, before mental fatigue sets in`,`It guarantees you'll finish faster`,`The order never makes a difference`], ans:1, exp:`Tackling challenging material while your focus is freshest can make it easier to push through, rather than saving it for when you're tired and less motivated.` },
    { q:`Why should a realistic study schedule include planned breaks?`, ch:[`Breaks are a waste of valuable study time`,`Breaks help prevent mental fatigue and can improve overall focus and retention`,`Breaks should only happen once a week`,`Scheduling breaks guarantees you'll get worse grades`], ans:1, exp:`Regular breaks help your brain recover and maintain focus, which actually improves the quality of your studying compared to pushing through exhaustion.` },
    { q:`If you find you consistently can't finish everything on your weekly study schedule, what's a reasonable adjustment?`, ch:[`Give up on having a schedule altogether`,`Reevaluate and adjust the schedule to be more realistic about how much time tasks actually take`,`Add even more tasks to the same time slots`,`Ignore the problem and hope it fixes itself`], ans:1, exp:`A schedule should be a flexible tool — if it's consistently unrealistic, adjusting it to match your actual pace makes it far more useful and less discouraging.` },
    { q:`What is a benefit of reviewing your study schedule at the end of each week?`, ch:[`It lets you see what worked, what didn't, and adjust the coming week accordingly`,`It has no practical benefit`,`It's only useful right before finals`,`Weekly reviews should replace daily studying entirely`], ans:0, exp:`A quick weekly check-in helps you spot patterns, like a subject that keeps taking longer than planned, so you can adjust and plan smarter going forward.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd105', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Healthy Habits for Students`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Why is getting enough sleep important for academic performance?`, ch:[`Sleep has no connection to memory or learning`,`Sleep plays a key role in consolidating memories and helping the brain process what you learned during the day`,`Sleep only matters for physical growth, not the brain`,`More sleep always lowers test scores`], ans:1, exp:`During sleep, your brain works to consolidate and strengthen the memories you formed during the day, which is a big reason pulling all-nighters tends to backfire.` },
    { q:`How can regular physical activity benefit a student's academic performance?`, ch:[`It has no effect on brain function`,`Exercise can improve mood, reduce stress, and support better concentration`,`Exercise always makes people too tired to study`,`Physical activity only benefits athletes, not students in general`], ans:1, exp:`Regular exercise increases blood flow to the brain and can reduce stress hormones, both of which support better focus and mood for studying.` },
    { q:`Why might skipping breakfast negatively affect focus during morning classes?`, ch:[`Breakfast has no impact on concentration`,`Low blood sugar from skipping meals can make it harder to concentrate and stay alert`,`Skipping breakfast always improves test scores`,`Breakfast only affects athletes`], ans:1, exp:`Your brain needs steady energy to function well, and skipping a meal can lead to low blood sugar, making it harder to focus and think clearly.` },
    { q:`What effect can chronic stress have on learning and memory if it's not managed?`, ch:[`Chronic stress always improves memory formation`,`Prolonged high stress can interfere with concentration and make it harder to encode and recall information`,`Stress has no biological effect on the brain`,`Stress only affects physical health, never mental performance`], ans:1, exp:`Ongoing high stress can affect brain regions involved in memory and focus, making learning and recall noticeably harder over time.` },
    { q:`Why is staying hydrated throughout the school day relevant to concentration?`, ch:[`Hydration has no effect on brain function`,`Even mild dehydration can affect concentration, alertness, and mood`,`Drinking water only matters during sports practice`,`Water intake affects only your appetite, not your focus`], ans:1, exp:`The brain is highly sensitive to hydration levels, and even mild dehydration has been linked to reduced concentration and increased fatigue.` },
    { q:`Which of these is an example of a healthy way to take a break during a long study session?`, ch:[`Scrolling social media for two straight hours`,`Taking a short walk or stretching for a few minutes`,`Starting a new, unrelated stressful task`,`Skipping the break entirely to "save time"`], ans:1, exp:`A brief walk or stretch helps refresh your focus and gives your brain a real rest, unlike screen-based breaks that can be just as mentally draining.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd106', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Growth Mindset Basics`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`What is the core idea behind a "growth mindset," as described by psychologist Carol Dweck?`, ch:[`Intelligence and abilities are fixed traits that can never change`,`Abilities and intelligence can be developed over time through effort, strategy, and learning`,`Only naturally talented people can succeed`,`Mindset has no effect on learning or achievement`], ans:1, exp:`A growth mindset is the belief that your abilities can improve through effort and good strategies, rather than being permanently fixed from birth.` },
    { q:`Which statement reflects a "fixed mindset" response to a bad test grade?`, ch:[`"I need to try a different study strategy next time"`,`"This score shows I'm just not smart enough for this subject"`,`"I can ask my teacher for feedback on what to improve"`,`"Let me figure out which topics I need to review more"`], ans:1, exp:`This response treats the grade as proof of a fixed, unchangeable trait, rather than as useful feedback that can guide improvement.` },
    { q:`How should someone with a growth mindset typically respond to failure or mistakes?`, ch:[`Avoid trying again to prevent future failure`,`View mistakes as evidence they should give up`,`See mistakes as useful information that shows what to work on next`,`Blame the mistake entirely on bad luck with no reflection`], ans:2, exp:`A growth mindset treats mistakes as a normal, valuable part of learning — information that points toward what to practice or adjust next.` },
    { q:`Why does praising effort and strategy ("You worked hard and tried a smart approach") tend to build a stronger growth mindset than praising natural talent ("You're just so smart")?`, ch:[`Effort-based praise has no real psychological effect`,`Praising talent alone can make students afraid to attempt hard tasks that might threaten their "smart" label`,`Talent-based praise always leads to better outcomes`,`There's no difference between the two types of praise`], ans:1, exp:`When praise focuses only on innate talent, students can become afraid of challenges that might make them look "not smart," while praising effort encourages them to keep trying and improving.` },
    { q:`What does the word "yet" added to a statement like "I don't understand this topic ___" typically represent in growth mindset language?`, ch:[`A sign of permanent failure`,`An acknowledgment that understanding hasn't happened yet, but can develop with more effort and time`,`A word that has no real meaning in this context`,`Evidence that the topic is impossible to learn`], ans:1, exp:`Adding "yet" reframes a current struggle as temporary and improvable, which is a small but powerful growth mindset habit.` },
    { q:`Which of these is an example of applying a growth mindset to studying for a difficult class?`, ch:[`Deciding you're "just bad" at the subject and stopping effort`,`Trying a new study method after a low quiz score, instead of assuming the subject is impossible for you`,`Avoiding the subject entirely for the rest of the year`,`Assuming your grade is permanently fixed no matter what you do`], ans:1, exp:`Adjusting your approach after setbacks, rather than assuming your ability is fixed, is a hallmark of applying a growth mindset in real life.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd107', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Reading Scientific Articles (Intro)`, diff:'Medium',
  qs:[
    { q:`When first approaching a scientific article, which section typically gives the best quick overview of the study's purpose, methods, and main findings?`, ch:[`The reference list`,`The abstract`,`The acknowledgments section`,`The figure captions only`], ans:1, exp:`The abstract is a short summary at the start of the article that outlines the goal, methods, and key results, making it a great first stop before diving into details.` },
    { q:`In a scientific article, what is the main purpose of the "Methods" section?`, ch:[`To list the authors' personal opinions about the topic`,`To describe exactly how the study was conducted, so others could evaluate or repeat it`,`To summarize unrelated studies from other fields`,`To provide a glossary of every scientific term used`], ans:1, exp:`The Methods section explains the study's procedures in detail, which lets readers judge whether the approach was sound and allows other scientists to reproduce the study.` },
    { q:`Why is it important to read the "Discussion" or "Conclusion" section critically, rather than assuming everything stated there is fully proven?`, ch:[`Discussion sections are always completely accurate with no room for interpretation`,`Authors may offer interpretations or possible explanations that go beyond what the data can fully prove, so it's worth thinking critically`,`This section is unrelated to the actual study results`,`Discussion sections never include the authors' own opinions`], ans:1, exp:`Authors often speculate about broader implications in the discussion, and a careful reader should distinguish between what the data directly shows and what is more speculative interpretation.` },
    { q:`What does it mean if a scientific study reports a "small sample size"?`, ch:[`The study is automatically wrong`,`The number of participants or subjects studied was relatively low, which can limit how confidently the results generalize to larger groups`,`The researchers used very small equipment`,`Small sample size only matters for medical studies`], ans:1, exp:`A small sample size means fewer participants were studied, which can make results less reliable or harder to generalize, so it's an important detail to notice when evaluating a study's strength.` },
    { q:`Why is it useful to check whether a scientific article was published in a "peer-reviewed" journal?`, ch:[`Peer review has no bearing on the article's credibility`,`Peer review means other experts in the field evaluated the study's methods and conclusions before publication, which adds a layer of quality control`,`Peer-reviewed articles are always guaranteed to be completely true`,`Peer review is only relevant to art and literature, not science`], ans:1, exp:`Peer review means qualified experts examined the study's methodology and reasoning before it was published, helping filter out major flaws, though it doesn't guarantee the findings are the final word.` },
    { q:`If a headline claims "Study shows chocolate cures headaches," what is a smart first step before accepting that claim?`, ch:[`Immediately accept the headline as scientific fact`,`Ignore the topic completely`,`Look at the actual study to check the sample size, methods, and how strong the evidence really is`,`Assume all headlines about science are automatically false`], ans:2, exp:`Headlines often oversimplify or exaggerate findings, so checking the original study's methods and evidence strength helps you judge whether the claim is actually well supported.` }
  ]
},
{
  id:'fnd108', cat:'Behavioral & Social Sciences', title:`Preparing for Standardized Tests`, diff:'Easy',
  qs:[
    { q:`Why is it helpful to take a full-length practice test under timed, realistic conditions before an actual standardized test?`, ch:[`Practice tests have no value for standardized test prep`,`It helps you build stamina, get familiar with pacing, and identify weak areas to focus on`,`Practice tests always predict your exact final score`,`Timed practice is only useful for math sections`], ans:1, exp:`Simulating real test conditions builds your ability to focus for the full length of the exam and shows you exactly where your pacing or content knowledge needs work.` },
    { q:`What is a smart way to use your results from a practice standardized test?`, ch:[`Ignore the results and move on to a new practice test immediately`,`Review the questions you got wrong to understand why, then target that content in future study`,`Only look at your overall score, not individual questions`,`Assume every wrong answer was just bad luck`], ans:1, exp:`Understanding exactly why you missed a question, whether it was a content gap or a careless mistake, helps you study more efficiently going forward.` },
    { q:`Why is starting standardized test prep weeks or months in advance generally more effective than cramming the week before?`, ch:[`Cramming the week before is always just as effective`,`Spacing out preparation allows content to be reviewed multiple times, supporting stronger long-term retention`,`Starting early has no real advantage`,`Standardized tests only cover material from the past week anyway`], ans:1, exp:`Like other learning, standardized test content benefits from spaced review over weeks or months, which builds more durable understanding than a last-minute cram session.` },
    { q:`On test day, why is it recommended to get a full night's sleep rather than staying up late for a final cram session?`, ch:[`Sleep has no impact on test-day performance`,`Being well-rested supports better focus, memory recall, and decision-making during the test`,`Staying up late always improves recall the next day`,`Sleep only matters for younger students, not teenagers`], ans:1, exp:`A well-rested brain concentrates better and recalls information more reliably, so sleep the night before often helps more than extra last-minute cramming.` },
    { q:`Why is it useful to understand a standardized test's format and question types (like whether there's a penalty for guessing) before test day?`, ch:[`Format and scoring rules have no impact on strategy`,`Knowing the format lets you plan pacing and decide on smart guessing strategies ahead of time, instead of figuring it out under pressure`,`All standardized tests use identical formats and rules`,`This information is intentionally kept secret from students`], ans:1, exp:`Understanding scoring rules and format in advance lets you build a pacing and guessing strategy calmly beforehand, rather than trying to figure it out during the actual test.` },
    { q:`Which of the following is the best approach to reviewing content weaknesses identified from a diagnostic or practice test?`, ch:[`Avoid the weak topics since they're clearly too hard`,`Spend extra, focused study time on those specific weak areas rather than only reviewing what you already know well`,`Study every topic equally regardless of your performance`,`Skip further practice tests entirely after the first one`], ans:1, exp:`Targeting your actual weak spots makes your study time far more efficient than spending equal time on material you've already mastered.` }
  ]
},
];
